WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.120
For online content creators the unavoidable&nbsp;
subject of 2021 has been NFT’s. 

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.080
From incredibly cringe-worthy ape profile pics, to&nbsp;
incomprehensibly tasteless tributes to deceased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:15.920
celebrities, to six-figure sales of the “original&nbsp;
copy” of a meme, it is the thing that is currently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.720
dominating the collective brain space of digital&nbsp;
artists and sucking up all the oxygen in the room.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:25.520
And I do want to talk about that, I want to&nbsp;
talk about my opinions on NFTs and digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.840
ownership and scarcity, all the myriad&nbsp;
dimensions of the issue, but I don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:37.440
just want to talk about NFTs, I can’t just talk&nbsp;
about NFTs because ultimately they are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:42.720
a symbol of so much more, and it is that&nbsp;
“more” that is ultimately important.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:57.840
So let me tell you a story.

00:01:11.080 --> 00:01:12.080
[Drumming]

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.960
In 2008 the economy functionally collapsed.

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.520
The basic chain reaction was this:

00:01:17.520 --> 00:01:20.400
Banks came up with a thing called&nbsp;
a mortgage-backed security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.560
a financial instrument that could be traded&nbsp;
or collected that was based on a bundle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:29.680
of thousands of individual mortgages. Based on the&nbsp;
general reluctance of banks to issue mortgages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.680 --> 00:01:34.640
the risk-aversion in lending someone hundreds&nbsp;
of thousands of dollars that they’ll pay off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:40.720
over the course of decades, these bonds&nbsp;
were seen as especially stable. However&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:47.440
they were also immensely profitable for the&nbsp;
banks who both issue the mortgages and the bonds.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.120
Because of that perceived stability a&nbsp;
lot of other financial organizations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.240
like pension funds and hedge funds, used them&nbsp;
as the backbone of their investment portfolio.

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.960
In this arrangement a mortgage becomes more&nbsp;
profitable to the bank issuing the mortgage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.800
as a component of a bond than it is as a&nbsp;
mortgage. Proportionally the returns per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.800 --> 00:02:11.600
mortgage from the bond are just that much better&nbsp;
than the returns from the isolated mortgage.

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:16.240
There’s some problems, though. Problem one is&nbsp;
that the biggest returns on a bond come from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:21.040
when it first hits the market, a new bond&nbsp;
that creates new securities sales is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.040 --> 00:02:27.280
more than an old bond that is slowly appreciating,&nbsp;
but not seeing much trade. Problem two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:30.880
is that there are a finite number&nbsp;
of people and houses in America;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:35.680
the market has to level out at a natural&nbsp;
ceiling as eventually all or nearly all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.880
mortgages are packaged into bonds, thus very&nbsp;
few new bonds can be generated and sold.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.040
So here’s the incentives that are created.

00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.440
One: it’s good for banks if there are more&nbsp;
houses that they can issue mortgages for&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.320
Two: the more mortgages issued the&nbsp;
better, because a bad mortgage is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.920
worth more as a component of a bond than&nbsp;
a good mortgage that’s not part of a bond.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:02.960
So real estate developers find that they have a&nbsp;
really easy time getting funding from banks for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:06.880
creating vast new suburbs full of houses&nbsp;
that can be sold to generate mortgages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:11.120
but rather than building the kind of&nbsp;
housing most people actually need and want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:16.400
they focus specifically on the kinds of&nbsp;
upper-middle-class houses that fit into the sweet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:21.680
spot from the perspective of the banks packaging&nbsp;
the bonds. Buyers, in turn, find that they have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.600
suspiciously easy time getting a mortgage despite&nbsp;
the fact that, for most people, the economy wasn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:33.200
doing so great. Wages were stagnant and yet even&nbsp;
though developers were going absolutely haywire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.520
building new housing, the houses being built&nbsp;
were all out of their price range to begin with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:44.640
and counter intuitively this massive increase&nbsp;
in supply wasn’t driving down the price.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:49.760
This is because the houses were being bought, just&nbsp;
mostly not by people intending to live in them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.400
They were being bought by speculators who&nbsp;
would then maybe rent them out or often just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.920
leave them vacant with the intent&nbsp;
to sell a couple years later.

00:03:57.920 --> 00:04:02.800
Because speculators were buying up the supply&nbsp;
it created synthetic demand. The price keeps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:08.480
going up because speculators keep buying, which&nbsp;
creates the illusion that the value is going up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:13.600
which attracts more speculators who buy up&nbsp;
more supply and further inflate the price.

00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.160
These speculators are enabled by a system&nbsp;
that is prioritizing generating new mortgages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:22.160
purely for the sake of having more&nbsp;
mortgages to package into bonds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:26.400
The down payments are low and the mortgages&nbsp;
all have a really attractive teaser rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.280
meaning that for the first three to seven years of&nbsp;
the mortgage the monthly payments are rock bottom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:36.480
as low as a few hundred dollars per month against&nbsp;
a mortgage that would normally charge thousands.

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.560
Caught up in all this are legitimate&nbsp;
buyers who have been lured into signing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.640
for a mortgage that they can’t afford&nbsp;
by aggressive salespeople who have an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:49.040
incentive to generate mortgages that they can&nbsp;
then sell to a bank who can put it into a bond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.840
to sell to pension funds to make a line go&nbsp;
up, because it’s good when the line goes up.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:54.880
It’s a bubble.

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.560
The bubble burst as the teaser rates&nbsp;
on the mortgages started to expire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:02.000
the monthly cost jumped up, and&nbsp;
since the demand was synthetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.160
there were no actual buyers for the&nbsp;
speculators to sell the houses to.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:11.040
So the speculators start dumping stock, which&nbsp;
finally drives prices down, but because the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:16.880
original price was so inflated the new price&nbsp;
is still out of reach of most actual buyers.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:20.400
Legitimate buyers caught in the&nbsp;
middle find their rates jumping, too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:24.880
but because the price of the house is going&nbsp;
down as speculators try and dump their stock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:28.800
the price of the house goes down&nbsp;
relative to the mortgage issued,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:33.120
and thus they can’t refinance and are&nbsp;
locked into paying the original terms.

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.400
Unable to sell the house and unable&nbsp;
to afford the monthly payments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:41.840
the owners, legitimate and speculators,&nbsp;
default on their mortgages, they stop paying.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:46.480
Eventually the default rate reaches&nbsp;
criticality and the bonds fail.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:52.640
As the bonds fail this impacts all the first order&nbsp;
buyers of the bonds, hedge funds, pension funds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:57.920
retirement savings funds, and the like.&nbsp;
It also cascades through all derivatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:03.120
which are financial products that take their&nbsp;
value directly from the value of the bond.

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.400
This creates a knock-on effect: huge segments&nbsp;
of the economy turn out to be dead trees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:12.560
rotten to the core, but as a rotten tree falls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.400
it still shreds its neighbours&nbsp;
and crushes anything below it.

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:20.320
It was a failure precipitated by a&nbsp;
combination of greed, active fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.360
and willful blindness at all levels of power.&nbsp;
The banks issuing the bad mortgages were the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:30.000
banks selling the bonds and providing the capital&nbsp;
to build the houses to generate the mortgages.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.480
The ratings agencies checking the bonds were,&nbsp;
themselves, publicly traded and dependent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:39.120
on being in good relations with the banks,&nbsp;
incentivized to rubber stamp whatever rating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:44.240
would make their client happy. The regulatory&nbsp;
agencies that should have seen the problem coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.240 --> 00:06:47.920
were gutted by budget cuts and mired&nbsp;
in conflicts of interest as employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.920 --> 00:06:52.080
used their positions in regulation to&nbsp;
secure higher paying jobs in industry.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:57.040
And, the cherry on top, the people largely&nbsp;
responsible for it all knew that because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.040 --> 00:07:02.000
and their toxic products were so interwoven into&nbsp;
the foundations of the economy they could count&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:15.840
on a bailout from the government because no matter&nbsp;
how rotten they were, they were very large trees.

00:07:17.880 --> 00:07:18.880
[Drumming]

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:23.280
This naked display of greed and fraud&nbsp;
created what would be fertile soil for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:26.640
both anti-capitalist movements&nbsp;
and hyper-capitalist movements:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:31.280
both groups of people who saw themselves as&nbsp;
being screwed over by the system, with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:37.040
group diagnosing the problem as the system’s&nbsp;
inherently corrupt and corrupting incentives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:43.440
and the other seeing the crisis as a consequence&nbsp;
of too much regulation, too much exclusion.

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:48.080
The hyper-capitalist, or anarcho-capitalist&nbsp;
argument is that in a less constrained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:53.520
market there would be more incentive to call&nbsp;
foul, that regulation had only succeeded in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.320
creating an in-group that was effectively&nbsp;
able to conspire without competition.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.440
Of course this argument fails to&nbsp;
consider that a substantial number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.440 --> 00:08:06.240
people within the system did, in fact, get&nbsp;
fabulously wealthy specifically by betting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.280
against the synthetic success&nbsp;
of the market, but regardless.

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:15.920
Into this environment in 2009 arrived Bitcoin,&nbsp;
an all-electronic peer-to-peer currency.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.920 --> 00:08:19.280
Philosophically Bitcoin, and&nbsp;
cryptocurrency in general,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.520
was paraded as an end to banks&nbsp;
and centralized currency.

00:08:23.520 --> 00:08:27.200
This is what will form the bedrock,&nbsp;
both philosophical and technological,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.560
that NFTs will be built on&nbsp;
top of. It’s a bit of a hike&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:35.920
from here to the Bored Ape Yacht Club,&nbsp;
so I guess get ready for that. Strap in.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:41.600
As we get into this we’re going to need to deal&nbsp;
with a lot of vocabulary, and a lot of complexity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:44.880
Some of this is the result of systems&nbsp;
that are very technically intricate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.320
and some of this is from systems that&nbsp;
are poorly designed or deliberately&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:53.040
obtuse in order to make them difficult to&nbsp;
understand and thus appear more legitimate.

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:57.280
The entire subject sits at the intersection&nbsp;
of two fields that are notoriously prone to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:03.440
hype-based obfuscation, computer tech and finance,&nbsp;
and inherits a lot of bad habits from both,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:07.440
with a reputation for making things&nbsp;
deliberately more difficult to understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.720
specifically to create the illusion that&nbsp;
only they are smart enough to understand it.

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:17.840
Mining and minting are both methods for making&nbsp;
tokens, which are the base thing that blockchains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:23.200
deal with, but the two are colloquially different&nbsp;
processes, where mining is a coin token created as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:28.160
a result of the consensus protocol and minting&nbsp;
is a user-initiated addition of a token to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:32.800
blockchain. All blockchains are made of nodes,&nbsp;
and these nodes can be watcher nodes, miner nodes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.800 --> 00:09:37.760
or validator nodes, though most miner nodes are&nbsp;
also validator nodes. Fractionalization is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.960
process of taking one asset and creating a new&nbsp;
asset that represents portions of the original.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:48.960
So you get $DOG, a memecoin crypto DeFi venture&nbsp;
capital fund backed by the fractionalization of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.680
the original Doge meme sold as an NFT&nbsp;
to PleasrDAO on the Ethereum network.

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:57.920
I’m sorry, some of this is&nbsp;
just going to be like that.

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.240
The idea behind cryptocurrency is that&nbsp;
your digital wallet functions the same as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:06.800
a bank account, there’s no need for a bank&nbsp;
to hold and process your transactions because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:11.600
rather than holding a sum that conceptually&nbsp;
represents physical currency, the cryptocoins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:17.120
in your cryptowallet are the actual money. And&nbsp;
because this money isn’t issued by a government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.120 --> 00:10:22.240
it is resistant to historical cash crises like&nbsp;
hyperinflation caused by governments devaluing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.240 --> 00:10:27.760
their currency on purpose or by accident. It&nbsp;
brings the flexibility and anonymity of cash and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:33.520
barter to the digital realm, allowing individuals&nbsp;
to transact without oversight or intermediaries.

00:10:33.520 --> 00:10:39.440
And in a one-paragraph pitch you can see the&nbsp;
appeal, there’s a compelling narrative there.

00:10:39.440 --> 00:10:44.240
But, of course, in the twelve years since&nbsp;
then none of that played out as designed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:48.240
Bitcoin was structurally too slow and&nbsp;
expensive to handle regular commerce.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.240 --> 00:10:52.560
The whole thing basically came out the&nbsp;
gate as a speculative financial vehicle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:56.560
and so the only consumer market&nbsp;
that proved to be a viable use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.560 --> 00:11:01.520
was buying and selling prohibited drugs where&nbsp;
the high fees, rapid price fluctuations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:08.320
and multi-hour transaction times were mitigated&nbsp;
by receiving LSD in the mail a week later.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:12.880
And as far as banking is concerned, Bitcoin&nbsp;
was never designed to solve the actual problems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:18.400
created by the banking industry, only to be the&nbsp;
new medium by which they operated. The principal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:23.280
offering wasn’t revolution, but at best a changing&nbsp;
of the guard. The gripe is not with the outcomes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:29.680
of 2008, but the fact that you had to be well&nbsp;
connected in order to get in on the grift in 2006.

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:34.480
And even the change of the guard is an illusion.&nbsp;
Old money finance assholes like the Winkelvoss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.480 --> 00:11:39.360
twins were some of the first big names to jump&nbsp;
on to crypto, where they remain to this day.

00:11:39.360 --> 00:11:43.600
Financial criminal Jordan Belfort, convicted&nbsp;
of fraud for running pump and dump schemes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:48.560
and barred for life from trading regulated&nbsp;
securities or acting as a broker, loves Crypto.

00:11:48.560 --> 00:11:52.640
Venture capitalist Chris Dixon, who has&nbsp;
made huge bank off the “old web” in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.640 --> 00:11:57.040
role as a general partner at VC firm Andreessen&nbsp;
Horowitz Capital Management, is super popular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:02.960
in the NFT space. He likes to paint himself as&nbsp;
an outsider underdog fighting the gatekeepers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:06.480
but he also sits on the boards of&nbsp;
Coinbase, a large cryptocurrency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.480 --> 00:12:09.840
exchange that makes money by being&nbsp;
the gatekeeper collecting a fee on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:15.680
all entries and exits to the crypto economy, and&nbsp;
Oculus VR, which is owned by Meta, nee Facebook.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:20.800
Peter Thiel, who also went from wealthy to&nbsp;
ultra-wealthy off the Web2 boom via PayPal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:24.160
loves crypto, and is friends with&nbsp;
a bunch of eugenics advocates who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:29.920
promote cryptocurrency as a return to “sound&nbsp;
money” for a whole bunch of extremely racist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:35.040
reasons because when they start talking&nbsp;
about banks and bankers, they mean Jews.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:38.160
Some of the largest institutional&nbsp;
holders of cryptocurrency&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.160 --> 00:12:42.480
are the exact same investment banks&nbsp;
that created the subprime loan crash.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:46.720
Rather than being a reprieve to the&nbsp;
people harmed by the housing bubble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.720 --> 00:12:50.400
the people whose savings and&nbsp;
retirements were, unknown to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:56.880
being gambled on smoke, cryptocurrency instantly&nbsp;
became the new playground for smoke vendors.

00:12:56.880 --> 00:13:03.360
This is a really important point to stress:&nbsp;
cryptocurrency does nothing to address 99% of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:08.800
the problems with the banking industry, because&nbsp;
those problems are patterns of human behaviour.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:13.680
They’re incentives, they’re social structures,&nbsp;
they’re modalities. The problem is what people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:20.320
are doing to others, not that the building they’re&nbsp;
doing it in has the word “bank” on the outside.

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:25.760
In addition to not fixing problems, Bitcoin&nbsp;
also came with a pretty substantial drawback.

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:30.320
The innovation of Bitcoin over previous attempts&nbsp;
at digital currency was to employ a distributed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:35.680
append-only ledger, a kind of database where new&nbsp;
entries can only be added to the end, and then to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:41.360
have several different nodes, called validators,&nbsp;
compete over who gets to validate the next update.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:46.480
These are, respectively, the blockchain,&nbsp;
and proof-of-work verification.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:50.480
Now, proof-of-work has an interesting&nbsp;
history as a technology, typically being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.480 --> 00:13:55.920
deployed as a deterrent to misbehaviour. For&nbsp;
example, if you require that for every email&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:01.520
sent the user’s computer has to complete a&nbsp;
small math problem it places a trivial load&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:06.000
onto normal users sending a few dozen,&nbsp;
or even a couple hundred emails a day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.000 --> 00:14:11.600
but places a massive load on the infrastructure&nbsp;
of anyone attempting to spam millions of emails.

00:14:12.160 --> 00:14:16.160
How it works in Bitcoin, simply put, is&nbsp;
that when a block of transactions are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:20.800
ready to be recorded to the ledger all of the&nbsp;
mining nodes in the network compete with one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:26.080
another to solve a cryptographic math problem&nbsp;
that’s based on the data inside the block.

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:31.760
Effectively they’re competing to figure out&nbsp;
the equation that yields a specific result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:36.400
when the contents of the block are fed into&nbsp;
it, with the complexity of the desired result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.400 --> 00:14:41.600
getting deliberately more difficult based on the&nbsp;
total processing power available to the network.

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:46.080
Once the math problem has been solved the&nbsp;
rest of the validation network can easily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:50.800
double-check the work, since the contents of&nbsp;
the block can be fed into the proposed solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.800 --> 00:14:54.080
and it either spits out the valid answer or fails.

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:58.000
If the equation works and the consensus&nbsp;
of validators signs off on it the block&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:01.440
is added to the bottom of the ledger and&nbsp;
the miner who solved the problem first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:04.080
is rewarded with newly generated Bitcoin.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:08.560
The complexity of the answer that the computers&nbsp;
are trying to solve scales up based on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:14.000
network’s processing power specifically to&nbsp;
incur heavy diminishing returns as a protection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:19.440
against an attack on the network where someone&nbsp;
just builds a bigger computer and takes over.

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.240
Critics pointed out that&nbsp;
this created new problems:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.240 --> 00:15:27.600
adversarial validation would deliberately incur&nbsp;
escalating processing costs, which would in turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.600 --> 00:15:31.520
generate perverse structural incentives&nbsp;
that would quickly reward capital holders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:36.080
and lock out any individual that wasn’t already&nbsp;
obscenely wealthy, because while the escalating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.080 --> 00:15:41.200
proof-of-work scheme incurs heavy diminishing&nbsp;
returns, diminishing returns are still returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.200 --> 00:15:45.920
so more would always go to those with&nbsp;
the resources to build the bigger rig.

00:15:45.920 --> 00:15:48.560
No matter what Bitcoin future was envisioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:52.640
in the here and now computer&nbsp;
hardware can be bought with dollars.

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:55.040
Rather than dismantling corrupt power structures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.040 --> 00:15:58.320
this would just become a new&nbsp;
tool for existing wealth.

00:15:58.320 --> 00:16:01.840
And that’s… exactly what happened.

00:16:01.840 --> 00:16:06.880
Thus began an arms race for bigger and bigger&nbsp;
processing rigs, followed by escalating demands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.880 --> 00:16:12.880
for the support systems, hardware engineers, HVAC,&nbsp;
and operating space needed to put those rigs in.

00:16:12.880 --> 00:16:16.480
And, don’t worry, we’re not&nbsp;
forgetting the power requirements.

00:16:16.480 --> 00:16:20.960
These rigs draw an industrial amount of&nbsp;
power and, because of the winner-takes-all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.960 --> 00:16:26.080
nature of the competition, huge amounts of&nbsp;
redundant work are being done and discarded.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:30.560
Estimates for this power consumption are&nbsp;
hard to verify, the data is very complex,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:34.000
spread across hundreds of operators&nbsp;
around the globe, who move frequently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:34.000 --> 00:16:39.040
in search of cheap electricity, and&nbsp;
it’s all pretty heavily politicized.

00:16:39.040 --> 00:16:44.240
But even conservative estimates from within the&nbsp;
crypto-mining industry puts the sum energy cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.240 --> 00:16:49.680
of Bitcoin processing on par with the power&nbsp;
consumption of a small industrialized nation.

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:54.880
Now, evangelists will counter that the global&nbsp;
banking industry also uses a lot of power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:54.880 --> 00:17:00.880
gesturing at things like idle ATMs humming away&nbsp;
all night long, which is strictly speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.880 --> 00:17:06.400
not untrue. On a factual level the&nbsp;
entire global banking industry does,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:09.680
in fact, use a lot of total electricity.

00:17:09.680 --> 00:17:14.400
But, for scale, it takes six hours of that&nbsp;
sustained power draw for the Bitcoin network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:18.640
to process as many transactions&nbsp;
as VISA handles in one minute,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:25.360
and during that time VISA is using fractions&nbsp;
of a cent of electricity per transaction.

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:29.680
And that’s just VISA. That’s&nbsp;
one major institution.

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:35.600
So, like, yes, globally the entirety of the&nbsp;
banking industry consumes a lot of power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.600 --> 00:17:39.840
and a non-trivial portion of that is&nbsp;
waste that could be better allocated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.840 --> 00:17:43.920
But it’s also the global banking&nbsp;
industry for seven billion people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.920 --> 00:17:47.520
and not the hobby horse of a few&nbsp;
hundred thousand gambling addicts.

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:52.720
So just to head all this off at the pass,&nbsp;
Bitcoin and proof-of-work cryptocurrency aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.720 --> 00:17:57.840
incentivizing a move to green energy sources,&nbsp;
like solar and wind, they are offsetting it.

00:17:57.840 --> 00:18:03.200
Because electrical consumption, electrical&nbsp;
waste, is the value that underpins Bitcoin.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:08.000
Miners spend X dollars in electricity to&nbsp;
mine a Bitcoin, they expect to be able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.000 --> 00:18:12.960
sell that coin for at least X plus profit.&nbsp;
When new power sources come online and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:27.840
price of electricity goes down, they don’t&nbsp;
let X go down, they build a bigger machine.

00:18:27.840 --> 00:18:27.963
[Drumming]

00:18:27.963 --> 00:18:32.320
In 2012 Vitalik Buterin, a crypto&nbsp;
enthusiast and butthurt Warlock main&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.320 --> 00:18:36.640
set out to fix what he saw as the&nbsp;
failings and inflexibilities of Bitcoin.

00:18:36.640 --> 00:18:41.840
Rather than becoming the new digital currency,&nbsp;
a thing that people actually used to buy stuff,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:45.280
Bitcoin had become an unwieldy&nbsp;
speculative financial instrument,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:50.720
too slow and expensive to use for anything&nbsp;
other than stunt purchases of expensive cars.

00:18:50.720 --> 00:18:54.960
It was infested with money&nbsp;
laundering and mired in bad press.

00:18:54.960 --> 00:18:59.600
After the FBI shut down Silk Road you&nbsp;
couldn’t even buy drugs with it anymore.

00:18:59.600 --> 00:19:03.600
In practice you couldn’t do anything&nbsp;
with your Bitcoin but bet on it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:03.600 --> 00:19:08.400
lock up money you already have in the hopes&nbsp;
that Bitcoin goes up later, and pray you don’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.400 --> 00:19:13.520
lose it all in a scam, lose access to your&nbsp;
wallet, or have it all stolen by an exchange.

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:19.040
The result, launched in 2014, was Ethereum, a&nbsp;
competing cryptocurrency that boasted lower fees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.040 --> 00:19:22.160
faster transaction times, a&nbsp;
reduced electrical footprint,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:25.920
and, most notably, a sophisticated&nbsp;
processing functionality.

00:19:25.920 --> 00:19:29.840
While the Bitcoin blockchain only tracks&nbsp;
the location and movement of Bitcoins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.840 --> 00:19:34.480
Ethereum would be broader. In addition to&nbsp;
tracking Ether coins, the ledger would also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.480 --> 00:19:38.640
be able to track arbitrary blocks of data.&nbsp;
As long as they were compatible with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.640 --> 00:19:42.320
structure of the Ethereum network,&nbsp;
those blocks of data could even be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:47.600
programs that would utilize the validation&nbsp;
network as a distributed virtual machine.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:52.080
Vitalik envisioned this as a vast, infinite&nbsp;
machine, duplicated and distributed across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:52.080 --> 00:19:57.680
thousands or millions of computers, a system onto&nbsp;
which the entire history of a new internet could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.680 --> 00:20:03.120
be immutably written, immune to censorship,&nbsp;
and impossible for governments to take down.

00:20:03.120 --> 00:20:08.240
He saw it dismantling banks and other intermediary&nbsp;
industries, allowing everyone to be their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.240 --> 00:20:13.360
bank, to be their own stock broker, to bypass&nbsp;
governments, regulators, and insurance agencies.

00:20:13.360 --> 00:20:16.800
His peers envisioned a future&nbsp;
where Ethereum became not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:22.320
a repository of financial transactions, but&nbsp;
of identity, with deeds, driver’s licenses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:25.360
professional credentials, medical&nbsp;
records, educational achievements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.360 --> 00:20:30.880
and employment history turned into tokens and&nbsp;
stored immutable and eternal on the chain.

00:20:30.880 --> 00:20:34.000
Through crypto and the ethereum&nbsp;
virtual machine they could bring all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.960
the benefits of Wall Street&nbsp;
investors and Silicon Valley&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:36.960 --> 00:20:41.760
venture capitalists to the poorest people&nbsp;
of the world, the unbanked and forgotten.

00:20:41.760 --> 00:20:45.440
This heady high-minded philosophy&nbsp;
is outlined in great detail in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:45.440 --> 00:20:49.760
journalistic abortion The Infinite Machine&nbsp;
by failed-journalist-turned-crypto-shill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:50.640
Camilla Russo.

00:20:51.200 --> 00:20:53.440
The book is actually really interesting.

00:20:53.440 --> 00:20:58.160
Not for the merits of the writing, Russo fails&nbsp;
to interrogate the validity or rationality of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.160 --> 00:21:02.960
even the simplest claims and falls just&nbsp;
shy of hagiography by occasionally noting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:02.960 --> 00:21:06.880
that something was a bit tacky or&nbsp;
embarrassing, but only just shy.

00:21:07.440 --> 00:21:11.680
She tells florid stories about the impoverished&nbsp;
people that Vitalik and friends claimed to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:16.720
working to save, but never once considers that&nbsp;
the solutions offered might not actually work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.720 --> 00:21:21.200
or that the people claiming to want to solve&nbsp;
those problems might not even be working on them.

00:21:21.200 --> 00:21:25.680
That’s actually a big issue, since the entire&nbsp;
crypto space, during the entire time that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:25.680 --> 00:21:31.040
Russo’s book covers, was absolutely awash with&nbsp;
astroturfing schemes where two guys would go to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:35.920
some small community in Laos or Angola, take&nbsp;
a bunch of pictures of people at a “crypto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.920 --> 00:21:40.960
investing seminar”, generate some headlines&nbsp;
for their coin or fund, and then peace out.

00:21:40.960 --> 00:21:45.200
For years dudes were going around asking&nbsp;
vendors if they could slap a Bitcoin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:45.200 --> 00:21:50.160
sticker onto the back of the cash register,&nbsp;
because the optics of making it look like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:50.160 --> 00:21:55.600
place takes Bitcoin was cheaper and easier&nbsp;
than actually using Bitcoin as a currency.

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:59.280
We have an entire decade of&nbsp;
credulous articles about how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.280 --> 00:22:03.600
Venezuela and Chile are on the verge&nbsp;
of switching entirely to crypto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:03.600 --> 00:22:08.720
based entirely on the claim of two&nbsp;
trust fund dudes from San Bernardino.

00:22:08.720 --> 00:22:13.360
A whole ten years littered with discarded&nbsp;
press releases about Dell and Microsoft and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:18.800
Square bringing crypto to the consumers&nbsp;
before just quietly discontinuing their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:18.800 --> 00:22:24.000
services after a year or two when they&nbsp;
realize the demand isn’t actually there.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:28.400
The fact that the development of Ethereum was&nbsp;
extremely dependent on a $100,000 fellowship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:32.880
grant from Peter Thiel is mentioned, but the&nbsp;
ideological implications of that connection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:39.280
are never explored, the entire subject occupies&nbsp;
a single paragraph sprinkled as flavour into a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:44.320
story about Vitalik and his co-developers airing&nbsp;
their grievances about some petty infighting.

00:22:44.320 --> 00:22:48.720
The book is mostly useful for it’s value&nbsp;
as a point of reference against reality.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:54.080
It’s a very thorough, if uncritical,&nbsp;
document of absolutely insane claims.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:57.280
“The idea was that traits of&nbsp;
blockchain technology—such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:57.280 --> 00:23:01.680
as having no central point of failure, being&nbsp;
uncensorable, cutting out intermediaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:01.680 --> 00:23:05.920
and being immutable—could also benefit&nbsp;
other applications besides money.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.920 --> 00:23:10.640
Financial instruments like stocks and bonds, and&nbsp;
commodities like gold, were the obvious targets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:10.640 --> 00:23:15.440
but people were also talking about putting other&nbsp;
representations of value like property deeds and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:15.440 --> 00:23:20.720
medical records on the blockchain, too. Those&nbsp;
efforts—admirable considering Bitcoin hadn’t,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:20.720 --> 00:23:25.600
and still hasn’t, been adopted widely&nbsp;
as currency—were known as Bitcoin 2.0.”

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:28.400
I love this paragraph because it&nbsp;
outlines just how disconnected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:32.240
from reality the people actually&nbsp;
building cryptocurrencies really are.

00:23:32.240 --> 00:23:36.400
They don’t understand anything about the&nbsp;
ecosystems they’re trying to disrupt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:36.400 --> 00:23:40.480
they only know that these are things&nbsp;
that can be conceptualized as valuable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:46.320
and assume that because they understand one very&nbsp;
complicated thing, programming with cryptography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:51.760
that all other complicated things must&nbsp;
be lesser in complexity and naturally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:51.760 --> 00:23:57.680
lower in the hierarchy of reality, nails easily&nbsp;
driven by the hammer that they have created.

00:23:57.680 --> 00:24:02.080
The idea of putting medical records on a&nbsp;
public, decentralized, trustless blockchain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:06.320
is absolutely nightmarish, and anyone who&nbsp;
proposes it should be instantly discredited.

00:24:06.320 --> 00:24:10.240
The fact that Russo fails to question&nbsp;
any of this is journalistic malpractice.

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:15.840
Now, in terms of improvements over&nbsp;
Bitcoin, Ethereum has many. It’s not hard.

00:24:15.840 --> 00:24:17.120
Bitcoin sucks.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:20.160
In terms of problems with Bitcoin,&nbsp;
Ethereum solves none of them and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:20.160 --> 00:24:24.080
introduces a whole new suite of problems&nbsp;
driven by the technofetishistic egotism of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:24.080 --> 00:24:28.160
assuming that programmers are uniquely&nbsp;
suited to solve society’s problems.

00:24:28.160 --> 00:24:32.320
Vitalik wants his invention to be&nbsp;
an infinite machine, so let’s ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:32.320 --> 00:24:34.640
what that machine is built to do.

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:40.080
[Drumming]

00:24:40.080 --> 00:24:44.320
In order to really understand the full&nbsp;
scope of this we do need to dig a bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:44.320 --> 00:24:48.480
more into the technical aspects,&nbsp;
because that technical functionality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.480 --> 00:24:51.200
informs the way the rest of the system behaves.

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:56.240
In a very McLuhanist way, the machine&nbsp;
shapes the environment around it.

00:24:56.240 --> 00:25:00.720
As already mentioned, a blockchain consists&nbsp;
of two broad fundamental components:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:00.720 --> 00:25:03.440
the ledger and the consensus mechanism.

00:25:03.440 --> 00:25:06.960
All currently popular blockchains&nbsp;
use an append-only ledger.

00:25:06.960 --> 00:25:12.240
Now this is, on its own, not that remarkable.&nbsp;
Append-only is just a database setting that only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:17.120
allows new entries to be added to the end of the&nbsp;
current database, once something is written to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:22.480
database it’s read-only. Standard applications&nbsp;
are typically things like activity logs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:27.600
which is conceptually all a blockchain&nbsp;
really is: a giant log of transactions.

00:25:27.600 --> 00:25:32.320
The hitch is that it’s decentralized, with&nbsp;
elective participants hosting a complete copy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:32.320 --> 00:25:39.120
of the entire log, and this is where the second&nbsp;
component comes in, the consensus mechanism.

00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:43.840
All validating participants, called nodes,&nbsp;
have a complete copy of the database,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:43.840 --> 00:25:47.920
and no one copy is considered&nbsp;
to be the authoritative copy.

00:25:47.920 --> 00:25:52.640
Instead there is a consensus mechanism that&nbsp;
determines which transactions actually happened.

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:54.960
This is all the proof of work stuff.

00:25:54.960 --> 00:25:57.280
Proof of work isn’t the only consensus mechanism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:01.520
but it’s popular because it’s very easy&nbsp;
to implement by just cloning Bitcoin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:05.520
and is resilient against the kinds of&nbsp;
attacks that crypto enthusiasts care about.

00:26:05.520 --> 00:26:07.120
It’s a very brute-force solution,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.120 --> 00:26:11.440
but legitimately if you want a network of&nbsp;
ledgers where no one trusts anyone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.440 --> 00:26:17.360
just making everyone on the network do extreme&nbsp;
amounts of wasted, duplicate work is a solution.

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:21.200
One of the major problems that this machine&nbsp;
solves is what’s called the double-spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.200 --> 00:26:25.760
problem: how do you stop someone&nbsp;
from spending the same dollar twice?

00:26:25.760 --> 00:26:30.080
If someone tries, how do you determine&nbsp;
which transaction really happened?

00:26:30.080 --> 00:26:34.640
Banks solve this problem by not correlating&nbsp;
account balance with any specific dollar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:39.520
processing transactions first-come-first-serve,&nbsp;
which they track with central resources,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:39.520 --> 00:26:42.880
and punishing users with overdraft&nbsp;
fees for double-spending.

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:47.440
Eschewing a central solution, cryptocurrencies&nbsp;
rely on their consensus mechanism.

00:26:47.440 --> 00:26:50.800
The most popular alternative to&nbsp;
proof-of-work is proof-of-stake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.800 --> 00:26:56.400
where validators post collateral of some kind,&nbsp;
usually whatever currency is endemic to the chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:56.400 --> 00:27:01.120
with the amount of collateral determining their&nbsp;
odds of being rewarded the validation bounty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:06.400
for any given transaction. The main proposal of&nbsp;
proof of stake is that it significantly reduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.400 --> 00:27:11.200
the wasted power problems of proof of work,&nbsp;
but it’s less resilient than proof of work.

00:27:11.200 --> 00:27:16.320
On the energy cost side of things, proof of stake&nbsp;
is still inefficient, just by virtue of the sheer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:16.320 --> 00:27:21.920
volume of redundancy, but on a per-user basis&nbsp;
it’s at least inefficient on the scale of, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:22.560 --> 00:27:25.760
an MMO as opposed to a steel foundry.

00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:29.920
This is difficult to assess because the&nbsp;
most popular proof of stake chains are still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:29.920 --> 00:27:34.000
unpopular and low traffic in the&nbsp;
scope of things, heavily centralized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:38.240
Claims about scalability are backed&nbsp;
up by nothing but the creators’ word.

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:42.160
Proof of stake is also significantly more&nbsp;
complex because there now needs to be some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.160 --> 00:27:46.560
mechanism for determining who gets to do the&nbsp;
validations, and also determining how audits&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:46.560 --> 00:27:49.760
are conducted, and then the question&nbsp;
of who has control over that system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:49.760 --> 00:27:54.000
and whether or not someone can gain control of&nbsp;
that system or control over the whole stake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:54.000 --> 00:27:57.840
via just buying out the staking&nbsp;
pool, et cetera et cetera et cetera.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:02.000
Proof of Stake also, even more explicitly,&nbsp;
rewards the wealthy who have the capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:07.600
to both stake and spend. It’s also even more&nbsp;
explicitly exclusionary. Ethereum’s proposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:13.760
proof-of-stake migration has a buy-in of 32 Ether,&nbsp;
which at the time of writing is about $130,000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:14.560 --> 00:28:18.160
so really only early adopters&nbsp;
and the wealthy can actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:18.160 --> 00:28:21.200
meaningfully participate&nbsp;
for more than just crumbs.

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:25.680
This is, in turn, compounding inherent problems&nbsp;
with the long term growth of the chain.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:25.680 --> 00:28:29.280
Even if you solve the escalating&nbsp;
power requirements of proof of work,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:29.280 --> 00:28:34.240
the data requirements of storing the chain&nbsp;
and participating as a validator are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:34.240 --> 00:28:39.680
prohibitive in a way that inevitably centralizes&nbsp;
power in the hands of a few wealthy operators.

00:28:39.680 --> 00:28:43.920
Vitalik: 85 terabytes per year is&nbsp;
actually fine, right? Like, because, uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.920 --> 00:28:48.560
85 terabytes per year, like, if you have, if&nbsp;
you have even one person that just keeps buying,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:51.920
like, um, a hundred dollar hard drive, like, uh,&nbsp;
I think once every month then they can store it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:51.920 --> 00:28:55.840
right, like it’s something like that. So, it’s&nbsp;
too big for just like a casual user that just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:55.840 --> 00:29:00.400
wants to run the chain on their laptop, but one&nbsp;
dedicated user who cares, they can totally afford&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:00.400 --> 00:29:04.080
to, uh, afford to store the chain. And so 85&nbsp;
terabytes, not a big deal, right, but once you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:04.080 --> 00:29:08.320
crank that number higher there comes a point&nbsp;
at which it starts becoming a really big deal.

00:29:08.320 --> 00:29:12.720
The major downside of all these systems is&nbsp;
that they’re extremely slow. Proof of work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:12.720 --> 00:29:16.560
is inefficient by design, and proof of stake&nbsp;
has multiple layers of lottery that need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:16.560 --> 00:29:21.520
be executed before any transaction can&nbsp;
be conducted, and in particular suffers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:26.000
from delays when elected validators are&nbsp;
offline and the system needs to draw again.

00:29:26.000 --> 00:29:28.800
As an extension of being slow&nbsp;
they’re also prone to getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:32.720
overwhelmed if too many people try&nbsp;
to make transactions simultaneously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:37.200
which can cause de-syncs between validators&nbsp;
and even lead to what’s called a fork,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:42.320
where two or more pools of validators reach a&nbsp;
different consensus about the state of the network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:42.320 --> 00:29:48.320
and each branch keeps on going afterwards&nbsp;
assuming that it is the authoritative version.

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:54.320
Forks can also be caused on purpose, and this&nbsp;
is, in fact, the only way to effectively undo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:54.320 --> 00:29:59.520
transactions. Like if someone stole your&nbsp;
coins the only way to get them back would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:59.520 --> 00:30:04.240
be to convince the people who manage the&nbsp;
chain itself to negotiate a rollback.

00:30:05.120 --> 00:30:07.200
That’s foreshadowing for later.

00:30:07.200 --> 00:30:10.960
Now, because the nature of a fork involves&nbsp;
a disagreement on what transactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:16.000
actually happened, and who got paid for those&nbsp;
transactions, this means that each arm of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:16.000 --> 00:30:22.080
fork has a vested interest in its own arm being&nbsp;
the authoritative arm, so resolving forks can turn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:22.080 --> 00:30:28.800
into irreconcilable schisms, which is funny as&nbsp;
an outside observer viewing it as inane internet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.800 --> 00:30:34.000
drama, but is frankly unacceptable for anything&nbsp;
posturing as a serious and legitimate currency.

00:30:34.000 --> 00:30:37.040
Like, it’s important to stress that&nbsp;
because of the nature of the chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:43.440
the way that the ID numbers of later blocks are&nbsp;
dependent on the outcomes of previous blocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:43.440 --> 00:30:48.400
these aren’t just a few disputed transactions&nbsp;
that need to be resolved between the buyer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:48.400 --> 00:30:53.120
the seller, and the payment processor,&nbsp;
these are disagreements on the fundamental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:53.120 --> 00:30:58.960
state of the entire economy that&nbsp;
create an entire alternate reality.

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:02.960
It’s an ecosystem that absolutely&nbsp;
demolishes consumer protections&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.960 --> 00:31:06.960
and makes the re-implementation&nbsp;
of them extremely difficult.

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:10.960
One of the big selling points of all this&nbsp;
technology is that it’s particularly secure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:10.960 --> 00:31:15.200
very difficult for anyone to hack it&nbsp;
directly, since there’s so much redundancy.

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:18.800
A lot of hay is made about the system’s&nbsp;
resiliency against man-in-the-middle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:18.800 --> 00:31:22.240
attacks, which are your classic&nbsp;
Hollywood hacker type attacks.

00:31:22.240 --> 00:31:27.360
Someone sends a command from point A and on the&nbsp;
way to point B it is intercepted and altered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:27.360 --> 00:31:32.880
Someone hacks into the bank and adds an arbitrary&nbsp;
number of zeroes onto their account balance.

00:31:32.880 --> 00:31:35.920
Evangelists will commonly claim&nbsp;
that blockchain could revolutionize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:35.920 --> 00:31:38.480
the global shipping industry and reduce fraud.

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:40.320
It’s a good claim to interrogate.

00:31:40.320 --> 00:31:43.600
First, the things that blockchain is&nbsp;
capable of tracking are things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:43.600 --> 00:31:48.320
manufacturers and shippers are already&nbsp;
tracking, or at least trying to track,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.320 --> 00:31:52.880
so this is not so much “revolution”&nbsp;
as it would be “standardization.”

00:31:52.880 --> 00:31:57.440
Even that is built on a predicate assumption&nbsp;
that everyone picks the same chain.

00:31:57.440 --> 00:32:00.160
It’s an extremely optimistic assumption.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:03.040
Many, many, many firms deliberately want their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:03.040 --> 00:32:07.360
information centralized and obfuscated&nbsp;
to protect against corporate espionage.

00:32:07.360 --> 00:32:11.680
The lack of a shared, standardized&nbsp;
bucket to put all the information into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:16.480
is not because it’s been heretofore&nbsp;
impossible, but because it’s been undesirable.

00:32:16.480 --> 00:32:21.600
Second, it assumes the existence of a theoretical&nbsp;
mechanism that ensures the synchronization of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:26.800
chain and reality above and beyond the&nbsp;
current capacity of logistics software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:26.800 --> 00:32:29.840
some means of preventing people from just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:29.840 --> 00:32:35.360
lying to the blockchain and giving it the&nbsp;
information it expects to be receiving,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.360 --> 00:32:40.640
the blockchain equivalent of ripping off the&nbsp;
shipping label and slapping it on the new box.

00:32:40.640 --> 00:32:43.360
The bigger problem here is&nbsp;
that in the pantheon of fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:43.360 --> 00:32:46.240
man-in-the-middle attacks&nbsp;
are actually pretty rare.

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:51.040
Global shipping needs to deal with it in certain&nbsp;
capacities, but taken on the whole the vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:51.040 --> 00:32:55.440
majority of fraud doesn’t come from altering&nbsp;
information as it passes between parties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:55.440 --> 00:32:59.840
rather from colluding parties&nbsp;
entering bad information at the start.

00:32:59.840 --> 00:33:04.080
Con artists don’t hack the Gibson to transfer&nbsp;
your funds to their offshore accounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:04.080 --> 00:33:06.400
they convince you to give them your password.

00:33:06.400 --> 00:33:12.560
Most fraud comes from people who technically&nbsp;
have permission to be doing what they’re doing.

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:15.200
Rather than preventing these&nbsp;
actual common types of fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:15.200 --> 00:33:19.440
cryptocurrency has made them absurdly easy,&nbsp;
and the main reason why cryptocurrency needs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:23.360
to be so resistant to man-in-the-middle&nbsp;
attacks is because the decentralized nature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:23.360 --> 00:33:27.840
of the network otherwise makes them&nbsp;
acutely vulnerable to those attacks.

00:33:27.840 --> 00:33:30.560
What this all kinda means&nbsp;
is that blockchains are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:30.560 --> 00:33:34.000
pretty bad at doing most of the things&nbsp;
they’re trying to do, and a lot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:34.000 --> 00:33:39.440
innovations in blockchains are attempts at&nbsp;
solving problems that blockchains introduced.

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:43.840
The biggest issue that cryptocurrencies have&nbsp;
suffered from is a lack of tangible things to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:43.840 --> 00:33:50.080
actually use them on as currencies, rent or food&nbsp;
or transit, and this is for pretty simple reasons.

00:33:50.080 --> 00:33:54.400
One is that the transaction fees on popular&nbsp;
chains are so prohibitive that it’s pointless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:54.400 --> 00:33:58.480
to use them on any transaction that isn’t&nbsp;
hundreds, if not thousands of dollars;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:58.480 --> 00:34:04.480
no one is going to buy the Bitcoin Bucket from&nbsp;
their local KFC. Ethereum’s main transaction fee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:04.480 --> 00:34:11.520
called Gas, on a good day runs around $20 US&nbsp;
per transaction, but that’s severely optimistic.

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:16.320
As of December 2021 the daily average&nbsp;
cost of Gas hasn’t gone below $50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:16.320 --> 00:34:20.960
US since August, with the three&nbsp;
month daily average riding over $130.

00:34:21.920 --> 00:34:24.480
And that’s just the daily average.

00:34:24.480 --> 00:34:28.880
The hourly price can, and does, swing&nbsp;
by up to two orders of magnitude.

00:34:28.880 --> 00:34:32.160
The throughput of blockchains is&nbsp;
so abysmal that transaction slots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:32.160 --> 00:34:36.080
are auctioned off to the highest bidder,&nbsp;
that’s why these numbers are so extreme.

00:34:36.080 --> 00:34:40.720
It only takes a few high rollers competing on&nbsp;
a transaction to drive the hourly price of gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:40.720 --> 00:34:47.840
up over $1000 US. The internal term for this&nbsp;
is a “gas war” because it’s just that common.

00:34:47.840 --> 00:34:51.280
Bots, in particular, can drive&nbsp;
gas prices well into the tens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:51.280 --> 00:34:54.400
of thousands of dollars as they&nbsp;
compete to capitalize on mistakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.400 --> 00:34:58.480
such as someone listing something for&nbsp;
sale well below its general market price.

00:34:58.480 --> 00:35:04.400
Also, it needs to be stressed: these gas wars&nbsp;
aren’t localized to just the thing that’s being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.400 --> 00:35:10.080
fought over. If Steam is getting hammered because&nbsp;
ConcernedApe posted Stardew Valley 2 by surprise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:10.080 --> 00:35:13.760
that will probably stay pretty well&nbsp;
contained. If Taylor Swift concert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:13.760 --> 00:35:18.000
tickets go on sale and LiveNation gets&nbsp;
crushed, you might never even know.

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:22.400
What those don’t do is cause&nbsp;
the cost of placing an order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:22.400 --> 00:35:26.960
on DriveThruRPG to spike by eight&nbsp;
thousand percent for three hours.

00:35:26.960 --> 00:35:31.040
The second big reason is that value on&nbsp;
the coins themselves is so volatile that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:31.040 --> 00:35:34.400
unless you’re willing to engage with&nbsp;
the speculative nature of the coins&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.400 --> 00:35:38.400
there’s actually a huge risk in&nbsp;
accepting them as payment for anything.

00:35:38.400 --> 00:35:42.880
Bitcoin in particular, owing to its glacial&nbsp;
transaction times, suffers from problems where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:42.880 --> 00:35:47.760
the value of the coin can change dramatically&nbsp;
between the start and end of a transaction.

00:35:47.760 --> 00:35:52.560
This is such a problem that it’s led to the rise&nbsp;
of an entire strata of middlemen in the ecosystem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:52.560 --> 00:35:56.320
so-called “stablecoin” exchanges&nbsp;
like Tether that exist to quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.320 --> 00:36:00.160
transfer cryptocurrencies between&nbsp;
each other and lock-in values.

00:36:00.160 --> 00:36:02.960
The stablecoins, rather than&nbsp;
having a speculative value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:02.960 --> 00:36:07.520
have a value that’s pegged to the value of an&nbsp;
actual currency, like the Euro or US dollar.

00:36:07.520 --> 00:36:11.360
The underlying problem that they&nbsp;
exist to solve is itself twofold.

00:36:11.360 --> 00:36:13.440
One is that converting cryptocurrency into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:13.440 --> 00:36:16.880
dollars is the step of the process that&nbsp;
makes you accountable to the tax man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.880 --> 00:36:21.760
and the primary goal of crypto in general is&nbsp;
to starve public services, so that’s a no go.

00:36:21.760 --> 00:36:25.440
The second is that there just&nbsp;
aren’t actually that many buyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:25.440 --> 00:36:30.160
and there’s not enough liquidity in&nbsp;
the ecosystem to cash out big holdings.

00:36:30.160 --> 00:36:33.840
Tether, the largest stablecoin, used&nbsp;
to advertise itself as being backed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:33.840 --> 00:36:37.920
on a one to one basis back when it was&nbsp;
called Realcoin, but that language has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:42.560
become far more nebulous over time as it’s&nbsp;
become obvious that it just isn’t true.

00:36:42.560 --> 00:36:47.280
This is a very complicated situation, but the&nbsp;
short version is that the people who own Tether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.280 --> 00:36:52.240
also own a real money exchange called Bitfinex,&nbsp;
and there’s evidence that the two services,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:56.320
both which require having actual dollars&nbsp;
on hand in order to back their products and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:56.320 --> 00:37:01.760
facilitate exchanges, are sharing the same pool&nbsp;
of money, swapping it back and forth as needed.

00:37:01.760 --> 00:37:06.000
This means that at any given time&nbsp;
either service is potentially backed by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.000 --> 00:37:10.240
zero dollars, or at least that’s what&nbsp;
the data implies might be the case.

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:13.840
Point is that if you’re a high&nbsp;
roller with tens of millions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:13.840 --> 00:37:16.480
of conceptual dollars tied up in cryptocurrency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:16.480 --> 00:37:21.040
there’s a fundamental cash problem. Your&nbsp;
holdings have inflated to tens, hundreds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:21.040 --> 00:37:27.040
or thousands of times what you put in, but that&nbsp;
price is just theoretical. It’s speculative.

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:30.480
You have all this crypto, you&nbsp;
can’t meaningfully spend it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.480 --> 00:37:33.360
and there’s not enough&nbsp;
buyers for you to get it out.

00:37:33.360 --> 00:37:39.120
In order for you to cash out you need&nbsp;
to convince someone else to buy in.

00:37:39.120 --> 00:37:44.320
These factors, taken holistically, mean&nbsp;
that cryptocurrency is a Bigger Fool scam.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:49.040
There’s a lot of digital ink spilt trying&nbsp;
to outline if it’s a decentralized Ponzi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:49.040 --> 00:37:54.080
scheme or a pyramid scheme or some hybrid&nbsp;
of the two, which is a taxonomical argument&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.080 --> 00:37:58.880
that I’m not here to settle, but, like&nbsp;
both of those, it’s a Bigger Fool scam.

00:37:58.880 --> 00:38:02.240
The whole thing operates by buying&nbsp;
worthless assets believing that you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:02.240 --> 00:38:05.360
will later be able to sell them to a bigger fool.

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:09.120
The entire structure of cryptocurrencies&nbsp;
at their basic level of operation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:09.120 --> 00:38:13.520
is designed to deliver the greatest rewards&nbsp;
to the earliest adopters, regardless of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.520 --> 00:38:18.400
if you’re talking about proof-of-work or&nbsp;
proof-of-stake. This is inherent to their being.

00:38:18.400 --> 00:38:20.080
As Stephen Diel put it

00:38:20.080 --> 00:38:24.960
“These schemes around crypto tokens cannot create&nbsp;
or destroy actual dollars, they can only shift&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:30.000
them around. If you sell your crypto and make a&nbsp;
profit in dollars, it’s only because someone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.000 --> 00:38:35.200
bought it at a higher price than you did. And&nbsp;
then they expect to do the same and so on and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.200 --> 00:38:41.360
so on ad infinitum. Every dollar that comes out of&nbsp;
cryptocurrency needs to come from a later investor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:41.360 --> 00:38:46.080
putting a dollar in. Crypto investments&nbsp;
cannot be anything but a zero sum game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:51.360
and many are actually massively negative sum. In&nbsp;
order to presume a crypto investment functions as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.360 --> 00:38:56.800
a store of value we simultaneously need to&nbsp;
suppose an infinite chain of greater fools&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:56.800 --> 00:39:01.680
who keep buying these assets at any&nbsp;
irrational price and into the future forever.”

00:39:03.520 --> 00:39:06.880
So with all that out of the way,&nbsp;
let’s talk about the ape in the room.

00:39:06.880 --> 00:39:12.400
♪ NFTs super-sexy, man it always lures me ♪
♪ I’m too focused and you know you’ll never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:12.400 --> 00:39:14.514
ever deter me ♪
♪ Crypto astronaut ♪&nbsp;

00:39:14.514 --> 00:39:16.640
♪ Degen moving so hazardous ♪
♪ Crypto what I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.640 --> 00:39:20.040
gon’ dabble uh ♪
♪ OpenSea is like chapel yeah ♪

00:39:20.720 --> 00:39:24.080
NFTs, non-fungible tokens.

00:39:24.080 --> 00:39:27.440
On a conceptual level the&nbsp;
tech acts as a sort of generic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.440 --> 00:39:31.280
database to mediate the exchange of digital stuff.

00:39:31.280 --> 00:39:36.480
The most optimistic read on it is a framework for&nbsp;
a type of computer code that creates so-called&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:36.480 --> 00:39:41.520
true digital objects, meaning digital&nbsp;
objects that possess the attributes of both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:45.600
physical objects and digital,&nbsp;
being losslessly-transmittable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:49.840
while providing strict uniqueness,&nbsp;
which is an important concept.

00:39:49.840 --> 00:39:53.680
Strict uniqueness is a way of&nbsp;
saying that two different things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:53.680 --> 00:39:58.880
are different things, even if they’re&nbsp;
different copies of the same thing.

00:39:59.440 --> 00:40:05.520
My copy of Grey by EL James is strictly unique.&nbsp;
While millions of copies of the book exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:05.520 --> 00:40:11.040
this is the only copy that is this copy,&nbsp;
and this is the only copy that has the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:11.040 --> 00:40:15.120
very specific damage of being glued&nbsp;
shut and thrown into the Bow River.

00:40:15.640 --> 00:40:16.640
[splash]

00:40:16.640 --> 00:40:20.880
It is also strictly scarce. While&nbsp;
millions of copies of Grey exist,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:20.880 --> 00:40:24.080
that’s still a finite number,&nbsp;
meaning it is possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:24.080 --> 00:40:28.960
though not immediately practical,&nbsp;
for the world to run out of copies.

00:40:28.960 --> 00:40:32.880
NFTs impose a simulacrum of this&nbsp;
physical scarcity and uniqueness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.880 --> 00:40:35.360
onto digital objects within their ecosystem.

00:40:36.440 --> 00:40:37.440
[splash]

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:42.320
On a technical level a non-fungible token&nbsp;
is just a token that has a unique serial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.320 --> 00:40:45.680
number and can’t be subdivided into smaller parts.

00:40:45.680 --> 00:40:48.800
Two tokens, even two that tokenize the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:48.800 --> 00:40:53.440
conceptual thing, are still strictly&nbsp;
unique with different serial numbers.

00:40:53.440 --> 00:40:56.880
Within the context of Ethereum&nbsp;
and its clones these tokens are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:56.880 --> 00:41:01.520
effectively a small packet of data&nbsp;
that can contain a payload of code.

00:41:01.520 --> 00:41:05.120
It’s a box that you can put a micro program into.

00:41:05.120 --> 00:41:10.000
That micro-program is called a smart contract, a&nbsp;
name that is so comically full of itself that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:10.000 --> 00:41:14.880
feel like it’s misleading to even call them&nbsp;
that, but I have to for simplicity's sake.

00:41:14.880 --> 00:41:18.960
But real quick, yes, that is how the&nbsp;
inventors conceptualize the role of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:18.960 --> 00:41:22.000
these things, as the unity of programming and law.

00:41:22.000 --> 00:41:25.920
The phrase “code is law” gets bandied&nbsp;
about as an aphorism, and it’s just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:25.920 --> 00:41:29.760
so full of holes that we’re going to be&nbsp;
spending the next forever talking about it.

00:41:29.760 --> 00:41:32.080
Before we move into that this is the root of it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.080 --> 00:41:37.440
there is a tremendous disconnect between what NFT&nbsp;
advocates say they do and what they actually do.

00:41:37.440 --> 00:41:39.680
But, and this is very important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:39.680 --> 00:41:45.280
both the claimed functionality and&nbsp;
the actual functionality are both bad.

00:41:45.280 --> 00:41:47.600
It’s all broken, none of it works well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:47.600 --> 00:41:52.000
so the idea of it becoming the norm is&nbsp;
terrible, but the prospect of what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:52.000 --> 00:41:58.880
world would look like if all the mythologizing&nbsp;
and over-promising came true also super sucks.

00:41:58.880 --> 00:42:03.760
The end goal of this infinite machine&nbsp;
is the financialization of everything.

00:42:03.760 --> 00:42:07.520
Any benefits of digital&nbsp;
uniqueness end up being a quirk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:07.520 --> 00:42:12.080
a necessary precondition of turning&nbsp;
everything into a stock market.

00:42:12.080 --> 00:42:16.960
There’s nothing particularly offensive&nbsp;
underpinning the concept of digital collectibles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:16.960 --> 00:42:21.760
frameworks and subcultures for&nbsp;
digital collectibles have existed for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:21.760 --> 00:42:25.760
decades within various contexts, but NFTs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:25.760 --> 00:42:32.400
exist to lend credibility and functionality to&nbsp;
the cryptocurrencies that they exist on top of.

00:42:32.400 --> 00:42:35.040
Okay, okay, okay, code is law.

00:42:35.040 --> 00:42:40.080
Now, what that little smart contract&nbsp;
program does is up to the token creator.

00:42:40.080 --> 00:42:44.960
It can be a relatively sophisticated applet that&nbsp;
allows the user to execute various commands,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:44.960 --> 00:42:50.800
or it can be a plain hypertext link to a&nbsp;
static URL of an image, or anything in between.

00:42:50.800 --> 00:42:55.520
Most are a lot closer to static URLs&nbsp;
than they are to functional programs.

00:42:55.520 --> 00:42:59.040
This is in part because the thing&nbsp;
that NFTs have become synonymous with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:59.040 --> 00:43:03.920
are digital artworks, but really what&nbsp;
the token represents is arbitrary.

00:43:03.920 --> 00:43:07.280
It can be a video game item, a&nbsp;
permission slip, a subscription,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:07.280 --> 00:43:12.560
a domain name, a virus that steals all your&nbsp;
digital stuff, or a combination of all of those.

00:43:12.560 --> 00:43:16.640
While the concept has been floating around since&nbsp;
2015 when the framework was thrown together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:22.160
in a day during a hack-a-thon, and the first&nbsp;
Ethereum implemented tokens were minted in 2017,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:22.160 --> 00:43:28.560
for the mainstream the story starts in the spring&nbsp;
of 2021 following a string of high-priced sales&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:28.560 --> 00:43:34.400
of tokens minted by digital artist Beeple,&nbsp;
culminating in a $69 million dollar sale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:34.400 --> 00:43:39.360
of a collage of Beeple’s work in March&nbsp;
via old money auction house Christie’s.

00:43:39.360 --> 00:43:44.160
This high-profile sale triggered a media&nbsp;
frenzy and an online gold rush as various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:49.760
minor internet celebrities raced to cash in on&nbsp;
the trend, hoping to be the next to cash out big.

00:43:49.760 --> 00:43:52.560
Over the course of about six&nbsp;
weeks the ecosystem burnt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:52.560 --> 00:43:55.200
through just about every relevant meme possible.

00:43:55.200 --> 00:43:59.440
Laina Morris, popularized on Reddit as&nbsp;
Overly Attached Girlfriend, sold the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:59.440 --> 00:44:03.200
“original screengrab” to Emirati&nbsp;
music producer Farzin Fardin Fard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:03.200 --> 00:44:06.160
for the equivalent of a little over $400,000 US.

00:44:06.720 --> 00:44:08.720
Zoe Roth, aka Disaster Girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:08.720 --> 00:44:13.280
sold the original Disaster Girl&nbsp;
photograph to Fard, for also $400,000.

00:44:14.720 --> 00:44:20.960
Kyle Craven sold the original Bad Luck Brian&nbsp;
photo for $36,000 to anonymous buyer @A.

00:44:20.960 --> 00:44:27.200
Nyan Cat sold for almost $600,000 with&nbsp;
multiple variants also selling for six figures.

00:44:27.200 --> 00:44:30.720
Allison Harvard sold Creepy&nbsp;
Chan I and II for $67,000 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:31.440 --> 00:44:34.000
$83,000, respectively, also both to Fard.

00:44:34.000 --> 00:44:38.960
In addition to these high-profile sales of things&nbsp;
that were already popular, whale buyers like Fard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:38.960 --> 00:44:43.040
were dropping four and five figure sales&nbsp;
onto a random assortment of other artworks.

00:44:43.040 --> 00:44:46.880
Corporations and individuals auctioned&nbsp;
off NFTs representing intangible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:46.880 --> 00:44:51.840
non-transferable concepts, like the&nbsp;
“first tweet” or “the first text message.”

00:44:51.840 --> 00:44:56.160
Cryptocurrency evangelists jumped at&nbsp;
every opportunity to proselytize this new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:56.160 --> 00:45:00.160
bold, revolutionary marketplace that&nbsp;
would free artists from the yoke of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:00.160 --> 00:45:03.840
the gig economy and provide buyers&nbsp;
with an immutable record of ownership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:03.840 --> 00:45:07.520
of authenticated artworks stored&nbsp;
on an eternal distributed machine.

00:45:07.520 --> 00:45:12.160
You could be holding the next generation’s&nbsp;
Picasso! Artists could continue to earn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:12.160 --> 00:45:17.760
passive revenue from secondary sales!&nbsp;
Imagine what it’ll be worth in five years!

00:45:17.760 --> 00:45:22.720
This created an air of absolute mania, as it&nbsp;
seemed like anything could be the golden ticket.

00:45:22.720 --> 00:45:26.000
Digital artists, especially those&nbsp;
working with media like generative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:30.240
art that’s difficult to monetize via&nbsp;
conventional channels like physical prints,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:30.240 --> 00:45:34.400
raced into the space and, on&nbsp;
the whole, lost a lot of money.

00:45:34.400 --> 00:45:38.000
Critics began questioning what it was&nbsp;
that was actually being bought and sold.

00:45:38.000 --> 00:45:42.320
Copyright? Commercial permissions?&nbsp;
A digital file? Bragging rights?

00:45:42.320 --> 00:45:46.480
In a huge number of cases the answer&nbsp;
wasn’t very clear, and even the sellers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:46.480 --> 00:45:51.760
caught in the promise of a payday, weren’t&nbsp;
altogether sure what they had even sold.

00:45:51.760 --> 00:45:56.480
Tons of the tokens did little more than point to&nbsp;
images stored on normal servers readily accessible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:56.480 --> 00:46:02.400
via HTTP, meaning the bought assets would be&nbsp;
just as vulnerable to link rot as anything else.

00:46:02.400 --> 00:46:09.600
Some pointed to images stored on peer-to-peer IPFS&nbsp;
servers, which are more resistant to link rot as,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:09.600 --> 00:46:14.720
similar to a torrent, it only requires that&nbsp;
someone keep the file active somewhere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:14.720 --> 00:46:20.080
rather than requiring the original server to&nbsp;
stay up, but as millions of dead torrents prove:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:20.080 --> 00:46:24.960
that’s still a far cry from the “eternal”&nbsp;
storage solution that evangelists were claiming.

00:46:24.960 --> 00:46:29.040
The images were stored and delivered the&nbsp;
same way as any other image on the internet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:32.960
easily saved or duplicated simply&nbsp;
by virtue of the fact that in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:32.960 --> 00:46:36.640
for your computer to display an&nbsp;
image it needs to download it.

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:43.280
Claims of digital scarcity apply only to the&nbsp;
token itself, not the thing the token signified.

00:46:43.280 --> 00:46:47.760
More than that, there was no cryptographic&nbsp;
relationship between the images and the tokens.

00:46:47.760 --> 00:46:52.000
The image associated with a token&nbsp;
could be easily altered or replaced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:52.000 --> 00:46:57.840
if the person with access to the server that&nbsp;
the image was hosted on just changed file names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:03.040
making the relationship between the two tenuous&nbsp;
and flimsy in a way that undermined the claims&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:03.040 --> 00:47:08.880
that this was somehow a more durable,&nbsp;
reliable way of transacting digital art.

00:47:08.880 --> 00:47:12.160
There was also no root proof of authenticity, no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:12.160 --> 00:47:16.880
confirmation that the person minting the&nbsp;
artwork was the person who created the artwork.

00:47:16.880 --> 00:47:21.680
This is a pretty handy encapsulation of the&nbsp;
way that blockchain fails to solve the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:28.160
common problems with fraud, which tend to start&nbsp;
with bad data going into a system at the input,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:28.160 --> 00:47:31.120
not data being altered mid-stream.

00:47:31.120 --> 00:47:33.920
Artists who complained about&nbsp;
this system which clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:33.920 --> 00:47:38.480
incentivized impersonating popular&nbsp;
artists, including deceased artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:38.480 --> 00:47:42.720
were told that it was their fault for&nbsp;
not jumping in sooner, that if they had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:42.720 --> 00:47:48.160
bought in and minted their stuff first then&nbsp;
it would be easy to prove the forgeries.

00:47:48.160 --> 00:47:52.000
Because evangelists don’t see this as&nbsp;
a tool, as a market that may or may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:52.000 --> 00:47:55.120
not fit into an artist’s business, they see it as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:47:55.120 --> 00:48:00.160
the future, so failure to participate isn’t&nbsp;
a business decision, it’s just a mistake.

00:48:00.720 --> 00:48:02.080
They’re terrible people.

00:48:02.080 --> 00:48:04.080
All told the frenzy collapsed pretty quickly.

00:48:04.080 --> 00:48:06.960
By June the market had&nbsp;
already receded by about 90%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:12.160
which, incidentally, just further incentivized&nbsp;
the low-effort process of minting other peoples’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:12.160 --> 00:48:17.280
artwork, to the point that art platform Deviant&nbsp;
Art implemented an extremely well received feature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:17.280 --> 00:48:21.120
that scans several popular NFT&nbsp;
marketplaces for matching images.

00:48:21.760 --> 00:48:24.480
The output of that is extremely depressing.

00:48:25.040 --> 00:48:29.520
Not to labour this point, but reposting&nbsp;
digital art without attribution is nothing new.

00:48:29.520 --> 00:48:32.560
Profiting off someone else's&nbsp;
art is also nothing new.

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:36.080
All that’s new is NFTs represent&nbsp;
a high-energy marketplace with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:36.080 --> 00:48:40.880
irrational pricing culture where the mean buyer is&nbsp;
easily flattered and not particularly discerning.

00:48:40.880 --> 00:48:45.600
The potential payoff is extremely high, much,&nbsp;
much higher than a bootleg Redbubble store,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:45.600 --> 00:48:50.400
the consequences border nonexistent, and&nbsp;
the market is clearly in an untenable state,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:50.400 --> 00:48:55.040
so there’s an incentive to get in at as&nbsp;
low a cost as possible before it collapses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:55.040 --> 00:48:57.200
hence the absolute plague of art theft.

00:48:57.200 --> 00:48:59.840
Even the argument that artists&nbsp;
could make passive revenue off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:48:59.840 --> 00:49:02.880
secondary sales turned out to&nbsp;
have a lot of caveats attached.

00:49:02.880 --> 00:49:06.880
One, the smart contract for the token needs&nbsp;
to have a function that defines royalties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:10.560
so anyone who minted a token based&nbsp;
off of hype making it sound like an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:10.560 --> 00:49:13.120
inherent function of the system was out of luck.

00:49:13.120 --> 00:49:18.800
And, two, the token doesn’t know what a sale&nbsp;
is and can’t differentiate between being sold&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:18.800 --> 00:49:23.920
and being transferred, so it’s actually&nbsp;
the marketplace that informs the token&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:23.920 --> 00:49:26.800
“you’re being sold” and collects the royalties.

00:49:26.800 --> 00:49:31.040
End result, royalties are easily bypassed&nbsp;
simply by using a marketplace that doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:31.040 --> 00:49:34.560
collect royalties or uses a different&nbsp;
format of royalty collection that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:34.560 --> 00:49:37.040
incompatible with the function the token uses.

00:49:37.040 --> 00:49:41.360
While a few sellers, legitimate and otherwise,&nbsp;
made off with undeniably big paydays,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:41.360 --> 00:49:45.280
hundreds of thousands of artists bought&nbsp;
in only to find that there wasn’t a new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:45.280 --> 00:49:48.720
revolutionary, highly trafficked&nbsp;
audience of digital art collectors.

00:49:48.720 --> 00:49:53.120
Instead there was a closed market dealing in&nbsp;
casino chips where the primary winners were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:53.120 --> 00:49:57.680
those already connected, who already had the means&nbsp;
to get the attention of the whales and the media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:49:57.680 --> 00:50:02.800
a market where participation required buying&nbsp;
into a cryptocurrency at a rapidly fluctuating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:02.800 --> 00:50:07.440
price in order to pay the minting costs to&nbsp;
post the work, where it would sit, unsold.

00:50:07.440 --> 00:50:10.800
This left those artists in the lurch,&nbsp;
where they had to choose between just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:10.800 --> 00:50:15.440
eating the losses, or attempting to convince&nbsp;
their existing audience to buy-in as well.

00:50:15.440 --> 00:50:19.680
The people who actually won were the people&nbsp;
with large holdings of cryptocurrency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:19.680 --> 00:50:24.240
specifically Ethereum which mediated the&nbsp;
vast majority of these big ticket purchases.

00:50:24.240 --> 00:50:27.120
David Gerrard, author of Attack&nbsp;
of the 50 Foot Blockchain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:27.120 --> 00:50:30.160
summarized it on his blog very succinctly as such:

00:50:30.160 --> 00:50:34.800
“NFTs are entirely for the benefit of the crypto&nbsp;
grifters. The only purpose the artists serve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:34.800 --> 00:50:38.560
is as aspiring suckers to pump the&nbsp;
concept of crypto — and, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:38.560 --> 00:50:41.600
to buy cryptocurrency to pay for ‘minting’ NFTs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:41.600 --> 00:50:45.360
Sometimes the artist gets some crumbs to&nbsp;
keep them pumping the concept of crypto.”

00:50:45.360 --> 00:50:50.240
The rush benefits them in two ways: first&nbsp;
the price of Ether itself goes up directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:50.240 --> 00:50:55.360
from the spike in demand, between January and&nbsp;
May the price of Ether rose from $700 to $4000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:50:56.480 --> 00:51:00.880
and second there’s new actual buyers&nbsp;
who aren’t just trading Bitcoin for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:00.880 --> 00:51:04.320
tether for ether and back again,&nbsp;
but are buying in with dollars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:04.320 --> 00:51:09.120
providing the whole system with the liquidity&nbsp;
needed for whales to actually cash out.

00:51:09.120 --> 00:51:13.840
This arrangement, needing to buy a highly&nbsp;
volatile coin from people who paid far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:13.840 --> 00:51:17.680
far less for it in order to participate&nbsp;
in a market that they control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:17.680 --> 00:51:20.960
is why people reflexively describe&nbsp;
the whole arrangement as a scam.

00:51:20.960 --> 00:51:26.240
If you buy in at $4000 and compete against&nbsp;
people who bought in at $4, you’re the sucker.

00:51:26.240 --> 00:51:30.320
It reveals the basic truth that these&nbsp;
aren’t marketplaces, they’re casinos.

00:51:30.320 --> 00:51:32.400
And, indeed, even through all the rhetoric&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:32.400 --> 00:51:36.480
of “protecting artists” and whatnot was&nbsp;
the ever-present spectre of the gamble:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:36.480 --> 00:51:40.400
whatever you buy now might be&nbsp;
worth hundreds of times more later.

00:51:40.400 --> 00:51:44.240
Constantly invoked is the opposite&nbsp;
proposition of the bad deal:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:44.240 --> 00:51:47.600
what if you had been the&nbsp;
person who bought in at $4?

00:51:48.560 --> 00:51:53.120
Let’s take a closer look at that eye&nbsp;
watering $69 million Beeple sale.

00:51:54.000 --> 00:51:58.480
Independent journalist Amy Castor has done an&nbsp;
enviable job running down the details here in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:51:58.480 --> 00:52:04.560
her piece “Metakovan, the mystery Beeple art&nbsp;
buyer, and his NFT/DeFi scheme“ but to summarize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:04.560 --> 00:52:08.560
the long and short of her notes, the buyer is&nbsp;
a crypto entrepreneur named Vignesh Sundaresan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:08.560 --> 00:52:13.120
who purchased the piece to boost the reputation&nbsp;
and value of his own crypto investment scheme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:13.120 --> 00:52:19.760
Metapurse and Metapurse’s own token B.20,&nbsp;
which Beeple owns 2% of the total supply of.

00:52:19.760 --> 00:52:26.240
Following the Christie's sale the reported&nbsp;
value of B.20 went from 36¢ per token to $23.

00:52:27.280 --> 00:52:29.600
And that’s just the anatomy of a single sale.

00:52:29.600 --> 00:52:35.040
The very obvious conclusion observers reached&nbsp;
was that none of this is about the art at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:35.040 --> 00:52:38.400
but the speculative value;&nbsp;
not what it’s worth to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:38.400 --> 00:52:41.840
but what it’ll potentially be worth&nbsp;
in the future to someone else.

00:52:41.840 --> 00:52:46.160
It’s not a market, it’s a casino, gambling on the&nbsp;
receipt for an image or video that’s otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:46.160 --> 00:52:47.840
infinitely digitally replicable.

00:52:47.840 --> 00:52:52.720
The thing itself is immaterial as&nbsp;
long as it can make a line go up.

00:52:52.720 --> 00:52:56.720
This is the essence of the market,&nbsp;
and a microcosm of what evangelists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:52:56.720 --> 00:53:01.440
imagine they want to see as the future,&nbsp;
the financialization of everything.

00:53:01.440 --> 00:53:04.800
The frenzied market around old Reddit&nbsp;
memes was doomed from the start.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:04.800 --> 00:53:09.440
There’s a finite supply of meaningful&nbsp;
originals, so to keep this thing going,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:09.440 --> 00:53:17.040
to keep the line going up, you need something&nbsp;
more, something less tied to anything specific.

00:53:23.320 --> 00:53:24.320
--

00:53:24.320 --> 00:53:27.920
In the months since the initial&nbsp;
craze the marketplace had mutated.

00:53:27.920 --> 00:53:31.680
The It Thing was no longer memes or&nbsp;
digital works from established artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:31.680 --> 00:53:36.960
but character profiles from large, procedurally&nbsp;
generated collections with names like CryptoPunks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:36.960 --> 00:53:41.040
Bored Ape Yacht Club, Lazy Lions,&nbsp;
Cool Cats, Ether Gals, Gator World,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:41.040 --> 00:53:45.680
Baby Llama Club, Magic Mushroom Club,&nbsp;
Rogue Society Bot, and Crypto Chicks.

00:53:45.680 --> 00:53:51.200
These were notable for, again, commanding utterly&nbsp;
irrational prices seemingly completely decoupled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:53:51.200 --> 00:53:57.440
from any legitimate or legal transaction,&nbsp;
and being generally extremely fugly.

00:53:57.440 --> 00:54:01.600
This surge generated an immediately&nbsp;
adversarial relationship between the buyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:01.600 --> 00:54:05.440
who touted them as both evidence&nbsp;
of their extravagant wealth and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:05.440 --> 00:54:11.200
their foresight into the future of digital&nbsp;
economies, and pretty much everyone else.

00:54:11.200 --> 00:54:16.160
A very common response to anyone using one of&nbsp;
these profile pics, or bragging about their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:16.160 --> 00:54:21.840
purchase, became saving and reposting the image&nbsp;
in reply, or even changing profile pics to match.

00:54:22.400 --> 00:54:25.760
Spurred by a speculative tweet from&nbsp;
Twitter developers suggesting that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:25.760 --> 00:54:30.400
they were working on an NFT verification&nbsp;
system, Twitter users broadly rallied at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:30.400 --> 00:54:34.480
opportunity to immediately identify&nbsp;
people that it was okay to bully.

00:54:34.480 --> 00:54:38.720
Tweets from within the cryptosphere started&nbsp;
leaking out, things like Santiago Santos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:54:38.720 --> 00:54:45.840
tweeting things like “NFTs = Identity 2.0. Can’t&nbsp;
remember the last time I used my pic on the left.”

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:50.800
Emphasis on physical traits. Genetic&nbsp;
lottery. Prone to bias and prejudice.

00:54:50.800 --> 00:54:56.240
Identity by choice. Unique and digitally&nbsp;
scarce. Representation of values and beliefs.

00:54:56.800 --> 00:55:01.840
Of course what Santiago was leaving out was&nbsp;
that he had paid the equivalent of $171,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:02.560 --> 00:55:09.520
US for that profile pic, which probably gives him&nbsp;
some incentive to really commit to it as something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:09.520 --> 00:55:13.520
interesting and special, even&nbsp;
though, stripped to the studs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:14.240 --> 00:55:20.080
literally all he was describing was using a&nbsp;
non-representational image as a profile pic.

00:55:20.080 --> 00:55:26.240
For about six years my profile pic on the Dungeons&nbsp;
and Dragons forums was a stock photo of a cabbage.

00:55:26.800 --> 00:55:30.800
So, not really breaking new ground here.

00:55:30.800 --> 00:55:34.880
Greg Isenberg sat at his computer and&nbsp;
decided to bang out “most people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:34.880 --> 00:55:37.600
make fun of NFTs
- Own zero NFTs&nbsp;

00:55:37.600 --> 00:55:41.520
- Have never minted an NFT
- Have never participated in a community&nbsp;

00:55:41.520 --> 00:55:45.840
- Have never staked their NFT
- Haven’t built on-top of an NFT project&nbsp;

00:55:45.840 --> 00:55:51.520
- Have never earned an NFT playing a game
- Missed out on BAYC, Punks, Cool Cats etc.”

00:55:51.520 --> 00:55:56.320
A chain of logic implying that skeptics of&nbsp;
the market are just uninformed sore losers.

00:55:56.320 --> 00:55:59.760
Incidents like these lent to the very&nbsp;
accurate perception that the loudest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:55:59.760 --> 00:56:03.440
voices in NFTs weren’t very familiar&nbsp;
with internet culture as a whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:03.440 --> 00:56:08.720
weren’t terribly smart in general, and were by and&nbsp;
large coming at all this from the financial side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:08.720 --> 00:56:13.920
further supported by all of them having “tweets&nbsp;
are not investment advice” in their twitter bios.

00:56:13.920 --> 00:56:22.160
In short the same people&nbsp;
who made and bought Juicero.

00:56:22.720 --> 00:56:26.080
It also laid bare that the&nbsp;
conversations were inherently suspect.

00:56:26.080 --> 00:56:30.000
How can you ever trust the sincerity&nbsp;
of someone telling you how awesome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:30.000 --> 00:56:37.360
their $171,000 investment is when your&nbsp;
persuasion stands to directly benefit them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:37.360 --> 00:56:41.120
by potentially driving up the value&nbsp;
of their investment portfolio?

00:56:41.120 --> 00:56:48.480
You’ve gotta be extremely rich for&nbsp;
$171,000 to not be an extremely sunk cost,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:48.480 --> 00:56:53.760
and that’s absolutely going to weigh on your&nbsp;
mind and influence how you see the market.

00:56:53.760 --> 00:56:57.600
So for my part I tweeted about several&nbsp;
of these incidents and was, in response,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:56:57.600 --> 00:57:01.920
hounded for days by annoying people with&nbsp;
NFT profile pics who insisted that I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:01.920 --> 00:57:05.680
just didn’t get it, I wasn’t seeing the community.

00:57:05.680 --> 00:57:07.760
So I decided to go see the community.

00:57:07.760 --> 00:57:11.360
My experiment started with the Cool Cats&nbsp;
Discord, one of the communities that I’d&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:11.360 --> 00:57:16.400
been assured was top of the pile. I&nbsp;
joined, read back through days and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:16.400 --> 00:57:22.400
days of conversation logs, watched the active&nbsp;
conversations, and was generally unimpressed.

00:57:22.400 --> 00:57:24.160
It was, at best, unremarkable.

00:57:24.160 --> 00:57:28.560
A primary concern was still rooted&nbsp;
in the monetary value of the tokens.

00:57:28.560 --> 00:57:31.760
“If you spend 1 million on a cat,&nbsp;
it’s not just for Twitter and no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:31.760 --> 00:57:34.880
one would be happy seeing someone&nbsp;
mint another one later for free.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:34.880 --> 00:57:39.840
That’s why punks are at 120 Eth…&nbsp;
symbols… memes… theres’ the value.”

00:57:41.120 --> 00:57:44.720
What wasn’t unremarkable is the&nbsp;
way that I was immediately deluged&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:44.720 --> 00:57:47.760
by bots sending me invites to other Discords.

00:57:47.760 --> 00:57:51.760
So, between the spam I was receiving&nbsp;
and the Cool Cats’ dedicated “shill”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:51.760 --> 00:57:54.400
channel I came up with an idea.

00:57:54.400 --> 00:57:59.360
♪ Rap crypto, y’all know Bitcoin ♪
♪ I want to thank y’all for checking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:57:59.360 --> 00:58:01.600
out this joint ♪
♪ See I’m Vandal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:01.600 --> 00:58:05.611
the token rapper ♪
♪ And this right here is a brand new chapter ♪&nbsp;

00:58:05.611 --> 00:58:11.880
♪ Follow me as I go Rambo ♪
♪ To the moon in my brand new Lambo ♪

00:58:12.800 --> 00:58:17.760
For days now I’ve been accepting every&nbsp;
spam NFT Discord invite I've received,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:17.760 --> 00:58:19.200
and it's getting dire.

00:58:19.200 --> 00:58:24.960
Stoner Cats, Oni Ronin, Magic Mushroom Club,&nbsp;
NFTITS, The Humanoids, EtherGals, Cool Cats, Senzu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:24.960 --> 00:58:30.320
Seeds, Pro Camel Riders, Long Ween Club, Stick&nbsp;
Humans, Bumping Uglies, World of Wojak, GenMAP,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:30.320 --> 00:58:36.720
DIMEZ, MagicMarblesNFT, Beverly Hills Car Club,&nbsp;
Cash Cows, Long Neck Cartel, Pug Force, HoodPunks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:36.720 --> 00:58:42.480
Gorilla Club, Alien Archives, Betting Buddahs,&nbsp;
Fighter Turtles Club, The Iconimals, Basement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:42.480 --> 00:58:48.720
Dwellers, Wizard Man Jenkins, KATI, METAMONZ,&nbsp;
CrazySkullz, BoxGang, Gym Punks, Unc0vered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:48.720 --> 00:58:55.200
Nitropunk, Crypto Bowls, Masquerade Massacre,&nbsp;
Cat Colony, SkelFtees, Daffy Panda Ganging Up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:55.200 --> 00:58:59.600
Betting Kongs, Wolves of Wall Street, The Llama&nbsp;
Farm, Happy Sharks, Teacup Pigs, Cool Llamas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:58:59.600 --> 00:59:04.160
Mad Carrot Gang, Barnyard Fashionistas,&nbsp;
Sol Cities, Oink Club, Outlaw Punks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:04.160 --> 00:59:08.880
Crypto Astronuts, NFT Worlds, Panda&nbsp;
Paradise, Time Travellers, CyberKongz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:08.880 --> 00:59:13.600
Party Ape Billionaire club, J Corps,&nbsp;
KwyptoKados, MetaBirds, and that’s about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:13.600 --> 00:59:18.640
as far as I got before more or less giving&nbsp;
up and finally shutting off Discord DMs.

00:59:18.640 --> 00:59:22.320
Now, this is a fraction of a fraction&nbsp;
of what’s out there, but I do think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:22.320 --> 00:59:26.640
it’s a pretty representative&nbsp;
sample, and I learned a lot.

00:59:26.640 --> 00:59:30.560
For example, the basic psychological&nbsp;
profile of the average buyer is someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:30.560 --> 00:59:35.360
who is tenuously middle class, socially&nbsp;
isolated, and highly responsive to memes.

00:59:35.360 --> 00:59:40.080
They are someone who has very little experience&nbsp;
with real businesses and production processes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:40.080 --> 00:59:44.880
thus are unlikely to be turned off by&nbsp;
unrealistic claims about future returns.

00:59:44.880 --> 00:59:47.600
They are insecure about their lack of knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:47.600 --> 00:59:51.440
and this makes them very susceptible to&nbsp;
flattery, in particular being reassured&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:59:51.440 --> 00:59:57.120
that the only reason for negativity is&nbsp;
because critics just don’t understand.

00:59:57.680 --> 01:00:01.680
Being tenuously middle class gives them&nbsp;
enough disposable income to engage with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:01.680 --> 01:00:07.600
a pretty expensive system, but also a very&nbsp;
potent anxiety about their financial future.

01:00:07.600 --> 01:00:10.080
It goes without saying that&nbsp;
they’re fixated on money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:10.080 --> 01:00:14.080
and they principally understand the&nbsp;
technology as a means of making money.

01:00:14.080 --> 01:00:19.600
Criticism of the system is typically met&nbsp;
with confusion. Don’t you want to make money?

01:00:19.600 --> 01:00:24.560
I also learned a lot about fraud, and how&nbsp;
to do it both on purpose and by accident.

01:00:24.560 --> 01:00:29.600
The term “rug pull” with its derivations “rug” and&nbsp;
“rugged”, all to describe projects that made big&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:29.600 --> 01:00:34.880
promises but then took the money and ran, embedded&nbsp;
themselves in my vocabulary extremely quickly.

01:00:34.880 --> 01:00:38.320
The market is just absolutely&nbsp;
lousy with fraud and deception.

01:00:38.320 --> 01:00:42.080
Wash trading, where you sell something&nbsp;
to your own sockpuppets in order to lure&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:42.080 --> 01:00:45.440
in a real buyer who thinks they’re&nbsp;
getting a good deal, is rampant.

01:00:45.440 --> 01:00:49.440
Market manipulation is so common and&nbsp;
accepted that it’s actually considered bad&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:49.440 --> 01:00:54.560
form if project leaders don’t actively engage&nbsp;
in it, like it’s considered disrespectful&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:00:54.560 --> 01:01:00.080
to the buyers if the project leaders&nbsp;
don’t help inflate the resale price.

01:01:00.080 --> 01:01:04.000
Also, and I really should not leave&nbsp;
this merely implied, the art is bad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:04.000 --> 01:01:06.240
but not in any sort of interesting way.

01:01:06.240 --> 01:01:08.640
It’s bad in a smoothed over copycat way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:08.640 --> 01:01:14.080
a low effort garbage dump from artists who have&nbsp;
largely given up on having ideas or opinions.

01:01:14.080 --> 01:01:16.640
Derivative, lazy, ugly, hollow, and boring.

01:01:16.640 --> 01:01:20.320
The capacity for original thought long having&nbsp;
been drained out of the illustrators kept in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:20.320 --> 01:01:24.080
the employ and proximity of people who&nbsp;
refuse to shut up about cryptocurrency,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:24.080 --> 01:01:29.120
there is an overwhelming tendency to fall&nbsp;
back on stale memes and self-flattery.

01:01:29.120 --> 01:01:34.480
A massive volume of cryptocurrency art ends&nbsp;
up being art about cryptocurrency, transparent&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:34.480 --> 01:01:39.760
pandering to an audience that is either deeply&nbsp;
stupid or easily pleased and quite possibly both.

01:01:39.760 --> 01:01:43.200
Absent any artistic insight, the vacuum&nbsp;
is instead filled to overflowing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:43.200 --> 01:01:46.960
references to doge memes, Bitcoin,&nbsp;
ethereum, stonks, to the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:46.960 --> 01:01:51.760
buy the dip, good morning, and desperate&nbsp;
pleas for sempai Elon Musk to notice them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:51.760 --> 01:01:55.680
all presented with the earnestness and&nbsp;
authenticity of a Pickle Rick bong.

01:01:55.680 --> 01:01:58.960
It's tempting to say that my&nbsp;
approach, accepting every spam invite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:01:58.960 --> 01:02:03.840
was a really flawed way to delve deeper,&nbsp;
that "obviously" accepting invites from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:03.840 --> 01:02:09.280
spam bots would mainly lead me into less&nbsp;
stable projects, but surprisingly no.

01:02:09.280 --> 01:02:13.440
I got just as much spam for successful&nbsp;
projects like Humanoids and NFT Worlds&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:13.440 --> 01:02:16.640
as I did for rug-pulls like&nbsp;
Crypto Astronuts and Hood Punks.

01:02:16.640 --> 01:02:20.080
On the whole, if you just look at the&nbsp;
pitch package and the sample product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:20.080 --> 01:02:24.560
there's very little material difference between&nbsp;
a project that's gonna sell out 10,000 tokens&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:24.560 --> 01:02:28.800
in six hours and one that's going to become a&nbsp;
trash fire as the project leader has a nervous&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:28.800 --> 01:02:33.200
breakdown and burns the mint three days&nbsp;
post-launch after only selling 800 tokens.

01:02:33.200 --> 01:02:37.120
Party Ape Billionaire Club sold&nbsp;
out, bringing in about $3.2 million,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:37.120 --> 01:02:40.400
and are now flexing their wealth by&nbsp;
partnering with other crypto projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:40.400 --> 01:02:46.080
like the 2chains-produced NFT cartoon&nbsp;
show The Red Ape Family, which just&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:46.080 --> 01:02:50.720
really, truly shows off the community’s&nbsp;
deep pockets and commitment to quality.

01:02:50.720 --> 01:02:52.960
[humming noise]

01:02:52.960 --> 01:02:57.600
Deep in their roadmap is the promise that&nbsp;
they will start production on an MMORPG,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:02:57.600 --> 01:03:00.480
a claim that buyers are entirely faithful in.

01:03:00.480 --> 01:03:09.840
Crypto Astronuts sold 1262 of their 9485 tokens,&nbsp;
bringing in an impressive $375,000. But as nice as&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:09.840 --> 01:03:14.018
that number is, it’s not remotely enough to even&nbsp;
start proceeding with their plan to build a full&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:14.018 --> 01:03:19.440
scale PvP-based MMO. Following the failed mint&nbsp;
in October the devs slowly went quiet and then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:19.440 --> 01:03:25.200
bit by bit, websites, social media accounts, and&nbsp;
eventually the Discord itself all disappeared.

01:03:25.200 --> 01:03:30.960
Based off the ostensible product, why should&nbsp;
one of these succeed and the other fail?

01:03:30.960 --> 01:03:35.600
The key distinction, ultimately, between a Party&nbsp;
Ape Billionaire Club and a Crypto Astronuts&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:35.600 --> 01:03:40.400
is that PABC was already flush with cash and&nbsp;
able to manufacture hype with high-priced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:40.400 --> 01:03:44.560
giveaways and expensive advertising.&nbsp;
Buying advertising space in Times Square,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:44.560 --> 01:03:46.880
in particular, is a specific fixation.

01:03:46.880 --> 01:03:49.600
But running a 10 second ad&nbsp;
every eight minutes on one&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:49.600 --> 01:03:53.760
out of the way billboard in Times Square&nbsp;
isn’t effective advertising, not externally.

01:03:53.760 --> 01:03:56.400
All it’s really good for is internal propaganda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:03:56.400 --> 01:04:01.040
making insiders feel like the money that&nbsp;
they’ve spent is buying credibility.

01:04:01.040 --> 01:04:04.720
And if you’re in a speculative bubble,&nbsp;
betting that someone down the line will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:04.720 --> 01:04:09.680
buy you out for more than you bought in,&nbsp;
credibility is worth everything in the world.

01:04:15.840 --> 01:04:17.040
This is pervasive.

01:04:17.040 --> 01:04:21.840
Even in the most established projects, ones that&nbsp;
have been going for years, there's not really an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:21.840 --> 01:04:27.520
underlying thing, these aren't fandoms in the&nbsp;
way you would experience them around a game&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:27.520 --> 01:04:33.840
or a TV show or a book, the product is pretty&nbsp;
insubstantial if not functionally non-existent.

01:04:33.840 --> 01:04:36.160
This is where these projects diverge significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:36.160 --> 01:04:39.360
from the artworks that drove the&nbsp;
speculative mania in the spring.

01:04:39.360 --> 01:04:43.280
They’re not positioning themselves as&nbsp;
art that is worth possessing for the art,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:43.280 --> 01:04:49.600
as flimsy and illusory as that claim was,&nbsp;
but as ongoing projects that you buy into.

01:04:49.600 --> 01:04:54.560
The purchase of a token is an abstracted&nbsp;
version of buying early stock in a company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:04:54.560 --> 01:05:00.480
a venture-capital style investment in&nbsp;
promises, which is a very significant mutation.

01:05:00.480 --> 01:05:04.240
Okay, so what do these phantom&nbsp;
companies claim to exist to do?

01:05:04.800 --> 01:05:09.120
Pretty much all of them promise direct&nbsp;
financial returns in some way shape or form.

01:05:09.120 --> 01:05:12.560
Sometimes this is just a nebulous&nbsp;
sense that the whole thing will go&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:12.560 --> 01:05:16.960
“to the moon”, meaning the value of the tokens&nbsp;
will take off and thus early buyers will be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:16.960 --> 01:05:22.000
to resell for huge profits, or more direct as&nbsp;
the project leaders promise the formation of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:22.000 --> 01:05:27.520
effectively, an unofficial hedge fund investing&nbsp;
a communal pool into other crypto products.

01:05:27.520 --> 01:05:31.280
Probably the funniest of all of these was&nbsp;
Betting Kongs, which wanted to create an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:31.280 --> 01:05:34.720
unregulated real money casino&nbsp;
in which token holders would be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:34.720 --> 01:05:40.560
explicitly, part owners, and also permitted&nbsp;
to gamble in the casino that they owned.

01:05:40.560 --> 01:05:46.480
“Betting Kongs is not your ordinary NFT&nbsp;
project, we aim to create passive income&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:05:46.480 --> 01:05:51.520
for our NFT owners by co-owning&nbsp;
a casino with a profit split.”

01:05:51.520 --> 01:05:55.440
“Welcome to the whitepaper fellow gamblers!”

01:05:55.440 --> 01:06:00.720
“Why are we doing this? To put it short,&nbsp;
we love gambling and noticed how a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:00.720 --> 01:06:06.080
of the operators out there are owned by large&nbsp;
corporations who have no care for the community&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:06.080 --> 01:06:11.520
of the service they provide… We want to&nbsp;
provide a project where everyone playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:11.520 --> 01:06:15.920
can be a co-owner of the place they’re&nbsp;
playing at and make money from it all.”

01:06:16.560 --> 01:06:21.680
Failure was probably the best outcome for&nbsp;
everyone, here, if we’re being honest.

01:06:21.680 --> 01:06:25.440
Some promise this in the form of&nbsp;
a more conventional media project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:25.440 --> 01:06:29.680
like a comic book or a cartoon&nbsp;
or a movie, or all of the above.

01:06:30.320 --> 01:06:37.120
“We have planned the creation of a series&nbsp;
of comics dedicated to the pixel girl”

01:06:37.120 --> 01:06:43.840
“The key image is static, or at least we think so&nbsp;
now. But the squeaky girl is still a seductress so&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:43.840 --> 01:06:50.720
the image of breasts will be presented in various&nbsp;
variations that our community will approve.”

01:06:51.280 --> 01:06:58.880
“Let’s imagine, there are 5,000 NFTITs holders.&nbsp;
There is a 30 million market of comics lovers in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:06:58.880 --> 01:07:06.720
the US… On average every comics lover spends&nbsp;
around 20 USD per comic per week. It means&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:06.720 --> 01:07:15.120
80$ per month and 960$ per year. The total&nbsp;
addressable market is worth 28,800,000,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:18.880 --> 01:07:28.400
USD. Let’s imagine that we take 1%&nbsp;
of this market. It’s 288,000,000 USD&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:28.960 --> 01:07:37.840
per year on the base of monthly subscriptions&nbsp;
raised in crypto. This is a cash flow.”

01:07:38.400 --> 01:07:42.320
“Nice to see some active communication,&nbsp;
and appreciate the effort (as announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:42.320 --> 01:07:46.320
this afternoon) trying to resurrect&nbsp;
this project into something successful…&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.320 --> 01:07:46.880
The proposed path forward announced this afternoon&nbsp;
is intriguing and am prepared to support. The only&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:46.880 --> 01:07:50.960
thing I would also like to see, assuming the&nbsp;
project can be successfully turned around&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:50.960 --> 01:07:55.440
is to perhaps, in addition to some revenue&nbsp;
sharing, have some revenue donated to Breast&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:07:55.440 --> 01:08:00.240
Cancer Research. To truly enjoy them,&nbsp;
we should keep them happy and healthy!”

01:08:00.240 --> 01:08:06.080
As already implied, many of them promise video&nbsp;
games, often an MMO, but just as often, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:07.040 --> 01:08:10.320
just the concept of a video game?

01:08:10.880 --> 01:08:13.280
Okay, wait, let me word that another way.

01:08:13.280 --> 01:08:15.680
They will promise “a video game”.

01:08:15.680 --> 01:08:21.200
Like, that’s the promise, in its entirety.&nbsp;
No clues for genre, style, scope, engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:21.200 --> 01:08:26.240
or platform or how any of the tokens would&nbsp;
interact with it. Just “a video game.” If&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:26.240 --> 01:08:33.920
we sell 10,000 tokens for $300 each we will start&nbsp;
brainstorming development ideas for a video game.

01:08:33.920 --> 01:08:37.840
Now, all of these tend to be in collections&nbsp;
of ten thousand, and there’s actually an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:37.840 --> 01:08:40.000
interesting sort of quirk inside that.

01:08:40.000 --> 01:08:41.840
The initial format was to mint an&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:41.840 --> 01:08:45.120
entire collection and just&nbsp;
shove them out onto the market.

01:08:45.120 --> 01:08:48.800
The cap comes into play purely&nbsp;
because you have to tell the program&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:48.800 --> 01:08:51.440
generating the garbage when to stop.

01:08:51.440 --> 01:08:55.280
However as the scheme started to&nbsp;
take off and competition rose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:55.280 --> 01:08:59.680
it became obvious that this was a sucker’s&nbsp;
way of doing it because that meant paying gas&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:08:59.680 --> 01:09:02.000
fees on everything up front, plus&nbsp;
the gas fees on the actual sale.

01:09:02.000 --> 01:09:06.000
So someone came up with a system for&nbsp;
minting a random output on demand which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:06.000 --> 01:09:08.960
shuffles the mint cost off onto the buyer.

01:09:08.960 --> 01:09:11.600
From there it all took off like wildfire.

01:09:11.600 --> 01:09:16.080
Very low up-front cost, extremely low&nbsp;
risk, plus it turns the entire system&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:09:16.080 --> 01:09:19.680
into a gacha game with different&nbsp;
rarity curves for attributes.

01:09:19.680 --> 01:09:24.800
At best these are all obfuscated&nbsp;
gambling schemes, at worst active scams.

01:09:24.800 --> 01:09:31.520
And the payoff of failure makes it extremely&nbsp;
difficult to parse one from the other.

01:09:31.520 --> 01:09:37.360
World of Wojak only sold 20 tokens to&nbsp;
11 people and still pocketed over $5000.

01:09:38.480 --> 01:09:44.320
Pro Camel Riders sold 114 tokens&nbsp;
to 71 buyers and took home $25,000.

01:09:45.280 --> 01:09:53.920
METAMONZ sold 722 of their 9999 tokens to&nbsp;
343 buyers and walked off with $211,000.

01:09:55.040 --> 01:10:03.120
Since the average buy-in is over $350, the payday&nbsp;
for failure makes it irrational to succeed.

01:10:03.120 --> 01:10:06.960
The interesting aspect of it all,&nbsp;
though, is the emergent fiction of it.

01:10:06.960 --> 01:10:11.120
These smoke vendors don’t have an actual&nbsp;
product more complex than the output of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:11.120 --> 01:10:16.640
a vending machine in the front of a grocery&nbsp;
store, so they need a story to sell instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:16.640 --> 01:10:21.440
and so we get this wave of poorly defined&nbsp;
projects pitching a token with an attached&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:21.440 --> 01:10:25.680
jpg that represents the concept of a&nbsp;
thing that could become a future business.

01:10:25.680 --> 01:10:30.080
What that business does is unimportant,&nbsp;
and indeed many of these projects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:30.080 --> 01:10:35.120
successful and otherwise, trade in a&nbsp;
flattering myth of decentralization&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:35.120 --> 01:10:39.040
where the direction of the business,&nbsp;
down to its fundamental product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:39.040 --> 01:10:44.880
is shunted into the indeterminate future to&nbsp;
be decided later by collective consensus.

01:10:44.880 --> 01:10:50.960
Are we a comic book, a movie, a hedge fund, a&nbsp;
casino, or a bimonthly curated box of snacks?&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:50.960 --> 01:10:53.840
Well that’s for the token holders to decide.

01:10:53.840 --> 01:10:58.080
All that matters is that whatever&nbsp;
it is, it will definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:10:58.080 --> 01:11:02.800
make the value of your tokens go up,&nbsp;
so you should definitely buy two.

01:11:04.240 --> 01:11:08.720
“One thing I learned about NFTs, if you think&nbsp;
something’s going to be a blue chip, you should&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:08.720 --> 01:11:12.640
definitely buy at least two, because you’re going&nbsp;
to get one, emotionally attached to one of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:13.520 --> 01:11:17.360
and you’re not going to want to sell it, because&nbsp;
NFTs are going to explode all over the world.

01:11:17.360 --> 01:11:22.880
And if you’ve got a blue chip today it’s going&nbsp;
to be incredibly more valuable in five years&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:22.880 --> 01:11:26.640
from now, or even three years from now, or&nbsp;
even two years from now, than it is today.”

01:11:26.640 --> 01:11:30.640
On the front of otherwise respectable, or&nbsp;
at least established, brands and people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:30.640 --> 01:11:35.200
getting into NFTs the results tend to&nbsp;
be extremely tepid and insubstantial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:35.200 --> 01:11:41.520
even by the standards of NFTs; very low-risk,&nbsp;
low-quality, low-engagement tokens shoveled out&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:41.520 --> 01:11:45.920
the door to capitalize on a hot buzzword,&nbsp;
chasing the cash that’s sloshing around.

01:11:50.440 --> 01:11:51.440
[Drumming]

01:11:51.440 --> 01:11:54.720
Tied up in all this, there's an&nbsp;
extremely pervasive resistance to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:11:54.720 --> 01:11:59.520
any form of skepticism that ultimately&nbsp;
manifests as a sort of toxic positivity.

01:11:59.520 --> 01:12:02.400
This is all part of a complex feedback loop.

01:12:02.400 --> 01:12:06.960
The projects, broadly speaking, lack any&nbsp;
kind of substantial product, existing almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:06.960 --> 01:12:12.640
entirely as promises backed by nothing more than&nbsp;
a screenshot of a roadmap and some sample PFPs.

01:12:12.640 --> 01:12:14.800
And, again, I think it's really important to keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:14.800 --> 01:12:18.640
in mind that that goes for successful&nbsp;
projects just as much as for rug-pulls.

01:12:18.640 --> 01:12:21.680
There really isn't any meaningful&nbsp;
difference between a Party Ape&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:21.680 --> 01:12:23.600
Billionaire Club and a Betting Kongs.

01:12:23.600 --> 01:12:28.480
Betting Kongs were never going to make a casino,&nbsp;
even if they hadn't tanked, and despite the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:28.480 --> 01:12:34.000
that they were running billboards in Time&nbsp;
Square PABC is never making an MMORPG.

01:12:34.000 --> 01:12:38.880
Both claims are equally ridiculous, but&nbsp;
one of the two made a huge pile of money.

01:12:38.880 --> 01:12:43.680
The primary product is ultimately hype,&nbsp;
which is both insubstantial and fickle.

01:12:43.680 --> 01:12:48.400
Negativity, both internal and external, can&nbsp;
have a meaningful impact on the willingness&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:48.400 --> 01:12:53.600
of people to buy into a project, and if buyers&nbsp;
are tepid then you won't get a runaway sale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:53.600 --> 01:12:58.000
and if you don't get a runaway sale then&nbsp;
that's going to turn off buyers even more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:12:58.000 --> 01:13:02.240
which means the secondary market for&nbsp;
tokens will likely fail to materialize.

01:13:02.800 --> 01:13:05.760
This is what makes enthusiasts&nbsp;
so deeply unreliable.

01:13:05.760 --> 01:13:09.280
They have meaningful financial&nbsp;
stake in an intangible, volatile&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:09.280 --> 01:13:12.160
thing that exists entirely&nbsp;
as a collective ideoform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:12.160 --> 01:13:17.600
a story about a potential future outcome, whose&nbsp;
value is based entirely on public perception.

01:13:17.600 --> 01:13:21.840
You can’t trust what they have to say because&nbsp;
they’re currently holding a hot potato,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:21.840 --> 01:13:24.800
and as much as they insist that they just really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:24.800 --> 01:13:28.080
really enjoy the feeling of a&nbsp;
burning hot potato in their hands,

01:13:28.800 --> 01:13:29.680
do they?

01:13:29.680 --> 01:13:31.760
Or are they just hoping that you’ll catch it?

01:13:31.760 --> 01:13:34.640
This creates an environment of&nbsp;
toxic positivity where doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:34.640 --> 01:13:36.960
is aggressively policed by both project leaders,

01:13:36.960 --> 01:13:41.440
who have an obvious financial interest in hype&nbsp;
since their big payday is the minting rush,

01:13:41.440 --> 01:13:46.240
and community members themselves, who have&nbsp;
a speculative financial interest in hype.

01:13:46.240 --> 01:13:50.880
While all of that is logical in the pure sense&nbsp;
that there's an effect that can be explained by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:50.880 --> 01:13:55.520
an incentive, the output is effectively&nbsp;
a self-organizing high-control group.

01:13:55.520 --> 01:13:58.240
Doubters are ostracized so aggressively&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:13:58.240 --> 01:14:02.400
that it chills all conversation&nbsp;
about a project's actual viability.

01:14:02.400 --> 01:14:06.880
All concerns are just FUD:&nbsp;
fear, uncertainty, and doubt.

01:14:06.880 --> 01:14:10.560
Questions that would be utterly&nbsp;
banal in any other investment forum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:10.560 --> 01:14:13.200
what has the team done, what assets do they have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:13.200 --> 01:14:18.320
why should anyone believe they can deliver&nbsp;
on their promises, are treated as hostile.

01:14:18.320 --> 01:14:21.680
And the frank reality is&nbsp;
because there aren't answers.

01:14:21.680 --> 01:14:25.280
Party Ape Billionaire Club is&nbsp;
just as vaporous, and yet they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:25.280 --> 01:14:31.440
superficially succeeded, so there's&nbsp;
incentive to enforce the collective illusion.

01:14:31.440 --> 01:14:35.280
This is multiplied by an internal&nbsp;
form of performative etiquette.

01:14:35.280 --> 01:14:38.720
Participants ritualistically wish each&nbsp;
other good morning and good night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:38.720 --> 01:14:41.200
boiled down to the shorthand GM and GN.

01:14:41.200 --> 01:14:46.240
That seems like a small thing, there’s nothing&nbsp;
inherently suspicious about good morning or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:46.240 --> 01:14:52.480
good night, but in observed practice it’s a very&nbsp;
distinct ritual, not merely a shibboleth, but a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:14:52.480 --> 01:14:58.800
repetitive action that signals in-group membership&nbsp;
and affirms loyalty on an ongoing basis.

01:14:58.800 --> 01:15:02.720
If you have Diamond Hands it means&nbsp;
you’re willing to Hold a token until&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:02.720 --> 01:15:08.000
some promised future where the value goes To&nbsp;
the Moon, you’re not a Paper Hands loser who&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:08.000 --> 01:15:12.640
is easily spooked by instability, volatility,&nbsp;
or the fact that there’s no reason to believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:12.640 --> 01:15:16.640
that anyone is ever going to want to&nbsp;
buy a Crypto Astronut in the future.

01:15:16.640 --> 01:15:22.720
The shorthand WAGMI, We’re All Going To Make It,&nbsp;
is aphoristically bandied about even in openly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:22.720 --> 01:15:28.960
zero-sum competitions where, by definition,&nbsp;
most participants explicitly won’t make it.

01:15:28.960 --> 01:15:32.480
But you can’t point that out,&nbsp;
because that would be FUD.

01:15:32.480 --> 01:15:36.400
And if you’re spreading FUD then&nbsp;
you’re NGMI, Not Going to Make It.

01:15:36.400 --> 01:15:39.840
And making it, getting rich, is all that matters.

01:15:39.840 --> 01:15:42.720
HFSP, have fun staying poor.

01:15:43.360 --> 01:15:49.440
These are synthesized into a No True Scotsman&nbsp;
paradigm. The “we” in We’re All Going To Make It&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:15:49.440 --> 01:15:55.600
does not refer to we all, it refers to the select,&nbsp;
the chosen, the Diamond Hands and the hodlers.

01:15:55.600 --> 01:16:00.560
Those who make it are clearly the We, and&nbsp;
if you didn’t make it, then you weren’t.

01:16:00.560 --> 01:16:04.480
People who get angry about being&nbsp;
scammed by a rug-pull or by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:04.480 --> 01:16:09.840
malware or by social engineering are berated&nbsp;
and belittled for not following the crowd.

01:16:09.840 --> 01:16:14.240
This incubates a community trained to&nbsp;
ignore warning signs and dismiss criticism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:14.240 --> 01:16:16.640
a community with internal&nbsp;
language and customs that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:16.640 --> 01:16:20.000
are explicitly incompatible&nbsp;
with outside communications.

01:16:20.000 --> 01:16:23.920
Skepticism is FUD from non-believers&nbsp;
who are trying to undermine the value&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:23.920 --> 01:16:28.080
of your assets and manipulate a crash&nbsp;
or trick you into being a paper hands.

01:16:28.080 --> 01:16:31.760
It all maps onto narratives of sin and deception,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:31.760 --> 01:16:36.000
a chosen-few who are privileged with&nbsp;
advance knowledge about the promised land,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:36.000 --> 01:16:40.640
which they can achieve by holding strong&nbsp;
to the rituals and expelling all doubt.

01:16:40.640 --> 01:16:44.560
The end product is a&nbsp;
self-organizing high-control group.

01:16:44.560 --> 01:16:49.040
And the results of that are obvious: there&nbsp;
are still people convinced that somehow&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:49.040 --> 01:16:52.240
someone is going to pick up&nbsp;
the ashes of Evolved Apes&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:16:52.240 --> 01:16:57.040
and manifest the rest of the project, a&nbsp;
belief based on no observable evidence.

01:16:57.760 --> 01:17:01.520
“You like to ban, you like to ban people,&nbsp;
right, you like to ban people from your&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:01.520 --> 01:17:05.520
Twitch stream, huh? All you’re doing&nbsp;
is shitting on Decentraland, bro.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:06.800 --> 01:17:10.160
I’m sorry you don’t like to make&nbsp;
money here in the network, but, uh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:11.040 --> 01:17:14.560
I wanted to talk on your Twitch stream, man&nbsp;
I probably could have brought a lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:14.560 --> 01:17:18.240
on there, and all you want to do is talk shit&nbsp;
and ban people, man you banned me instantly.”

01:17:18.960 --> 01:17:23.600
So the question, then, is what&nbsp;
do the tokens actually do?

01:17:24.240 --> 01:17:29.040
As mentioned before the token itself is just&nbsp;
a box that a bit of data can be put into.

01:17:29.040 --> 01:17:31.040
Now, this box is extremely small,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:31.040 --> 01:17:35.600
to the point that even an average cell phone&nbsp;
photograph is several times too large to fit.

01:17:35.600 --> 01:17:37.280
Due to the nature of the chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:37.280 --> 01:17:41.920
updating software that’s put onto the&nbsp;
chain is both difficult and expensive.

01:17:41.920 --> 01:17:45.520
Complicated programs need to be&nbsp;
broken up into multiple tokens each&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:45.520 --> 01:17:48.800
containing a smart contract that&nbsp;
defines a portion of the whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:48.800 --> 01:17:52.320
and all of these contracts need to&nbsp;
interlink and reference each other,

01:17:52.320 --> 01:17:58.560
but with an eye towards the fact that&nbsp;
each transaction that either computes or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:17:58.560 --> 01:18:05.280
alters information requires paying processing&nbsp;
fees, only reading information is free.

01:18:05.280 --> 01:18:10.080
The process of fixing a bug in a smart contract&nbsp;
basically amounts to minting a new copy of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:10.080 --> 01:18:15.120
contract and then jumping through some hoops&nbsp;
to re-name the old and new contract so that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:15.120 --> 01:18:19.520
the new copy has the name that any other&nbsp;
interacting contracts are looking for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:19.520 --> 01:18:23.280
paying fees for just about&nbsp;
every step of that process.

01:18:23.280 --> 01:18:27.680
This creates a very funny Catch 22 where&nbsp;
on one hand is the insistence that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:27.680 --> 01:18:32.880
NFTs that end users buy are potentially&nbsp;
extremely powerful, able to be miniature&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:32.880 --> 01:18:38.480
self-governing applets that exist in a&nbsp;
web of like applets, and on the other hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:38.480 --> 01:18:43.120
is a large stack of incentives to&nbsp;
put as little into them as possible.

01:18:43.120 --> 01:18:47.840
For an example of the pitfalls, Wolf Game&nbsp;
was a somewhat popular NFT based gambling&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:47.840 --> 01:18:52.640
game that bragged about being hosted&nbsp;
entirely on-chain, at great expense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:18:52.640 --> 01:18:57.600
Everything from the pixel art wolves and sheep&nbsp;
to the game’s code was stored on Ethereum.

01:18:57.600 --> 01:19:03.200
The idea behind the game is that users would mint&nbsp;
a character token, which had a 90% chance of being&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:03.200 --> 01:19:08.800
a sheep, and a 10% chance of being a wolf, with&nbsp;
a finite supply of wolves and sheep available.

01:19:08.800 --> 01:19:14.320
The important thing here is that the character&nbsp;
tokens weren’t just a bit of art and a serial&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:14.320 --> 01:19:19.680
number, but tiny programs that allowed&nbsp;
users to perform interactions like staking.

01:19:19.680 --> 01:19:24.400
The problem is that their code was full of&nbsp;
multiple bugs, and since some of those bugs&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:24.400 --> 01:19:32.480
were contained in the character tokens, they&nbsp;
were replicated across all 13,809 minted tokens.

01:19:33.120 --> 01:19:38.560
A glitchy token can’t be&nbsp;
patched, it must be replaced.

01:19:38.560 --> 01:19:43.280
So the Wolf Game devs were forced to&nbsp;
not only re-deploy all their contracts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:43.280 --> 01:19:50.000
but attempt to re-mint and distribute&nbsp;
identical copies of every token generated.

01:19:50.000 --> 01:19:51.200
It didn’t go well.

01:19:51.760 --> 01:19:55.840
Given the risks inherent in putting&nbsp;
functionality inside the token itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:55.840 --> 01:19:59.280
and the high cost of interaction,&nbsp;
the most common application&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:19:59.280 --> 01:20:04.320
is to use external systems to simply&nbsp;
check for possession of a token.

01:20:04.320 --> 01:20:09.840
This is where we introduce another player into&nbsp;
the ecosystem: wallet managers like MetaMask.

01:20:09.840 --> 01:20:14.640
In a few short years the system has already&nbsp;
grown so bloated and difficult to interact with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:14.640 --> 01:20:17.200
that it’s become necessary to develop middleware&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:17.200 --> 01:20:20.560
applications that simplify the&nbsp;
process of generating wallets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:20.560 --> 01:20:25.440
switching wallets, and mediating handshakes&nbsp;
between the wallet and external systems.

01:20:25.440 --> 01:20:29.760
From one point of view these wallet managers&nbsp;
are the golden key that makes it all work.

01:20:29.760 --> 01:20:35.120
They solve the single-sign on problem allowing&nbsp;
your web browser to simply know that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:35.120 --> 01:20:39.920
you who is using it, automatically negotiating&nbsp;
permissions based on relevant tokens.

01:20:39.920 --> 01:20:44.720
It’s your log in, your credit card, your Steam&nbsp;
profile, and your bank account all rolled into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:44.720 --> 01:20:51.520
a single point of contact making interaction with&nbsp;
Web3, the internet of the future, frictionless.

01:20:51.520 --> 01:20:56.240
From another point of view it’s a massive point&nbsp;
of failure that contains so much information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:20:56.240 --> 01:21:00.800
so many permissions, so much of worth, that it&nbsp;
becomes an extremely obvious point of attack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:00.800 --> 01:21:04.800
manufactured by idiots who claim to&nbsp;
be building a security product in 2021&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:04.800 --> 01:21:09.840
that doesn’t obfuscate key phrases within&nbsp;
the UI or use two factor authentication.

01:21:09.840 --> 01:21:13.440
Remember, again, that these people want&nbsp;
to put medical records, drivers licenses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:13.440 --> 01:21:16.480
professional accreditations, and&nbsp;
real estate deeds into their system.

01:21:16.480 --> 01:21:17.840
They should not be trusted.

01:21:17.840 --> 01:21:20.400
The entire market is absolutely lousy with scams,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:20.400 --> 01:21:23.120
and has been since Bitcoin&nbsp;
first gained any traction.

01:21:23.120 --> 01:21:28.000
Every single scam structure imaginable has been&nbsp;
dusted off and redeployed into this explicitly&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:28.000 --> 01:21:31.520
unregulated market where victims&nbsp;
are largely without recourse.

01:21:31.520 --> 01:21:35.360
These range from institutional scams,&nbsp;
like Ponzi schemes, pump and dumps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:35.360 --> 01:21:39.680
and insider trading, to middle-weight&nbsp;
scams like gold brick and wash trading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:39.680 --> 01:21:43.520
to grittier scams like phishing&nbsp;
and sending fake links.

01:21:43.520 --> 01:21:47.520
Pump and dumps, in particular, are conducted&nbsp;
in broad daylight, since it’s not illegal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:47.520 --> 01:21:51.120
it’s just against the terms of service&nbsp;
of the exchanges that you use to do it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:51.120 --> 01:21:53.920
so the worst case scenario&nbsp;
is you burn your account.

01:21:53.920 --> 01:21:57.120
They’ll straight up walk you through&nbsp;
the process of doing a pump and dump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:21:57.120 --> 01:21:59.440
no codewords, diagrams and everything.

01:21:59.440 --> 01:22:03.120
They’re notable as they’re actually&nbsp;
a two-headed scam because, you see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:03.120 --> 01:22:07.040
you might get recruited onto&nbsp;
the pump half of the scheme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:07.040 --> 01:22:12.080
or you might think you’re being recruited&nbsp;
to pump, but you’re actually the dump.

01:22:12.080 --> 01:22:16.720
As already mentioned, blockchain is resilient to&nbsp;
direct man-in-the-middle attacks, where someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:16.720 --> 01:22:21.920
tries to inject bad data straight into the&nbsp;
chain, but man in the middle attacks are rare.

01:22:21.920 --> 01:22:26.240
In an environment like cryptocurrency,&nbsp;
with few, if any, repercussions for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:26.240 --> 01:22:32.080
misbehaviour that stays within the cryptosphere,&nbsp;
man-in-the-middle attacks are wholly unnecessary.

01:22:32.080 --> 01:22:36.960
Why try to brute force your bad data onto&nbsp;
the chain when you can trick someone into&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:36.960 --> 01:22:40.640
giving you access to their wallet,&nbsp;
then transfer all their stuff out?

01:22:40.640 --> 01:22:44.000
Every smart contract becomes a&nbsp;
self-rewarding bug bounty where&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:44.000 --> 01:22:48.480
the payout is whatever apes and coins&nbsp;
you can grab before anyone notices.

01:22:48.480 --> 01:22:50.880
And that’s not even touching&nbsp;
on the subject of malware!

01:22:50.880 --> 01:22:53.280
Smart contracts are just code, they’re software,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:22:53.280 --> 01:22:58.880
there’s no reason they can’t be viruses or worms,&nbsp;
the primary limitation is processing power.

01:22:58.880 --> 01:23:02.560
But, also, it’s a virus that someone can drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:02.560 --> 01:23:07.440
directly into your bankless bank account&nbsp;
and just wait for you to activate it.

01:23:07.440 --> 01:23:12.000
And, yeah, that’s right, there’s no&nbsp;
offer/confirmation step in sending&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:12.000 --> 01:23:16.720
tokens back and forth, someone who knows your&nbsp;
wallet can just drop stuff right into it,

01:23:16.720 --> 01:23:20.000
so, like, pin that somewhere in your brain.

01:23:20.000 --> 01:23:24.480
The best part about this is that the whole&nbsp;
ecosystem operates on a strict assumption that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:24.480 --> 01:23:28.480
possession is ownership and access is permission,

01:23:28.480 --> 01:23:30.960
which is absolutely buckwild coming from software&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:30.960 --> 01:23:34.000
developers who claim to be very&nbsp;
concerned with systems security.

01:23:34.000 --> 01:23:38.240
If someone tricks you into sending&nbsp;
them your Bored Ape it’s now theirs.

01:23:38.240 --> 01:23:41.680
The only mechanism in the machine&nbsp;
for parsing legitimate transactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:41.680 --> 01:23:44.800
from illegitimate transactions&nbsp;
is the consensus mechanism,

01:23:44.800 --> 01:23:50.080
which is only concerned with whether or not the&nbsp;
transactions followed the rules of the software.

01:23:50.080 --> 01:23:55.280
The only illegitimacy it recognizes&nbsp;
are people trying to insert fake data.

01:23:55.280 --> 01:23:57.840
When you are tricked into doing something&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:23:57.840 --> 01:24:03.360
all of the mechanics of what follows are,&nbsp;
by the rules of the system, legitimate.

01:24:03.360 --> 01:24:04.800
It’s a legal transfer.

01:24:04.800 --> 01:24:08.320
More relevant, however, is the&nbsp;
sheer density of these scams.

01:24:08.320 --> 01:24:13.040
The one market that cryptocurrency has&nbsp;
successfully disrupted is the market of fraud.

01:24:13.040 --> 01:24:17.200
Think of this this way: a big population of&nbsp;
people have willingly self-identified that&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:17.200 --> 01:24:22.000
they have substantial disposable income,&nbsp;
poor judgment, low social literacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:22.000 --> 01:24:26.240
a high tolerance for nonsensical&nbsp;
risk, and are highly persuadable.

01:24:26.240 --> 01:24:30.160
People who fall victim to these scams&nbsp;
have basically no options other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:30.160 --> 01:24:34.320
taking to social media and attempting to&nbsp;
whip up enough of a frenzy that they can&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:34.320 --> 01:24:40.000
convince marketplaces like OpenSea to act as&nbsp;
de facto censors by delisting stolen tokens.

01:24:40.000 --> 01:24:44.720
There’s no authority you can report them to&nbsp;
that has the power to return your tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:44.720 --> 01:24:51.280
the best you can hope for is denying the scammers&nbsp;
profit on their incredibly low-cost operation.

01:24:51.280 --> 01:24:54.240
If your business is tricking&nbsp;
people out of their money&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:24:54.240 --> 01:24:57.920
you would be a fool if you&nbsp;
didn’t take the opportunity.

01:24:57.920 --> 01:25:02.560
Not only have participants advertised their&nbsp;
susceptibility to incoherent promises of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:02.560 --> 01:25:07.680
future returns, the immutable structure of the&nbsp;
chain, the persistence of data, and the ease&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:07.680 --> 01:25:14.880
with which that data can be collated, means that&nbsp;
for scammers it’s extremely easy to find marks.

01:25:14.880 --> 01:25:20.960
Every Discord for a rugpull NFT project is&nbsp;
a roster of potential victims. The ledger&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:20.960 --> 01:25:25.600
of BAYC holders is a shopping list of&nbsp;
targets. The twitter account of anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:25.600 --> 01:25:32.160
complaining about what they lost on Evolved Apes&nbsp;
is the low hanging fruit of a very ripe orchard.

01:25:32.160 --> 01:25:40.120
And that’s a pretty good segue&nbsp;
into talking about privacy issues!

01:25:40.120 --> 01:25:41.120
[Drumming]

01:25:41.120 --> 01:25:44.480
A low trust environment is&nbsp;
also a low privacy environment.

01:25:44.480 --> 01:25:47.520
Anything you do on a blockchain is, by design,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:25:47.520 --> 01:25:50.800
accessible to everyone who&nbsp;
knows how to navigate the data.

01:25:50.800 --> 01:25:55.280
Now, you might be thinking, isn’t that&nbsp;
the opposite of how it’s supposed to work?

01:25:55.840 --> 01:25:57.680
I thought crypto was all anonymous?

01:25:58.880 --> 01:26:06.320
So, yes, crypto is anonymous by default, there’s&nbsp;
no inherent requirement for proof of identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:06.320 --> 01:26:10.480
and in fact numerous applications&nbsp;
that would benefit from a stricter&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:10.480 --> 01:26:14.960
1-to-1 correlation between accounts&nbsp;
and people struggle with this.

01:26:14.960 --> 01:26:17.920
On forums and social media&nbsp;
it’s called sockpuppeting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:17.920 --> 01:26:21.760
in crypto it’s called a Sibyl attack,&nbsp;
where users are able to generate&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:21.760 --> 01:26:25.600
numerous alternate identities by&nbsp;
creating additional accounts or wallets.

01:26:25.600 --> 01:26:30.560
The epidemic of wash trading on OpenSea&nbsp;
relies on the ability to pretend you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:30.560 --> 01:26:36.160
several different people as you buy apes from&nbsp;
yourself for hundreds of thousands of dollars.

01:26:36.160 --> 01:26:38.960
Rather than anonymous this is pseudonymous.

01:26:38.960 --> 01:26:43.280
Everyone can see what wallets and&nbsp;
contracts your wallet interacts with&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:43.280 --> 01:26:46.640
based off the long hash addresses like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:46.640 --> 01:26:52.880
but that hash is only implicitly connected to&nbsp;
your identity based on circumstantial connections.

01:26:52.880 --> 01:26:56.320
Of course a big circumstantial&nbsp;
connection would be something like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:26:56.320 --> 01:27:00.800
registering with a crypto-based social auction&nbsp;
platform that intrinsically connects the two.

01:27:00.800 --> 01:27:04.880
This makes it pretty easy to see that&nbsp;
Laina Morris, Overly Attached Girlfriend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:04.880 --> 01:27:09.840
paid $153 on March 31st to mint&nbsp;
the Overly Attached Girlfriend NFT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:09.840 --> 01:27:15.200
followed by a $205 reserve on April&nbsp;
2nd to list the NFT on Foundation.

01:27:15.200 --> 01:27:20.640
Ethereum whale Farzin Fardin Fard then bought&nbsp;
the token on April 3rd for 200 Ethereum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:20.640 --> 01:27:25.280
30 of which went to Foundation, with&nbsp;
Laina getting the remaining 170,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:25.280 --> 01:27:30.880
which she transferred into USD using the&nbsp;
exchange service Kraken in a block of 105&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:30.880 --> 01:27:40.480
on April 4th and a block of 65 on April 22nd&nbsp;
for a combined total payout of $374,726.

01:27:41.360 --> 01:27:46.000
After her sale Zoe Roth transferred&nbsp;
the Ethereum to dollars via Coinbase&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:46.000 --> 01:27:49.360
the next day and hasn’t touched the wallet since.

01:27:49.360 --> 01:27:55.680
Kyle craven minted several… other… tokens, but&nbsp;
for the most part just split the Ethereum from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:27:55.680 --> 01:28:00.240
the initial sale into two separate holding&nbsp;
wallets where it’s sat untouched since.

01:28:00.880 --> 01:28:03.760
You don’t need to be some super&nbsp;
hacker to figure this stuff out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:03.760 --> 01:28:08.720
it’s all publicly available, that’s&nbsp;
the entire point of the system.

01:28:08.720 --> 01:28:11.440
And in another feature-not-bug arrangement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:11.440 --> 01:28:16.160
remember nothing can be deleted from the&nbsp;
blockchain without tremendous effort.

01:28:16.160 --> 01:28:21.840
Now, that’s fine if the blockchain only contains&nbsp;
a contextually relevant log of transactions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:21.840 --> 01:28:26.960
there are absolutely contexts where that level&nbsp;
of transparency is desirable, but that falls&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:26.960 --> 01:28:31.840
apart when you start talking about using the&nbsp;
blockchain itself as the storage medium for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:32.400 --> 01:28:34.640
like, an entire social network.

01:28:34.640 --> 01:28:39.920
Blockchain-based social network Scuttlebutt&nbsp;
seems tacitly aware that this is a bad idea,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:39.920 --> 01:28:44.960
like somewhere inside their human brains they&nbsp;
recognize that it might be a mistake to make it&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:44.960 --> 01:28:49.280
impossible to remove things from the system&nbsp;
when they warn users that anyone willing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:28:49.280 --> 01:28:56.240
dig can surface any old usernames, photos, and&nbsp;
bios, but that doesn’t actually give them pause.

01:28:56.240 --> 01:29:01.680
So, if someone posts, say, child abuse&nbsp;
imagery, revenge porn, your home address,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:01.680 --> 01:29:05.760
intimate details of your private life, there’s&nbsp;
just nothing you can really do about that.

01:29:05.760 --> 01:29:10.160
If you mistakenly over-share, post some&nbsp;
information that maybe you shouldn’t have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:10.160 --> 01:29:12.000
it’s already too late.

01:29:12.000 --> 01:29:15.040
You can try to hide it, but you can’t delete it.

01:29:15.040 --> 01:29:20.000
Remember that if someone knows your wallet&nbsp;
address they can just put tokens directly into it?

01:29:20.000 --> 01:29:24.560
As alluded there’s a whole scam where you&nbsp;
drop someone an NFT that lifts the art from&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:24.560 --> 01:29:30.080
who cares, somewhere, but the smart contract is&nbsp;
malicious code that drains their wallet if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:30.080 --> 01:29:33.360
ever interact with it to&nbsp;
move it, sell it, stake it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:33.360 --> 01:29:37.440
burn it, it just becomes a landmine&nbsp;
sitting in their wallet forever.

01:29:37.440 --> 01:29:41.600
“Yo, this is fake, this is fake, this is fake,&nbsp;
this is fake, they popped up in my wallet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:41.600 --> 01:29:47.200
I clicked on it to delete it, immediately they&nbsp;
stole 19 grand. Happily I just started this&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:47.200 --> 01:29:51.520
wallet, but already they stole 19 thousand&nbsp;
out of it. Need fuckin’ help immediately.”

01:29:51.520 --> 01:29:54.480
Even on the non-malware side of&nbsp;
things people have already been&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:54.480 --> 01:29:58.800
using this to dump promotional tokens into&nbsp;
the wallets of celebrities and influencers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:29:58.800 --> 01:30:03.120
but, you know dick pics are an “any&nbsp;
second now” kinda thing, right?

01:30:03.120 --> 01:30:07.920
“Oh, look, I minted a photo of your front door&nbsp;
and dropped it directly into your wallet.”

01:30:07.920 --> 01:30:09.520
And you can’t just, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:09.520 --> 01:30:13.760
delete it, you need to actively send it&nbsp;
somewhere, and pay gas fees to do so.

01:30:13.760 --> 01:30:16.160
Revolutionary new vectors of harassment.

01:30:16.160 --> 01:30:18.960
The end product here is a massive power imbalance&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:18.960 --> 01:30:22.560
that’s baked into the fabric of how&nbsp;
you engage with this new world order.

01:30:22.560 --> 01:30:27.440
Users who engage with the system authentically&nbsp;
as themselves expose vast swathes of information&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:27.440 --> 01:30:31.520
about themselves and their activities,&nbsp;
while users who engage disingenuously&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:31.520 --> 01:30:35.120
are empowered in their ability to&nbsp;
deceive, defraud, and disappear.

01:30:35.120 --> 01:30:39.360
A lot of this rhetoric stems from a pretty deep&nbsp;
failure to understand what a central authority&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:39.360 --> 01:30:44.400
really is, or that you can decentralize&nbsp;
data storage while centralizing data.

01:30:44.400 --> 01:30:49.040
Ethereum is ultimately a central platform,&nbsp;
and the fact that a few dozen people need to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:49.040 --> 01:30:54.160
sign off on every major change before it can be&nbsp;
implemented is largely meaningless and symbolic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:30:54.160 --> 01:30:58.880
with the validation network ultimately sitting&nbsp;
somewhere between consortium and cartel.

01:30:58.880 --> 01:31:03.040
Every large platform has multiple&nbsp;
internal and external stakeholders&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:03.040 --> 01:31:05.920
that form a consensus about&nbsp;
the direction of the platform.

01:31:05.920 --> 01:31:08.640
Windows is not a single-minded monolith.

01:31:08.640 --> 01:31:10.880
Apple issues voting shares.

01:31:10.880 --> 01:31:13.360
Google is basically a hydra.

01:31:13.360 --> 01:31:17.840
While the network of Ethereum miners and&nbsp;
validators are not a formal corporation

01:31:18.480 --> 01:31:19.120
Yet

01:31:19.120 --> 01:31:23.120
There’s no mechanism in existence that&nbsp;
compels them to act in the interest of users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:23.120 --> 01:31:28.400
particularly poor and disempowered users,&nbsp;
where those interests conflict with their own.

01:31:28.400 --> 01:31:32.960
The movement of Ethereum from proof of&nbsp;
work to proof of stake has been vapourware&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:32.960 --> 01:31:37.520
in no small part because the&nbsp;
validators simply choose not to.

01:31:37.520 --> 01:31:42.160
Because proof of work, volatility, and high&nbsp;
gas fees benefit them in the here and now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:42.160 --> 01:31:46.960
while proof of stake and low gas fees only&nbsp;
benefit them in a hypothetical future.

01:31:46.960 --> 01:31:50.560
Because these extremely obvious&nbsp;
pitfalls are what you get when&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:50.560 --> 01:31:54.960
you let guys like Vitalik Buterin&nbsp;
and Elon Musk design the future.

01:31:54.960 --> 01:31:59.200
The sole protection for users of Ethereum&nbsp;
is that the system is so cumbersome&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:31:59.200 --> 01:32:02.480
that all but the most egregious&nbsp;
breeches aren’t worth addressing.

01:32:02.480 --> 01:32:07.840
Of course that, in truth, means that only the&nbsp;
wealthy have access to justice within the system.

01:32:07.840 --> 01:32:11.600
If you take all these different things,&nbsp;
all these parts of your identity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:11.600 --> 01:32:15.680
all your economic activity, all the video&nbsp;
games you play, all the groups you join,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:15.680 --> 01:32:19.600
and stick them into one system,&nbsp;
that’s a central system.

01:32:19.600 --> 01:32:23.520
It doesn't matter how many different&nbsp;
servers that system spans or how many&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:23.520 --> 01:32:30.160
validators need to agree before changes can be&nbsp;
made, you’ve pooled all that data in one place.

01:32:30.160 --> 01:32:36.480
The proposed web3, crypto-driven future&nbsp;
of the internet is a privacy disaster.

01:32:36.480 --> 01:32:39.440
This is why I find MetaMask&nbsp;
horrifying as a product.

01:32:39.440 --> 01:32:41.360
It’s a bucket that’s asking you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:32:41.360 --> 01:32:44.480
pour unfathomable amounts of&nbsp;
data and permissions into it.

01:32:44.480 --> 01:32:48.160
It is such a tremendous and&nbsp;
monumental point of failure.

01:32:48.160 --> 01:32:53.840
And this is where NFTs really&nbsp;
bloom into their final form.

01:32:56.200 --> 01:32:57.200
[Drumming]

01:32:57.200 --> 01:33:03.680
The primary use case of tokens beyond speculation,&nbsp;
ultimately, is to function as access passes.

01:33:03.680 --> 01:33:08.480
From a web engineering perspective imagine tokens&nbsp;
that are the mother of all cookies and consider a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:08.480 --> 01:33:14.240
tracking token that users don’t just willingly&nbsp;
associate themselves with but enthusiastically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:14.240 --> 01:33:20.480
drag with them from device to device ensuring a&nbsp;
continuity of tracking across all web activity.

01:33:20.480 --> 01:33:22.000
The future version of the web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:22.000 --> 01:33:27.280
built on the back of cryptocurrency and&nbsp;
mediated by financialized tokens, is a dystopia.

01:33:27.280 --> 01:33:30.080
It’s a technology that’s built&nbsp;
to turn everything into money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:30.080 --> 01:33:33.440
to treat every corner of our&nbsp;
social existence as a marketplace,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:33.440 --> 01:33:38.720
to attach an abstract, representative token to&nbsp;
everything from video games to labour unions.

01:33:38.720 --> 01:33:41.440
Now, proponents of Web3 will&nbsp;
disagree with this assessment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:41.440 --> 01:33:46.880
particularly the claim that cryptocurrency is&nbsp;
endemic to Web3 and the two are intractable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:46.880 --> 01:33:50.240
but that’s the practical reality of the situation.

01:33:50.240 --> 01:33:54.000
Every substantial project branding&nbsp;
itself under the banner of Web3 is&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:54.000 --> 01:33:57.840
strapped to the side of a blockchain,&nbsp;
be it issuing governance tokens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:33:57.840 --> 01:34:02.400
or relying on the chain’s smart contract layer,&nbsp;
or requiring possession of a cryptocurrency in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:02.400 --> 01:34:06.000
order to pay the processing fees that&nbsp;
are mandatory in order to participate.

01:34:06.000 --> 01:34:10.240
They are at this point philosophically&nbsp;
and technologically entwined.

01:34:10.240 --> 01:34:14.960
Less accessible, less free, less interesting,&nbsp;
and substantially more expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:14.960 --> 01:34:20.880
Web3 is the vanguard of a million paywalls and&nbsp;
oppressive "code enforced" DRM schemes sold to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:20.880 --> 01:34:25.520
idealists as a decentralized system where they,&nbsp;
and not wealthy stakeholders, have the power.

01:34:25.520 --> 01:34:29.840
I see tremendous blind spots in a community&nbsp;
that has spent so long focusing on the hype of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:29.840 --> 01:34:35.040
an untenable fantasy Metaverse where they're the&nbsp;
ones cowing corporations with immutable ownership&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:35.040 --> 01:34:40.240
guaranteeing their ability to resell video game&nbsp;
horse armour that they've failed to consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:40.240 --> 01:34:44.960
that the "enforcement of ownership"&nbsp;
can and will be used against them&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:44.960 --> 01:34:48.720
if and when corporations decide to&nbsp;
leverage their power in the space.

01:34:48.720 --> 01:34:52.640
The fantasy amongst evangelists is that&nbsp;
a tokenized economy where digital goods&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:52.640 --> 01:34:56.320
are mediated by NFTs would give&nbsp;
them more power, that goods like&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:34:56.320 --> 01:35:00.560
digital games would have true ownership,&nbsp;
encapsulated in the ability to be resold,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:00.560 --> 01:35:04.400
but there is no reason to believe that&nbsp;
this is how things would shake out.

01:35:04.400 --> 01:35:08.640
The far, far more probable result&nbsp;
is that the tokens are used to lock&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:08.640 --> 01:35:10.800
products down even tighter.

01:35:10.800 --> 01:35:13.680
The Squid Game token scam is illustrative here.

01:35:13.680 --> 01:35:19.600
In early November, 2021, a new meme token popped&nbsp;
up, styled after the hit Netflix show Squid Game.

01:35:19.600 --> 01:35:21.600
Over the course of about a week the price of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:21.600 --> 01:35:26.000
token was pumped from a few&nbsp;
cents to just under $3000 US.

01:35:26.000 --> 01:35:30.960
Estimates are that the Squid developers&nbsp;
took in about $3.38 million dollars&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:30.960 --> 01:35:35.360
before cashing out their holdings, deleting&nbsp;
their socials, and vanishing into thin air.

01:35:35.360 --> 01:35:39.760
A classic rug pull, but the&nbsp;
truly transcendental detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:39.760 --> 01:35:46.080
is that it left buyers with not merely a useless&nbsp;
token, but a token that couldn’t be sold at all.

01:35:46.080 --> 01:35:50.720
Built into SQUID was a requirement that&nbsp;
in order to transfer or sell the tokens&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:50.720 --> 01:35:56.320
a corresponding number of MARBLES tokens&nbsp;
needed to be spent at the same time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:35:56.320 --> 01:35:59.120
The hitch was that MARBLES were never available.

01:35:59.120 --> 01:36:05.440
No rules were broken in this scam, and indeed the&nbsp;
SQUID tokens do exactly what they say they do.

01:36:05.440 --> 01:36:08.240
If you have the appropriate&nbsp;
amount of MARBLES tokens&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:08.240 --> 01:36:11.840
you can absolutely transfer or sell your SQUID.

01:36:12.640 --> 01:36:14.240
Just, good luck getting any.

01:36:14.240 --> 01:36:20.080
A game developer can in any of myriad ways&nbsp;
deliver on a “truly owned” digital token&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:20.080 --> 01:36:24.880
that is rendered untradeable in practice,&nbsp;
and all by playing by the rules.

01:36:25.440 --> 01:36:29.360
Rules must always be evaluated&nbsp;
for their power to oppress.

01:36:29.360 --> 01:36:34.320
This is a blind spot to crypto enthusiasts because&nbsp;
they just assume that they’re the early adopters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:34.320 --> 01:36:37.920
they’re the ones who will have power, they’re&nbsp;
the ones who will get to set the rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:37.920 --> 01:36:40.320
and they’re the ones who will do the oppressing.

01:36:40.320 --> 01:36:45.520
Consider that any token that can be used to&nbsp;
grant access can also be used to revoke it.

01:36:45.520 --> 01:36:50.320
Like, let’s say that I create a hangout spot&nbsp;
in one of the Metaverse contender platforms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:50.320 --> 01:36:54.240
Decentraland, and we call&nbsp;
it the Ahegao Alpaca Oasis.

01:36:54.240 --> 01:36:58.320
The Oasis has a back room that only&nbsp;
allows registered players to enter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:36:58.320 --> 01:37:03.120
meaning you need to have your metamask&nbsp;
linked to Decentraland in order to get in.

01:37:03.120 --> 01:37:08.800
This type of gate is typically used to create&nbsp;
VIP areas, places where only people who hold a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:08.800 --> 01:37:14.800
specific token or class of token can enter.&nbsp;
Only people with official Ahegao Alpacas&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:14.800 --> 01:37:21.360
can enter. But, as is well known, within the&nbsp;
lore of the Metaverse the Ahegao Alpacas have&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:21.360 --> 01:37:26.800
long been at war with the Bored Ape Yacht Club,&nbsp;
so rather than checking for an Alpaca token,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:26.800 --> 01:37:30.080
I check for Ape tokens, and then forbid entrance.

01:37:30.080 --> 01:37:35.280
Or maybe I just make my game artificially&nbsp;
more difficult for them, or inflate my prices.

01:37:35.280 --> 01:37:38.480
Now imagine that instead of running&nbsp;
a hangout spot in a video game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:38.480 --> 01:37:43.840
that I’m a Decentralized Finance organization&nbsp;
giving out mortgages in cryptocurrency and I scour&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:43.840 --> 01:37:49.040
your transaction history for donations to the&nbsp;
NAACP as part of my “risk assessment protocol”.

01:37:49.040 --> 01:37:52.720
Imagine that Nestle is able to track&nbsp;
unionization efforts in real time&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:37:52.720 --> 01:37:56.640
because the union is issuing governance&nbsp;
tokens on a publicly auditable blockchain.

01:37:56.640 --> 01:38:02.000
The belief that the world will be fairer if&nbsp;
the rules are enshrined in code, enforced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:02.000 --> 01:38:07.600
computers, and made extremely difficult&nbsp;
to change or circumvent is laughable.

01:38:07.600 --> 01:38:11.120
It’s not merely naive, but&nbsp;
categorically ahistoric.

01:38:11.120 --> 01:38:13.520
This is where a lot of my resistance comes from.

01:38:13.520 --> 01:38:18.000
You can create specialized crypto chains&nbsp;
that have a negligible environmental impact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:18.000 --> 01:38:21.760
but the force of that model&nbsp;
is culturally destructive.

01:38:21.760 --> 01:38:26.000
The current system sucks, but this is just&nbsp;
a worse version of the current system.

01:38:26.000 --> 01:38:29.120
It doesn’t even stop there.&nbsp;
It’s tokens all the way down.

01:38:29.120 --> 01:38:34.400
“This game it has a, a good, a high barrier&nbsp;
of entry, so most people can’t do it unless&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:34.400 --> 01:38:37.760
they have what’s called a scholarship where&nbsp;
someone essentially pays for their barrier&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:37.760 --> 01:38:41.760
of entry to allow them to play the game and&nbsp;
then they can split the profits afterwards.”

01:38:41.760 --> 01:38:46.960
Like, okay, Axie Infinity is a so-called&nbsp;
“play to earn” video game based on Ethereum,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:46.960 --> 01:38:52.400
but because Ethereum is too slow and expensive to&nbsp;
interact with directly the developers, Sky Mavis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:52.400 --> 01:38:58.480
created a side-chain called Ronin, which consists&nbsp;
of a governance currency called AXS, the game&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:38:58.480 --> 01:39:04.240
character tokens called Axies, and the in-game&nbsp;
currency called Smooth Love Potions, or SLP.

01:39:04.240 --> 01:39:06.960
All of these components are&nbsp;
built to function as money,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:06.960 --> 01:39:09.200
because that’s what the machine is built to do.

01:39:09.200 --> 01:39:12.000
The so-called play-to-earn model is a great case&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:12.000 --> 01:39:16.240
study in what the end result of the&nbsp;
crypto ecosystem actually looks like.

01:39:16.240 --> 01:39:20.720
Axie Infinity gets a lot of credulous coverage&nbsp;
from the press because a handful of people in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:20.720 --> 01:39:25.440
Philippines are able to make a marginal living&nbsp;
by playing the game and flipping their SLP,

01:39:25.440 --> 01:39:30.000
which lets Sky Maven turn around and pretend&nbsp;
that they’re a humanitarian organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:30.000 --> 01:39:33.840
and not a for-profit business who makes&nbsp;
money off all the people rushing to buy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:33.840 --> 01:39:36.880
a team of Axies believing they&nbsp;
can get paid just for playing.

01:39:36.880 --> 01:39:40.800
Now, real quick, Axie Infinity is&nbsp;
a lightweight card-based PvP game&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:40.800 --> 01:39:43.680
where players fight with a team&nbsp;
of three critters called Axies.

01:39:43.680 --> 01:39:46.400
It’s a bit Pokemon and a bit Slay the Spire.

01:39:46.400 --> 01:39:49.280
Axis have randomized attributes&nbsp;
and are all tokenized,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:49.280 --> 01:39:53.040
so if you sell an Axie you’re&nbsp;
selling that specific Axie.

01:39:53.040 --> 01:39:57.920
Smooth Love Potions are used to breed Axies and&nbsp;
thus form the basis of the economy as they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:39:57.920 --> 01:40:00.640
fungible relative to the Axies themselves.

01:40:00.640 --> 01:40:02.480
Matches are either ranked or unranked,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:02.480 --> 01:40:07.520
but only ranked matches, entered using energy&nbsp;
which recharges every day, can earn SLP.

01:40:07.520 --> 01:40:10.320
The amount of energy you have to&nbsp;
work with depends on the number&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:10.320 --> 01:40:14.000
of axies in your wallet, and ranges from 20 to 60.

01:40:14.000 --> 01:40:17.600
Additionally higher ranked&nbsp;
matches are worth more SLP.

01:40:17.600 --> 01:40:20.000
More SLP means a bigger stable of Axies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:20.000 --> 01:40:23.600
which means more energy to play matches,&nbsp;
more flexibility within the metagame,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:23.600 --> 01:40:28.160
better team compositions, and baseline&nbsp;
better stats, enabling easier wins.

01:40:28.160 --> 01:40:30.960
So the difference in earning&nbsp;
potential between higher and&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:30.960 --> 01:40:33.680
lower ranked accounts is pretty substantial.

01:40:34.240 --> 01:40:38.000
What coverage tends to note and then&nbsp;
quickly move on from is the fact that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:38.000 --> 01:40:43.120
game is extremely expensive to onboard, costing&nbsp;
hundreds of dollars to assemble a basic team,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:43.120 --> 01:40:47.520
which has led to the rise of what are&nbsp;
euphemistically called scholarship programs:

01:40:47.520 --> 01:40:51.280
businesses like Whale Scholars that&nbsp;
set up a large number of accounts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:51.280 --> 01:40:55.440
give players access to the game&nbsp;
account but not the underlying wallet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:40:55.440 --> 01:41:00.000
and then pay players a split of&nbsp;
the SLP the account generates.

01:41:00.000 --> 01:41:02.720
Whale Scholars, and other “mentorship” businesses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:02.720 --> 01:41:08.560
retain full control of the wallet, and&nbsp;
thus the Axies, the SLP, and any AXS.

01:41:08.560 --> 01:41:10.880
It’s capitalism in its rawest form.

01:41:10.880 --> 01:41:16.160
Players invest their time into grinding SLP,&nbsp;
and then the owners take half the returns.

01:41:16.160 --> 01:41:20.400
Players get to barely make minimum wage&nbsp;
while the owners, who are taking 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:20.400 --> 01:41:25.200
from dozens, if not hundreds of players,&nbsp;
get to speculate on digital land.

01:41:25.200 --> 01:41:36.240
“Look what the Whale Scholars just got. We have&nbsp;
land in the arctic baby! We have arctic land!”

01:41:36.960 --> 01:41:42.000
Axie’s energy system is fundamentally broken in&nbsp;
how it promotes this exact middleman arrangement.

01:41:42.000 --> 01:41:46.720
If you have 21 Axies in your wallet you&nbsp;
can create one account that has 60 energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:46.720 --> 01:41:52.320
at its disposal, or you can create seven&nbsp;
bare-minimum accounts with 20 energy each.

01:41:52.320 --> 01:41:57.600
A high-ranked account with 60 energy will&nbsp;
dramatically out-perform a single low ranked&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:41:57.600 --> 01:42:03.280
account with 20 energy, but seven accounts&nbsp;
that are played daily by other people is far&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:03.280 --> 01:42:09.120
and away the best ROI because it involves doing&nbsp;
basically nothing except collecting your cut.

01:42:09.120 --> 01:42:11.760
Even more illustrative, between August and October&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:11.760 --> 01:42:15.440
2021 the internal economy&nbsp;
of Axie Infinity crashed.

01:42:15.440 --> 01:42:20.560
Not absolute rock bottom, but bad enough that&nbsp;
all but the highest ranking players, fell below&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:20.560 --> 01:42:26.400
the average daily wage in the Philippines, with&nbsp;
low ranked players falling below minimum wage.

01:42:26.400 --> 01:42:30.640
Naavik, a think tank and consulting firm&nbsp;
that does deep research on game economies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:30.640 --> 01:42:34.080
has done a pretty deep and thorough&nbsp;
analysis of the Axie economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:34.080 --> 01:42:38.000
and in their write up they identify&nbsp;
the core source is psychological:

01:42:38.000 --> 01:42:42.480
players aren’t playing to play the&nbsp;
game, they’re playing to make money,

01:42:42.480 --> 01:42:48.080
they treat the game as a job and thus have&nbsp;
little interest in game items for their own sake.

01:42:48.080 --> 01:42:54.400
Once they have enough stuff to cover their job&nbsp;
they stop reinvesting and start cashing out.

01:42:54.400 --> 01:42:57.200
The price of low-quality axies, the ones that new&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:42:57.200 --> 01:43:01.200
players are likely to buy in order to&nbsp;
onboard for the lowest cost possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:01.200 --> 01:43:06.880
is propped up entirely by players buying them&nbsp;
roughly at the same rate as they’re generated.

01:43:06.880 --> 01:43:11.680
This requires either mentors to be scaling&nbsp;
up their teams by buying axies instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:11.680 --> 01:43:17.120
generating them, or new players injecting&nbsp;
new money into the ecosystem by onboarding.

01:43:17.120 --> 01:43:21.760
But the more players that are playing, the&nbsp;
more SLP and axies that will be generated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:21.760 --> 01:43:25.760
thus the fundamentally unsustainable&nbsp;
economic model where an infinite supply&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:25.760 --> 01:43:30.720
of new players must enter the ecosystem&nbsp;
forever purely to keep the price stable.

01:43:30.720 --> 01:43:36.320
If it tips one way or the other you either&nbsp;
get runaway inflation or runaway deflation.

01:43:36.320 --> 01:43:41.040
This happens all the time in games,&nbsp;
games bork their internal economy&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:41.040 --> 01:43:44.720
constantly, it just doesn’t normally&nbsp;
matter because you’re talking about&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:44.720 --> 01:43:48.320
completely fictional gold,&nbsp;
or gems, or dragon bones.

01:43:48.320 --> 01:43:53.280
A truly stable economy isn’t even desirable&nbsp;
from a gameplay standpoint, anyway.

01:43:53.840 --> 01:43:58.640
Gentle inflation ends up helping newer and&nbsp;
more casual players by driving down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:43:58.640 --> 01:44:04.160
costs of things in the in-game economy which&nbsp;
lets those players participate and have fun.

01:44:04.160 --> 01:44:08.720
Of course if your pitch is “play to&nbsp;
earn” and not “play to have fun”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:08.720 --> 01:44:13.600
where the optics buoying up the ficitive&nbsp;
valuation of your company rest entirely on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:13.600 --> 01:44:18.720
assumption that players can earn, then that’s&nbsp;
a bit of a different incentive set, isn’t it?

01:44:18.720 --> 01:44:23.760
More people play Axie Infinity to try and make&nbsp;
money than play it because it’s a game they enjoy.

01:44:23.760 --> 01:44:26.240
This is a fundamental flaw in the model.

01:44:26.240 --> 01:44:30.080
If you pitch your game based on earning&nbsp;
potential, you are going to attract people&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:30.080 --> 01:44:36.480
seeking to industrialize your platform faster&nbsp;
and in greater numbers than would otherwise play.

01:44:36.480 --> 01:44:40.000
This is exactly the parasitic&nbsp;
situation that games for decades&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:40.000 --> 01:44:44.880
now have been actively minimizing because&nbsp;
it creates vicious negative externalities.

01:44:44.880 --> 01:44:48.880
If players can sell their in-game stuff&nbsp;
then it changes the way they play the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:48.880 --> 01:44:52.720
it changes they way they optimize their playtime.

01:44:52.720 --> 01:44:56.320
Since the vast majority of games&nbsp;
are openly a non-investment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:44:56.320 --> 01:44:58.720
a straight exchange of money for entertainment,

01:44:58.720 --> 01:45:00.960
also known as a “purchase”,

01:45:00.960 --> 01:45:05.520
players tend to optimize their play time for&nbsp;
intangible returns like fun, distraction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:05.520 --> 01:45:10.960
socialization, relaxation, challenge, achievement,&nbsp;
and narrative fulfillment, with absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:10.960 --> 01:45:15.840
no expectation that the money and time put in&nbsp;
should return anything other than those things.

01:45:16.560 --> 01:45:20.480
The key shift here, and the meaningless&nbsp;
buzzphrase that you’ll encounter online,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:20.480 --> 01:45:24.640
is the proposition that the results of&nbsp;
playing a game should “retain value”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:24.640 --> 01:45:27.760
which is code for having a&nbsp;
potentially speculative price.

01:45:28.400 --> 01:45:31.840
In order to try and keep the grift&nbsp;
mill running for another few months&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:31.840 --> 01:45:36.960
Sky Maven have tweaked the economy to reduce&nbsp;
the amount of SLP players can earn per hour.

01:45:36.960 --> 01:45:42.560
It is a nightmarishly thin edge to be walking on,&nbsp;
and the main thing it has managed to accomplish&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:42.560 --> 01:45:48.960
is enabling an entire strata of pit bosses running&nbsp;
teams of players grinding out Smooth Love Potions.

01:45:48.960 --> 01:45:52.880
And, like all bosses, they have&nbsp;
not taken the downturn in stride,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:45:52.880 --> 01:45:55.200
and have instead started cracking the whip.

01:45:55.200 --> 01:46:00.000
“We do not accept mediocre gaming&nbsp;
anymore. Need at least 120-150&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:00.560 --> 01:46:05.200
SLP a day, and those who yield more will&nbsp;
be rewarded with additional percentage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:05.760 --> 01:46:10.960
I prioritize those who have gaming experience,&nbsp;
we have a separate program for charity.”

01:46:10.960 --> 01:46:14.560
Evangelists like to point to this&nbsp;
as though it’s inspiring, people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:14.560 --> 01:46:19.440
economically disempowered countries able to make&nbsp;
a meagre living by simply playing a video game.

01:46:19.440 --> 01:46:22.080
I reject that framing. It’s horrifying.

01:46:22.080 --> 01:46:25.520
Our global system is so fundamentally&nbsp;
unjust that people are patting themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:25.520 --> 01:46:29.360
on the back for generating a whole new&nbsp;
kind of online UwU pit boss who tells&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:29.360 --> 01:46:33.600
you to grind harder or you’re fired&nbsp;
but caps it off with blushy emoji.

01:46:33.600 --> 01:46:37.200
Oopsie, wooks wike somewun&nbsp;
didn’t meet theiwr qwota.

01:46:37.840 --> 01:46:39.840
This sucks.

01:46:39.840 --> 01:46:41.703
♪ We all gonna make it ♪&nbsp;

01:46:41.703 --> 01:46:45.363
♪ Love love love love ♪
♪ Do you wanna make it ♪&nbsp;

01:46:45.363 --> 01:46:46.960
♪ love ♪
To the moon!

01:46:46.960 --> 01:46:51.600
I think the thing that normies don't get&nbsp;
about NFT bros is their dedication, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:51.600 --> 01:46:56.240
staggering volume of capital they already&nbsp;
control, and how deeply rooted they are in&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:46:56.240 --> 01:47:00.160
the culture of the people who operate&nbsp;
the platforms we all use every day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:00.160 --> 01:47:03.680
and that alone is a good reason&nbsp;
for people to pay attention.

01:47:03.680 --> 01:47:08.880
They have a lot of money and a lot of clout&nbsp;
that they can use to try and make Fetch happen.

01:47:08.880 --> 01:47:11.760
This is something of the&nbsp;
splitting point. Basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:11.760 --> 01:47:14.640
the future shakes out in one of two broad ways.

01:47:14.640 --> 01:47:19.280
One is that some new technological buzzword&nbsp;
comes along and “blockchain” and “web3” lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:19.280 --> 01:47:22.800
their sway over investors, the&nbsp;
stream of new buyers dries up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:22.800 --> 01:47:26.800
and the early investors cash out as&nbsp;
best they can, popping the whole bubble.

01:47:26.800 --> 01:47:30.720
The other is that they’re successful,&nbsp;
and cryptocurrency is able to crowbar&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:30.720 --> 01:47:33.680
its way into enough corners of our&nbsp;
lives that it becomes unavoidable,

01:47:33.680 --> 01:47:37.280
we’re all forced in some way&nbsp;
to maintain a crypto wallet&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:37.280 --> 01:47:42.400
to manage whatever coins and tokens become&nbsp;
necessary for participation in society,

01:47:42.400 --> 01:47:46.480
providing early investors with a captive&nbsp;
audience and steady flow of capital.

01:47:46.480 --> 01:47:51.520
To quote German sociotechnologist Jürgen&nbsp;
Geuter, better known by his online alias Tante

01:47:51.520 --> 01:47:55.520
“There are parts of your digital life&nbsp;
that currently you can’t really sell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:47:55.520 --> 01:48:00.720
but that’s what they want to change. Everything&nbsp;
needs to be bought and sold, everything is just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:00.720 --> 01:48:06.400
vehicle for more speculation. The reason they want&nbsp;
you to be able to resell your access token to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:06.400 --> 01:48:11.120
service (instead of buying or renting it like&nbsp;
today) is to create even more markets for&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:11.120 --> 01:48:16.720
speculation and the smart contracts can be set&nbsp;
up in a way that at every corner they profit.”

01:48:16.720 --> 01:48:19.680
The claims that this technology&nbsp;
facilitates an immutable ledger&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:19.680 --> 01:48:25.280
of ownership is itself largely hollow&nbsp;
posturing, even from within the ecosystem.

01:48:25.280 --> 01:48:30.480
Remember that most of the actual things being&nbsp;
referenced are not contained within the chains&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:30.480 --> 01:48:34.400
themselves, because the chains are too&nbsp;
slow, restrictive, and bad at their job&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:34.400 --> 01:48:40.320
to actually store media, and because many of&nbsp;
the things being sold are purely ephemeral.

01:48:40.320 --> 01:48:46.160
The IPFS address for any given media token can be&nbsp;
effortlessly minted onto another competing chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:46.160 --> 01:48:49.760
or even the same chain. That’s not&nbsp;
even right-click saving, that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:49.760 --> 01:48:52.000
referencing the exact same media.

01:48:52.000 --> 01:48:58.320
Why is the token on Ethereum more authoritative&nbsp;
than the token on Tezos or Cardano or Solana or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:48:58.320 --> 01:49:01.600
Ergo or Celo or Binance or Alogorand or Polkadot&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:01.600 --> 01:49:06.400
or Eos or Tron or VeChain or Ethereum&nbsp;
Classic or Fantom or Stellar or Stacks&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:06.400 --> 01:49:11.840
or Neo or Waves or Holo or LINK or Radix or&nbsp;
Harmony or Oasis or ICON or Secret or IOTA or&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:11.840 --> 01:49:16.240
Crown or TERA or Omni or Enigma or Elastos&nbsp;
or Edgeware or Bytom or Fuse or Gather?

01:49:16.240 --> 01:49:21.200
If a chain hard-forks, which version&nbsp;
of your stuff is the real one?

01:49:21.200 --> 01:49:27.600
Bitcoin is, itself, mired in a turf war&nbsp;
between Bitcoin, Bitcoin Cash, and Bitcoin SV.

01:49:27.600 --> 01:49:33.360
The myth of immutable ownership governed by these&nbsp;
systems is predicated on a monolithic victor.

01:49:33.360 --> 01:49:38.720
In reality your NFTs within the Ethereum&nbsp;
ecosystem are ultimately just as trapped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:38.720 --> 01:49:42.880
sandboxed, and meaningless&nbsp;
as your Steam trading cards.

01:49:42.880 --> 01:49:47.760
You’ll hear about protocols like Polygon that&nbsp;
aim to let you move stuff from chain to chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:47.760 --> 01:49:49.360
but that’s sleight of hand.

01:49:49.360 --> 01:49:54.160
You can’t remove something from a chain, so&nbsp;
all they really do is create a new token at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:54.160 --> 01:49:58.400
the destination and add a note to the&nbsp;
bottom of the original token that says&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:49:58.400 --> 01:50:01.440
“I’m currently somewhere else,&nbsp;
please don’t move or sell me.”

01:50:01.440 --> 01:50:06.000
It’s an ask that’s governed by smart&nbsp;
contracts, so vulnerable to bad coding.

01:50:06.000 --> 01:50:10.960
In video game terms they would be immediately&nbsp;
hammered looking for item duplication glitches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:10.960 --> 01:50:15.520
a vulnerability that’s basically&nbsp;
inevitable in a mass adoption scenario.

01:50:15.520 --> 01:50:17.040
It’s a system that is at once&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:17.040 --> 01:50:22.640
impenetrable and brittle, and that arrangement&nbsp;
disproportionately empowers the dishonest.

01:50:22.640 --> 01:50:27.040
One of the complications is that it’s basically&nbsp;
impossible to extricate the digital scarcity&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:27.040 --> 01:50:32.800
concepts of NFTs from cryptocurrency and the core&nbsp;
philosophies that cryptocurrency was built from.

01:50:32.800 --> 01:50:36.720
One rose out of the other and they&nbsp;
are basically forever entwined.

01:50:36.720 --> 01:50:41.040
One of the ironies of all this is that&nbsp;
any legitimate artistic or anti-capitalist&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:41.040 --> 01:50:45.680
uses of the underlying technology are&nbsp;
contingent on the tech remaining niche.

01:50:46.240 --> 01:50:49.280
On a very basic level, the systems just suck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:49.280 --> 01:50:53.200
being slow, difficult to use, and&nbsp;
generally oblique. For the most part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:50:53.200 --> 01:50:58.320
to the degree that they’re usable at all, it is&nbsp;
largely at the mercy of only having a few users.

01:50:58.320 --> 01:51:02.240
There are blockchains that are reasonably&nbsp;
responsive and reasonably cheap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:02.240 --> 01:51:03.920
because they’re not popular.

01:51:03.920 --> 01:51:09.440
Hic Et Nunc is a well regarded art market on the&nbsp;
Tezos blockchain. Transaction fees and deflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:09.440 --> 01:51:14.160
are, at the moment, relatively minimal and thus a&nbsp;
lot of the transactions are able to operate in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:14.160 --> 01:51:20.560
range of five to twenty dollars, but that state&nbsp;
exists by the grace of being 45th in popularity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:20.560 --> 01:51:26.000
just high enough to actually have users, but&nbsp;
not high enough to have attracted too many bots.

01:51:26.000 --> 01:51:30.080
If Tezos goes, as they say, to&nbsp;
the moon, then that all changes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:30.080 --> 01:51:34.480
Users adopt the platform disproportionate to&nbsp;
the scale of validators, the value of of Tez&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:34.480 --> 01:51:39.360
skyrockets, and the actual marketplace of people&nbsp;
using Hic Et Nunc experience hyperdeflation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:39.360 --> 01:51:42.880
where currency hoarders are rewarded&nbsp;
handsomely and buyers are punished.

01:51:42.880 --> 01:51:47.440
Okay, we need to pause here for a moment,&nbsp;
because this is actually really important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:47.440 --> 01:51:52.400
but absurdly complex, like textbook-length&nbsp;
subject matter, so here’s the short version.

01:51:52.400 --> 01:51:55.280
Deflation is counter-intuitive&nbsp;
because the line is going up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:55.280 --> 01:51:58.000
which makes it look like a good&nbsp;
thing, but it’s only good if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:51:58.000 --> 01:52:02.240
already have the currency in hand. As the&nbsp;
purchasing power of a currency increases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:02.240 --> 01:52:06.640
typically because cash gets more scarce,&nbsp;
the cost of goods and labour goes down.

01:52:06.640 --> 01:52:09.040
A deflationary economy punishes buying things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:09.040 --> 01:52:12.640
as anything that you buy today will&nbsp;
inevitably be cheaper to buy in the future.

01:52:12.640 --> 01:52:17.680
If you need to buy things that aren’t financial&nbsp;
assets, things that don’t appreciate in value,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:17.680 --> 01:52:22.160
like food, clothing, rent, vehicles,&nbsp;
transit fare, you screw yourself over.

01:52:22.160 --> 01:52:27.680
This is hyperdeflation, and it’s not only&nbsp;
designed into cryptocurrencies with their&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:27.680 --> 01:52:32.800
hard cap on total coin supply, but considered&nbsp;
desirable by their creators and evangelists.&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:32.800 --> 01:52:35.920
This is what going “to the moon” means.

01:52:36.960 --> 01:52:38.320
Now let’s talk about unions.

01:52:38.880 --> 01:52:43.040
Using tokens to verify union membership&nbsp;
and participate in union activities&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:43.040 --> 01:52:47.040
relies on the tech being oblique enough&nbsp;
that union busters and their clients&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:47.040 --> 01:52:49.840
don’t see it as a meaningful arena to monitor.

01:52:49.840 --> 01:52:52.080
Also that ship has already sailed.

01:52:52.080 --> 01:52:55.360
Union busters and gig economy evangelists love&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:52:55.360 --> 01:53:00.080
crypto, they love DeFi, they love&nbsp;
smart contracts, and they love NFTs.

01:53:00.080 --> 01:53:01.280
And why wouldn’t they?

01:53:01.280 --> 01:53:03.360
It’s an environment that demolishes consumer&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:03.360 --> 01:53:07.360
protections and transfers tremendous&nbsp;
amounts of explicit power to the wealthy.

01:53:07.360 --> 01:53:11.600
In a lot of ways this is all just a system for&nbsp;
deferring trust onto machines and pretending&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:11.600 --> 01:53:16.640
that there aren’t humans on the other end, and if&nbsp;
there’s one thing that union busters love it’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:16.640 --> 01:53:23.200
prospect of an unbreakable individual contract&nbsp;
whose inequities can all be blamed on a machine.

01:53:23.200 --> 01:53:24.480
The current state of the web,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:24.480 --> 01:53:29.360
concentrated in a few mega platforms,&nbsp;
is the result of compounding complexity.

01:53:29.360 --> 01:53:34.640
We used to have a web where anyone could learn&nbsp;
to write a webpage in HTML in an afternoon.

01:53:34.640 --> 01:53:38.640
It’s just writing text and then&nbsp;
using tags to format the text.

01:53:38.640 --> 01:53:43.520
But over time people, understandably,&nbsp;
wanted the web to do more, to look better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:43.520 --> 01:53:49.360
and so the things that were possible expanded&nbsp;
via scripting languages that allowed for dynamic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:49.360 --> 01:53:50.880
interactive content.

01:53:50.880 --> 01:53:56.400
Soon the definition of what a “website” was and&nbsp;
looked like sailed out of reach of casual users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:53:56.400 --> 01:54:00.720
and eventually even out of reach of&nbsp;
all but the most dedicated hobbyists.

01:54:00.720 --> 01:54:04.320
It became the domain of&nbsp;
specialists. So casual users,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:04.320 --> 01:54:09.280
excluded by complexity, moved to&nbsp;
templates, services, and platforms.

01:54:09.280 --> 01:54:11.840
This process gradually concentrated a critical&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:11.840 --> 01:54:15.920
mass of users into a handful&nbsp;
of social media platforms.

01:54:15.920 --> 01:54:19.360
Already, even within the space,&nbsp;
new hegemons are forming.

01:54:19.360 --> 01:54:23.760
Tremendous amounts of capital and power are&nbsp;
concentrating in corporations like Consensys,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:23.760 --> 01:54:29.040
who own MetaMask, and Animoca Brands, who have&nbsp;
wide and deep investment in crypto gaming.

01:54:29.040 --> 01:54:33.760
OpenSea, the at present dominant marketplace&nbsp;
for tokens on a couple different chains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:33.760 --> 01:54:36.480
is filling the power roles users need.

01:54:36.480 --> 01:54:40.320
While the chain itself is, in theory,&nbsp;
the arbiter of truth, nothing prevents&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:40.320 --> 01:54:46.320
people from filling the chain with lies, and so&nbsp;
arises a demand for not merely a chain parser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:46.320 --> 01:54:51.680
a service that enables users to interact with&nbsp;
the chain, but an interpreter of the chain.

01:54:52.320 --> 01:54:55.440
Motivational speaker and easy&nbsp;
mark Calvin Baccera claimed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:54:55.440 --> 01:54:59.680
have lost three Bored Ape Yacht Club&nbsp;
tokens to a social engineering scam.

01:54:59.680 --> 01:55:04.400
In reaction to this he took to Twitter to whip&nbsp;
up a mob that could pressure OpenSea and two&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:04.400 --> 01:55:09.440
other marketplaces into flagging the tokens&nbsp;
as stolen and blocking them from being sold.

01:55:09.440 --> 01:55:14.320
Calvin was eventually able to resecure his&nbsp;
tokens by paying a ransom, because that’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:14.320 --> 01:55:18.080
really all you can do and he doesn’t seem&nbsp;
to consider that from the perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:18.080 --> 01:55:21.600
of the thieves that’s an&nbsp;
entirely desirable outcome.

01:55:21.600 --> 01:55:23.840
They won. Their plan worked.

01:55:23.840 --> 01:55:25.920
This happens all the time.

01:55:25.920 --> 01:55:29.360
Bored Ape members are particularly&nbsp;
susceptible targets of fraud owing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:29.360 --> 01:55:33.280
to their specific combination of&nbsp;
greed and low social literacy.

01:55:33.280 --> 01:55:38.160
Looking to avoid paying platform fees and&nbsp;
royalties many of them moved off OpenSea to doing&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:38.160 --> 01:55:43.200
transactions on a shady little platform called&nbsp;
NFT Trader, which allowed scammers to run a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:43.200 --> 01:55:48.640
simple link swap scam and steal at least a dozen&nbsp;
different ape tokens in the span of a couple days.

01:55:48.640 --> 01:55:50.960
The meat of Calvin’s incident is the way in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:50.960 --> 01:55:55.040
the platforms that interact with the&nbsp;
chain are being deputized by users&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:55:55.040 --> 01:56:02.880
to be the de facto authority not on what&nbsp;
the chain says, but what the chain means.

01:56:02.880 --> 01:56:13.840
It is just a recreation of existing power&nbsp;
structures within the new environment.

01:56:15.120 --> 01:56:15.280
[Drumming]

01:56:15.280 --> 01:56:18.960
This is where evangelists insist&nbsp;
that the answer lies in DAOs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:18.960 --> 01:56:24.640
decentralized autonomous organizations, a&nbsp;
“revolutionary” new way to organize people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:24.640 --> 01:56:28.240
that will allow for the decentralized&nbsp;
governance of these systems.

01:56:28.240 --> 01:56:33.360
So that’s the claim, but what is it,&nbsp;
exactly, once you strip off the paint?

01:56:33.360 --> 01:56:36.160
A DAO is an organization whose membership, roles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:36.160 --> 01:56:40.640
and privileges are governed by possession&nbsp;
of relevant tokens on a given blockchain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:40.640 --> 01:56:44.720
and it’s also the underlying software&nbsp;
that executes relevant operations.

01:56:44.720 --> 01:56:47.120
And that’s kinda really about it.

01:56:47.120 --> 01:56:53.760
So just to be very clear here, a conceptual&nbsp;
DAO consists of three things: people, a digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:56:53.760 --> 01:56:59.280
machine built of smart contracts, and a token that&nbsp;
allows the people to interact with the machine.

01:57:00.240 --> 01:57:06.320
In practice most things that call themselves&nbsp;
DAOs don’t have the machine at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:06.320 --> 01:57:12.000
and a substantial number either don’t&nbsp;
have the token, or only have the token.

01:57:12.000 --> 01:57:17.840
The upside of a DAO is that it makes it&nbsp;
easy-ish to create a formal organization at&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:17.840 --> 01:57:22.480
theoretically any scale, from only a couple&nbsp;
people to massive pools of stakeholders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:22.480 --> 01:57:27.200
and the program layer makes it possible to&nbsp;
automate certain activities and the results.

01:57:27.200 --> 01:57:30.160
If the organization votes via the DAO interface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:30.160 --> 01:57:34.720
the results of that vote are automatically&nbsp;
recorded, and potentially executed.

01:57:34.720 --> 01:57:37.680
Though that framing is misleading.

01:57:37.680 --> 01:57:41.440
As with tokens themselves there’s&nbsp;
no inherent functionality in a DAO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:41.440 --> 01:57:43.520
it’s just a box that code goes into.

01:57:43.520 --> 01:57:46.880
I might as well be referring to all&nbsp;
the things you could do with a webpage.

01:57:47.520 --> 01:57:50.160
As already mentioned, many&nbsp;
organizations presenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:50.160 --> 01:57:53.360
themselves as a DAO have no&nbsp;
machine functionality at all.

01:57:53.360 --> 01:57:56.720
It’s pretty standard to find a DAO&nbsp;
that has issued a governance token,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:57:56.720 --> 01:58:02.080
the scrip that’s used for voting, with the&nbsp;
systems to actually use that token being placed&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:02.080 --> 01:58:04.960
somewhere in the nebulous future of the roadmap.

01:58:04.960 --> 01:58:09.360
That open-endedness is actually important because&nbsp;
while the claim is that these machines will&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:09.360 --> 01:58:14.960
further democratize the internet, the technical&nbsp;
complexity that they add, the new specialized&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:14.960 --> 01:58:19.200
programming expertise that they require,&nbsp;
concentrates a lot of power in the hands&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:19.200 --> 01:58:24.400
of the people who can build the templates that in&nbsp;
turn enable non-programmers to actually use it.

01:58:24.400 --> 01:58:28.400
It’s just laying the seeds for the&nbsp;
future recreation of the status quo.

01:58:28.400 --> 01:58:33.840
The Facebook/Google/Amazon dominated internet&nbsp;
arose because the technical cost of building a&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:33.840 --> 01:58:38.320
modern website rose far beyond what the&nbsp;
vast majority of amateurs could manage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:38.320 --> 01:58:42.480
so everyone moved to templates, and then&nbsp;
to services, and finally to platforms.

01:58:42.480 --> 01:58:45.600
This doesn’t even reset the clock on that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:45.600 --> 01:58:50.640
the technical cost of creating a DAO is&nbsp;
already far beyond any casual amateur,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:58:50.640 --> 01:58:55.440
in part because all of this is being built by&nbsp;
programmers, and in part because of the stakes.

01:58:55.440 --> 01:58:59.680
The only thing this stuff is truly&nbsp;
good for is managing on-chain assets.

01:58:59.680 --> 01:59:05.120
A DAO program can see the state of the chain and&nbsp;
interact with it, so the DAO humans can vote on&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:05.120 --> 01:59:10.240
what should happen to those assets and then the&nbsp;
DAO program can automatically act on the results.

01:59:10.240 --> 01:59:11.840
But that raises the stakes.

01:59:11.840 --> 01:59:17.280
Because a DAO can see and interact directly&nbsp;
with on-chain assets there’s the risk that via&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:17.280 --> 01:59:23.680
bad programming or unforeseen exploits a malicious&nbsp;
actor can use a DAO to access all kinds of stuff.

01:59:23.680 --> 01:59:28.240
The risk is directly proportional to&nbsp;
the value of the assets kept on-chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:28.240 --> 01:59:33.280
and remember, again, that evangelists&nbsp;
want to put everything on-chain.

01:59:33.280 --> 01:59:37.040
The hilarious thing is that this&nbsp;
has already played out once before.

01:59:37.040 --> 01:59:41.440
In fact it played out with the first&nbsp;
DAO ever built, called The DAO.

01:59:42.080 --> 01:59:46.960
This whole story unfolds over the course of&nbsp;
three months in 2016, from April to June.

01:59:46.960 --> 01:59:52.480
The DAO was an Ethereum-based venture capital fund&nbsp;
that aimed to use code to create an investment&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:52.480 --> 01:59:57.360
firm without a conventional management structure&nbsp;
or board of directors, a scheme that’s positioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

01:59:57.360 --> 02:00:01.600
as “lightweight” and “reducing bureaucratic&nbsp;
overhead”, but really it just translated&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:01.600 --> 02:00:06.000
to an attempt at minimizing human liability&nbsp;
for the actions and behaviours of the fund.

02:00:06.000 --> 02:00:09.360
This unparalleled expression of&nbsp;
greed made the major speculative&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:09.360 --> 02:00:12.000
players in Ethereum so horny that during the April&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:12.000 --> 02:00:18.880
and May presale they funneled 14% of the entire&nbsp;
volume of ether into The DAO’s central wallet.

02:00:18.880 --> 02:00:23.840
Now, because The DAO’s underlying code was open&nbsp;
source, experts and malicious actors alike were&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:23.840 --> 02:00:28.560
able to pour over it for vulnerabilities,&nbsp;
and, indeed, vulnerabilities were found.

02:00:28.560 --> 02:00:32.880
However, because at the end of the day&nbsp;
fleshy humans are the ones actually pushing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:32.880 --> 02:00:37.440
buttons and making decisions, the actual&nbsp;
leadership of the nominally-leaderless DAO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:37.440 --> 02:00:41.280
horny for money and prestige,&nbsp;
decided to launch in late May anyway.

02:00:41.280 --> 02:00:45.950
Three weeks later The DAO’s programming was&nbsp;
exploited and the attacker was able to transfer&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:45.950 --> 02:00:52.160
1/3rd of The DAO’s funds into a holding wallet,&nbsp;
about 5% of the entire Ethereum economy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:52.160 --> 02:00:55.600
valued at the time around&nbsp;
16 to 17 million dollars.

02:00:55.600 --> 02:00:59.040
Now, because this threatened the&nbsp;
bottom line of capital holders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:00:59.040 --> 02:01:03.200
the Ethereum project as a whole, the&nbsp;
entire thing, was almost immediately&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:03.200 --> 02:01:06.720
forked in order to undo the hack and&nbsp;
protect the interests of the wealthy.

02:01:06.720 --> 02:01:11.200
Ethereum Classic, the arm of the fork that&nbsp;
didn’t undo the attack, persists to this day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:11.200 --> 02:01:15.120
though it’s notably less popular despite&nbsp;
being demonstrably more principled.

02:01:15.120 --> 02:01:17.760
Because all the talk about&nbsp;
“decentralization” is a myth.

02:01:17.760 --> 02:01:18.720
It’s just words.

02:01:18.720 --> 02:01:23.200
At the end of the day the guys in charge, the guys&nbsp;
who built the system to serve their interests,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:23.200 --> 02:01:26.000
are still in charge and keep a&nbsp;
killswitch in their back pocket.

02:01:26.000 --> 02:01:31.920
Crypto is barely a decade old and organizations&nbsp;
deemed too big to fail already exist.

02:01:31.920 --> 02:01:34.720
The whole fiasco laid out&nbsp;
the truth from the word go:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:34.720 --> 02:01:39.840
calling a DAO a revolutionary structure is&nbsp;
smoke and mirrors, it’s just voting shares.

02:01:39.840 --> 02:01:43.120
You might as well call Apple “a bold&nbsp;
experiment in democracy” because&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:43.120 --> 02:01:46.720
a baker’s dozen individuals make&nbsp;
the decisions instead of just one.

02:01:46.720 --> 02:01:50.720
Regardless of the future pitfalls,&nbsp;
DAOs are also extremely limited.

02:01:50.720 --> 02:01:52.400
They are, again, just code.

02:01:52.400 --> 02:01:55.920
While evangelists promise that they&nbsp;
can reinvent the social organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:55.920 --> 02:01:59.920
mentally consider all the problems,&nbsp;
conflicts, and decision making that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:01:59.920 --> 02:02:06.480
social organizations deal with and ask how&nbsp;
many of those even can be solved by code.

02:02:06.480 --> 02:02:09.440
Some of them can easily be&nbsp;
turned into computer programs.

02:02:09.440 --> 02:02:13.440
Automated bookkeeping, payouts,&nbsp;
collections, data tracking, sure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:13.440 --> 02:02:17.040
that’s all stuff organizations&nbsp;
conceptually can make use of.

02:02:17.040 --> 02:02:21.840
But how do you code for the fact that Red&nbsp;
just really doesn’t get along with Blue?

02:02:21.840 --> 02:02:26.000
The pitch promises organizations&nbsp;
bound by unbreakable rules,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:26.000 --> 02:02:30.480
but how many organizations actually&nbsp;
benefit from that level of rigidity?

02:02:30.480 --> 02:02:35.280
In particular what happens when the&nbsp;
version of the rules enforced by code&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:35.280 --> 02:02:38.880
run up against a complication&nbsp;
that the coders didn’t consider?

02:02:38.880 --> 02:02:41.360
What happens if someone with&nbsp;
legitimate stake in the DAO&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:41.360 --> 02:02:44.960
starts spamming the organization’s&nbsp;
internal systems with bad requests?

02:02:44.960 --> 02:02:47.440
What if not enough people participate in voting?

02:02:47.440 --> 02:02:49.600
What if the system locks itself up?

02:02:49.600 --> 02:02:51.440
What if the rules are rigged?

02:02:51.440 --> 02:02:54.320
What if the system commits a crime?

02:02:54.320 --> 02:02:59.360
If this technology did see mass adoption, a future&nbsp;
timebomb already exists in the fact that very,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:02:59.360 --> 02:03:04.320
very few of these systems have factored mortality&nbsp;
into the considerations of their structures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:04.320 --> 02:03:09.680
because “what if someone with an important token&nbsp;
dies?” is a really easy thing to overlook when&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:09.680 --> 02:03:14.640
you’re the kind of insulated techbro who reinvents&nbsp;
vending machines and calls them Bodega Boxes.

02:03:14.640 --> 02:03:19.920
Now, all of these hypotheticals are technically&nbsp;
addressable, you can build contingency systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:19.920 --> 02:03:24.480
that can account for them, but then&nbsp;
you need to consider contingencies&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:24.480 --> 02:03:28.480
for those contingencies, because what if&nbsp;
someone uses systems intended for dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:28.480 --> 02:03:32.480
with deceased or absentee members to&nbsp;
expel people they just don’t like?

02:03:32.480 --> 02:03:35.120
And, again, you can only use code to enforce&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:35.120 --> 02:03:38.480
interactions that the programmers&nbsp;
make enforceable via the code.

02:03:38.480 --> 02:03:43.680
ConstitutionDAO, a hastily set up scheme to bid on&nbsp;
one of the few remaining original copies of the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:43.680 --> 02:03:48.560
constitution, already ran aground most of these&nbsp;
problems as the project failed to win the auction&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:48.560 --> 02:03:54.480
and is now trying to issue refunds, a thing that&nbsp;
the slapdash machine was never intended to do.

02:03:54.480 --> 02:03:58.880
The reality is that most organizations&nbsp;
with any meaningful social complexity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:03:58.880 --> 02:04:05.360
even tiny organizations like video game guilds,&nbsp;
are too complex to properly express in code.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:05.360 --> 02:04:10.320
There’s too many contingencies and contingencies&nbsp;
for those contingencies and contingencies for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:10.320 --> 02:04:15.600
those contingencies to account for, so rather&nbsp;
than trying to turn social interactions into code&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:15.600 --> 02:04:21.360
the DAO is marginalized into only handling&nbsp;
code-appropriate tasks, like bookkeeping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:21.360 --> 02:04:24.960
digital signature verification,&nbsp;
and on-chain asset management.

02:04:24.960 --> 02:04:30.480
But that’s not a revolutionary new way to&nbsp;
organize people, that’s just a productivity tool.

02:04:30.480 --> 02:04:34.320
The DAO can have a process for voting on&nbsp;
actions, but the moment the outcomes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:34.320 --> 02:04:40.000
those actions move off-chain, i.e. into the&nbsp;
real world, the DAO program is powerless.

02:04:40.000 --> 02:04:43.680
The program can’t make humans&nbsp;
execute the decisions of the group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:43.680 --> 02:04:45.920
that’s still an analog problem.

02:04:45.920 --> 02:04:50.160
The whole thing very quickly runs into&nbsp;
an incentive wall where it’s just faster&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:50.160 --> 02:04:55.840
and easier to solve problems verbally, via&nbsp;
abstract trust relationships and promises,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:04:55.840 --> 02:04:57.920
to the same end results.

02:04:57.920 --> 02:05:02.640
This is why it’s so common for DAOs to not&nbsp;
actually have any of the inner machine that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:02.640 --> 02:05:07.120
would actually make them into what they&nbsp;
claim to be: it’s easier to just not.

02:05:07.120 --> 02:05:10.400
Taken as a whole DAOs aren’t some&nbsp;
revolutionary new model, they’re a&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:10.400 --> 02:05:15.280
tool built onto the side of cryptocurrency that&nbsp;
only has meaningful advantages when interacting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:15.280 --> 02:05:20.240
with cryptocurrency as a tool for speculative&nbsp;
trading and managing financial instruments.

02:05:20.240 --> 02:05:25.200
The rest is just a gimmick, a slow,&nbsp;
inflexible tool for executing straw polls.

02:05:25.200 --> 02:05:30.240
Again, a lot of these boil down to a scheme to&nbsp;
minimize liability on the part of the creators.

02:05:30.240 --> 02:05:36.160
The creators of Inu Yasha Token, a meme coin DAO&nbsp;
based on nothing except the ephemeral concept of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:36.160 --> 02:05:42.080
the InuYasha anime, demonstrated this admirably&nbsp;
when they were pressed on the issue of copyright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:42.080 --> 02:05:45.600
openly trading on a known&nbsp;
brand specifically for clout.

02:05:45.600 --> 02:05:50.240
Their answer to the question boils down&nbsp;
to, one, a failure to understand copyright,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:50.240 --> 02:05:54.320
and two, an insistence that it doesn’t&nbsp;
matter because no one’s responsible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:05:54.320 --> 02:06:01.760
the DAO did it, no humans are liable,&nbsp;
just this amorphous sentient carbon cloud.

02:06:01.760 --> 02:06:04.240
“You have a really good way&nbsp;
to explain it about the um&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:04.960 --> 02:06:07.360
about the copyright issue&nbsp;
that everyone’s afraid of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:07.360 --> 02:06:10.640
because they don’t want to invest in a token&nbsp;
that’s going to be told to cease and desist or..

02:06:11.840 --> 02:06:16.320
Right, yeah so I mean, to clear that up&nbsp;
I think first you need to distinguish&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:16.320 --> 02:06:23.760
between a mark and copy. Right? So&nbsp;
right now on the website there’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:24.800 --> 02:06:30.000
it’s all custom art done by Steven. Your&nbsp;
friend steven. So there is no copy. I mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:30.000 --> 02:06:34.480
the only person that can really copyright&nbsp;
us right now is Steven. Uh. You know?

02:06:35.600 --> 02:06:38.080
What about logo likeness or character likeness?

02:06:38.640 --> 02:06:47.360
Yeah, so trademarks. Um, it’s possible that the&nbsp;
InuYasha mark it it could become scrutinized.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:47.920 --> 02:06:55.520
However we’re a decentralized autonomous&nbsp;
organization officially, and the token is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:06:55.520 --> 02:06:59.840
launched on the Ethereum blockchain, so&nbsp;
there’s, there’s really no going back.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:00.400 --> 02:07:07.040
I’m just a community member. I’m not an&nbsp;
owner, there’s really no single entity&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:07.040 --> 02:07:12.720
that has ownership so I mean it, it’s on&nbsp;
the blockchain now, there’s no going back.”

02:07:12.720 --> 02:07:18.080
And that bit also just so brilliantly demonstrates&nbsp;
the underlying mentality of a lot of these guys.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:18.080 --> 02:07:23.360
They wanted to use the Inu Yasha brand for&nbsp;
clout, but they didn’t want to ask for permission&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:23.360 --> 02:07:28.480
because they would probably get rejected,&nbsp;
so they did it anyway, so now that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:28.480 --> 02:07:34.800
“on chain” it can’t be easily taken down,&nbsp;
so I guess you gotta just let them do it?

02:07:34.800 --> 02:07:37.760
“it’s on the blockchain&nbsp;
now, there’s no going back.”

02:07:37.760 --> 02:07:41.200
Things get funny in that frustrating&nbsp;
way when you do come across a DAO&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:41.200 --> 02:07:44.320
that’s trying to be legitimate, and&nbsp;
they tip their hand by revealing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:44.320 --> 02:07:49.440
underneath they’re legally a co-op or an&nbsp;
LLC or some other extant legal entity.

02:07:49.440 --> 02:07:51.280
Unless your goal is a grift,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:07:51.280 --> 02:07:55.840
there’s nothing truly revolutionary&nbsp;
about their structure or functionality.

02:07:55.840 --> 02:08:00.160
Li Jin, the co-founder of a bunch of predatory&nbsp;
venture capital firms that focus on polishing&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:00.160 --> 02:08:05.600
the image of the gig economy to distract from the&nbsp;
ways in which its eroding labour, has an extended&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:05.600 --> 02:08:10.800
Twitter thread where she tries to pitch DAOs as&nbsp;
the future of unions, though her rationale is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:10.800 --> 02:08:16.720
only shaky, relying heavily on magical thinking,&nbsp;
it’s also peppered with inexplicable lies.

02:08:16.720 --> 02:08:22.480
For example she champions Yield Guild, a DAO that&nbsp;
she describes as “a gaming guild comprised of&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:22.480 --> 02:08:27.360
thousands of play-to-earn gamers. An onramp that&nbsp;
brings more players into play-to-earn gaming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:27.360 --> 02:08:30.960
it can represent gamers &amp; lobby&nbsp;
game devs for better policies.&nbsp;

02:08:30.960 --> 02:08:36.000
Its scale also enables the collective to offer&nbsp;
benefits and protections (e.g. healthcare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:36.000 --> 02:08:40.160
paid time off) that would be infeasible&nbsp;
if gamers were operating on their own.”

02:08:40.880 --> 02:08:46.000
This is a tremendous overstatement of what&nbsp;
Yield actually is. It’s not a union, nor does it&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:46.000 --> 02:08:51.120
function as a union, nor does it have aspirations&nbsp;
of functioning as a union. It’s not even a DAO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:51.120 --> 02:08:54.560
though it does have aspirations of&nbsp;
transitioning into being one. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:54.560 --> 02:08:58.880
at best a mildly decentralized cartel that’s&nbsp;
experimenting with shaking down players with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:08:58.880 --> 02:09:02.720
the promise of helping them by gamifying&nbsp;
the process of participating in the guild.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:02.720 --> 02:09:06.800
In practice it’s a Discord server that helps&nbsp;
play-to-earn players find sponsorships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:06.800 --> 02:09:10.800
pivot from one game to another, and generally&nbsp;
bitch about how much their jobs suck.

02:09:10.800 --> 02:09:14.480
In fact, in response to Li Jin’s tweets&nbsp;
the server residents had this to say

02:09:15.120 --> 02:09:19.680
“I've read that through the YGG DAO members&nbsp;
are able to get access to healthcare -- is&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:19.680 --> 02:09:23.040
this true? is there any more info on this?&nbsp;
anywhere to learn more about what is offered?&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:23.040 --> 02:09:27.600
or is this still in the works? as a freelancer&nbsp;
i'm always interested to learn about more options”

02:09:28.400 --> 02:09:32.160
“i actually don't know that lol. that would&nbsp;
be awesome to get partnered with healthcare”

02:09:32.160 --> 02:09:35.280
“Where did you get that info though?&nbsp;
We haven't heard of any kind about it”

02:09:35.280 --> 02:09:42.000
“Hmm yeah I think Li used it as an example of&nbsp;
potential benefits and didn't mean it literally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:42.000 --> 02:09:44.880
but nothing of that sort has been discussed yet”

02:09:44.880 --> 02:09:48.400
Now, on a functional level most&nbsp;
DAOs use an administrative system&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:48.400 --> 02:09:52.640
based off the use and spending of&nbsp;
internal scrip, the governance tokens.

02:09:52.640 --> 02:09:56.560
There’s a decent amount of variability in how&nbsp;
they’re used, but basically they function either&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:09:56.560 --> 02:10:01.680
as proportional voting power, exactly the same&nbsp;
as voting shares in a publicly traded company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:01.680 --> 02:10:06.480
fiat voting power where there’s no point in&nbsp;
possessing more than one, or direct voting&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:06.480 --> 02:10:12.160
power where tokens are spent to cast votes and&nbsp;
more tokens can be spent to cast more votes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:12.160 --> 02:10:15.760
Typically this scrip can be bought and&nbsp;
sold, even on a secondary market, and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:15.760 --> 02:10:19.680
indeed, possession of it is typically&nbsp;
a definitional part of membership.

02:10:19.680 --> 02:10:21.360
Rather than structuring like a union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:21.360 --> 02:10:24.320
Yield’s overt goal for their DAO&nbsp;
is to function as a hedge fund,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:24.320 --> 02:10:29.280
using the exchange value of their token as a means&nbsp;
to raise funds to invest into play-to-earn games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:29.280 --> 02:10:33.600
and allowing Yield members to spend their tokens&nbsp;
to gain access to these DAO-owned resources.

02:10:33.600 --> 02:10:37.040
In fact Yield is so far removed from&nbsp;
the purpose and functionality of a union&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:37.040 --> 02:10:40.560
that the roadmap includes potentially&nbsp;
implementing what’s called holographic&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:40.560 --> 02:10:44.960
consensus, which is a futures market where&nbsp;
participants gamble on what proposals&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:44.960 --> 02:10:50.960
will or won’t be passed using their governance&nbsp;
tokens as the stakes. It’s amazing if you wanted&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:50.960 --> 02:10:55.600
to build a machine whose sole purpose is to&nbsp;
concentrate political power slowly over time.

02:10:55.600 --> 02:10:59.600
Additionally many use a proof-of-stake&nbsp;
staking system to reward members with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:10:59.600 --> 02:11:03.760
additional tokens with no gate on how&nbsp;
many tokens any single member can hold.

02:11:03.760 --> 02:11:06.800
This whole arrangement creates&nbsp;
a system where participants with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:06.800 --> 02:11:11.440
only a few tokens are incentivized&nbsp;
to not vote against the interests&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:11.440 --> 02:11:16.000
of highly staked members, plus anything&nbsp;
you spend limits what you can stake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:16.000 --> 02:11:21.360
and thus reduces all future income of tokens,&nbsp;
which means even less voting power in the future.

02:11:21.360 --> 02:11:25.680
Members who possess a disproportionate share&nbsp;
of tokens can afford to out-spend on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:25.680 --> 02:11:30.160
outcome of any vote AND still retain&nbsp;
a proportional future voting power.&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:30.160 --> 02:11:35.120
At best you end up with high powered voting&nbsp;
blocs, and at worst a functional monopoly.

02:11:35.120 --> 02:11:38.320
The internal discourse of&nbsp;
Yield is, like all crypto,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:38.320 --> 02:11:42.960
focused on the price of the DAO’s scrip, and not&nbsp;
it’s actual functionality within the organization.

02:11:43.600 --> 02:11:45.520
Rather than creating a more equitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:45.520 --> 02:11:49.120
democratic organization that looks&nbsp;
out for the needs of all its members,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:49.120 --> 02:11:53.840
Yield is a scheme that explicitly rewards its&nbsp;
highest stakeholders with more power and access.

02:11:54.400 --> 02:11:58.560
Now, conceptually you could make a DAO&nbsp;
that behaves towards actually useful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:11:58.560 --> 02:12:04.000
worker-focused goals, but you could also do that&nbsp;
without a DAO, because it’s just an organization.

02:12:04.000 --> 02:12:06.880
The DAO itself is just a&nbsp;
mechanism of an organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:06.880 --> 02:12:10.480
and more often than not its involvement&nbsp;
is little more than tech fetishism.

02:12:10.480 --> 02:12:14.400
So most actual DAOs don’t resemble&nbsp;
anything like a flat hierarchy.

02:12:14.400 --> 02:12:18.480
In fact the ability to buy and sell voting&nbsp;
power, and the hierarchy that results,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:18.480 --> 02:12:23.920
is seen as a strict advantage in that it allows&nbsp;
emotionally uninvested members to make money&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:23.920 --> 02:12:28.240
and gives them a thing that they can reward&nbsp;
people with that will “align incentives”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:28.240 --> 02:12:32.640
and despite the fact that Li Jin is directly&nbsp;
involved with Yield as the “philosopher in&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:32.640 --> 02:12:37.680
residence” Yield is neither structured like a&nbsp;
labour union nor does it have ambitions to be one.

02:12:37.680 --> 02:12:42.080
The point is that thought leaders like Li Jin,&nbsp;
who get a lot of social traction by promising&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:42.080 --> 02:12:46.960
that their technofetishistic community are&nbsp;
solving big societal problems, are liars.

02:12:46.960 --> 02:12:50.960
They love the pageantry of democracy because&nbsp;
it allows them to pretend to be democratic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:50.960 --> 02:12:54.400
because they can paint their&nbsp;
detractors as being undemocratic.

02:12:54.400 --> 02:12:57.680
It’s all hollow handwaving&nbsp;
and technobabble to distract&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:12:57.680 --> 02:12:59.600
from the fact that it’s just shareholding.

02:12:59.600 --> 02:13:04.240
It’s the corporatization of everything, the&nbsp;
conversion of the entire world into claves&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:04.240 --> 02:13:07.600
governed by power granted via&nbsp;
token possession and enforced&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:07.600 --> 02:13:10.960
by machines that allow humans to&nbsp;
wash their hands of the outcomes.

02:13:10.960 --> 02:13:15.440
At the end of the day every DAO pretending to&nbsp;
be useful is still a forced entry point to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:15.440 --> 02:13:29.840
hype-driven memecoin whose existence only benefits&nbsp;
its creators and the exchange that sells it.

02:13:29.840 --> 02:13:37.360
[Subway rattling]

02:13:37.360 --> 02:13:42.400
In 2008 the economy functionally collapsed.&nbsp;
The basic chain reaction was this:&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:42.400 --> 02:13:46.000
bankers took mortgages and turned them&nbsp;
into something they could gamble on.

02:13:46.000 --> 02:13:48.640
This created a bubble, and then the bubble popped.

02:13:48.640 --> 02:13:54.080
When you drill down into it you realize that the&nbsp;
core of the crypto ecosystem, the core of Web3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:13:54.080 --> 02:14:00.080
the core of the NFT marketplace, is a turf&nbsp;
war between the wealthy and ultra-wealthy.

02:14:00.080 --> 02:14:04.480
Technofetishists who look at people like&nbsp;
Bill Gates and Jeff Bezos, billionaires&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:04.480 --> 02:14:08.960
minted via tech industry doors that have&nbsp;
now been shut by market calcification,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:08.960 --> 02:14:13.440
and are looking for a do-over, looking&nbsp;
to synthesize a new market where they&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:13.440 --> 02:14:18.960
can be the one to ascend from a merely wealthy&nbsp;
programmer to a hyper-wealthy industrialist.

02:14:18.960 --> 02:14:21.520
It’s a cat fight between the 5% and the 1%.

02:14:22.400 --> 02:14:27.360
Ultimately the driving forces underlying&nbsp;
this entire movement are economic disparity.

02:14:27.360 --> 02:14:32.000
The wealthy and tenuously wealthy are looking for&nbsp;
a space that they can dominate, where they can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:32.000 --> 02:14:36.960
trendsetters and tastemakers and can seemingly&nbsp;
invent value through sheer force of will.

02:14:36.960 --> 02:14:40.720
This is, in my opinion, the&nbsp;
blindspot of many casual critics.

02:14:40.720 --> 02:14:46.720
The fact that tokens representing ape PFPs&nbsp;
are useless, yet somehow still expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:14:46.720 --> 02:14:50.640
isn't an overlooked glitch in&nbsp;
the system, it's half the point.

02:14:50.640 --> 02:14:55.600
It's a digital extension of inconvenient&nbsp;
fashion. It's a flex and a form of mythmaking.

02:14:55.600 --> 02:15:00.480
And that's how it draws in the bottom: people&nbsp;
who feel their opportunities shrinking, who see&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:00.480 --> 02:15:06.080
the system closing around them, who have become&nbsp;
isolated by social media and a global pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:06.080 --> 02:15:11.680
who feel the future getting smaller, people&nbsp;
pressured by the casualization of work as&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:11.680 --> 02:15:17.040
jobs are dissolved into the gig economy, and&nbsp;
want to believe that escape is just that easy.

02:15:17.040 --> 02:15:22.320
All you gotta do is bet on the right Discord and&nbsp;
you might be air-dropped the next new hotness.

02:15:22.320 --> 02:15:25.946
It could be you plucked out of the&nbsp;
crowd on Rarible and bestowed a six&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:25.946 --> 02:15:28.879
figure price by an elusive Emerati music producer.

02:15:28.880 --> 02:15:33.760
Get a BAYC in your wallet, hodl like a&nbsp;
good diamond hands, and enjoy that yield.

02:15:33.760 --> 02:15:39.680
All you need is $5000 in seed money and you can&nbsp;
buy a Farmer's World milk cow, and if you milk&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:39.680 --> 02:15:44.480
that cow every four hours, day and night, for two&nbsp;
weeks, why there's all your money back right there&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:44.480 --> 02:15:49.520
and now it's pure profit (minus, naturally, the&nbsp;
overhead of all the WAX you needed to stake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:49.520 --> 02:15:52.400
the barn you needed to buy and build, the&nbsp;
barley you needed to purchase and grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:52.400 --> 02:15:55.760
the food you needed to buy to refill the energy&nbsp;
you needed to milk the cow, build the barn,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:15:55.760 --> 02:15:59.440
and grow the barley, plus you actually need to&nbsp;
cash out which isn't getting paid, it's quitting).

02:15:59.440 --> 02:16:03.280
This is your chance to stick it to&nbsp;
Wall Street and Venture Capitalists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:03.280 --> 02:16:06.240
as long as you pay no attention&nbsp;
to the VCs behind the curtain.

02:16:06.800 --> 02:16:09.600
The line can only go up.

02:16:09.600 --> 02:16:15.040
It’s a movement driven in no small part&nbsp;
by rage, by people who looked at 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:15.040 --> 02:16:19.200
who looked at the system as it exists,&nbsp;
but concluded that the problems with&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:19.200 --> 02:16:24.000
capitalism were that it didn’t provide&nbsp;
enough opportunities to be the boot.

02:16:24.000 --> 02:16:30.400
And that’s the pitch. Buy in now, buy in early,&nbsp;
and you could be the high tech future boot.

02:16:30.400 --> 02:16:35.600
Our systems are breaking or broken,&nbsp;
straining under neglect and sabotage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:35.600 --> 02:16:40.960
and our leaders seem at best complacent,&nbsp;
willing to coast out the collapse.

02:16:40.960 --> 02:16:43.040
We need something better.

02:16:43.040 --> 02:16:50.240
But a system that turns everyone into petty&nbsp;
digital landlords, that distills all interaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:50.240 --> 02:16:56.399
into transaction, that determines the value&nbsp;
of something by how sellable it is and whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:16:56.399 --> 02:17:02.639
or not it can be gambled on as a fractional&nbsp;
tokens sold via micro-auction, that’s not it.

02:17:02.640 --> 02:17:05.920
A different system does not&nbsp;
inherently mean a better system,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:05.920 --> 02:17:08.720
we replace bad systems with&nbsp;
worse ones all the time.

02:17:08.720 --> 02:17:13.200
We replaced a bad system of work and&nbsp;
bosses with a terrible system of apps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:13.200 --> 02:17:15.120
gigs, and on-demand labour.

02:17:15.120 --> 02:17:20.640
So it’s not just that I oppose NFTs because&nbsp;
the foremost of them are aesthetically vacuous&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:20.640 --> 02:17:24.720
representations of the dead inner lives&nbsp;
of the tech and finance bros behind them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:24.720 --> 02:17:27.760
it’s that they represent the&nbsp;
vanguard of a worse system.

02:17:27.760 --> 02:17:32.080
The whole thing, from OpenSea fantasies&nbsp;
for starving artists to the buy-in for&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:32.080 --> 02:17:37.760
Play to Earn games, it's the same hollow,&nbsp;
exploitative pitch as MLMs. It's Amway,&nbsp;&nbsp;

02:17:37.760 --> 02:17:41.840
but everywhere you look people&nbsp;
are wearing ugly-ass ape cartoons.

